WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.970
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.770
where we discuss science

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.963
and science based tools for everyday life.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.280
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.840
and I'm a professor of neurobiology

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.000
and ophthalmology at
Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.850
Today, we are going to discuss the use

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.500
of deliberate cold exposure

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.540
for health and performance.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.730
Temperature is a powerful
stimulus on our nervous system

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.810
and indeed on every organ
and system of our body

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.500
and cold in particular can be leveraged

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.220
to improve mental health,
physical health, and performance,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.370
meaning for endurance
exercise, for recovering

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.230
from various forms of exercise,

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:41.480
for actually improving strength and power

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.930
and for enhancing mental capacity.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.960
In order to properly leverage
deliberate cold exposure

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.300
for sake of mental health,
physical health and performance,

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.280
you have to understand how cold
impacts the brain and body.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.330
So today we are going to discuss that.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.080
We're going to talk about
some of the neural circuits

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.650
and pathways, some of
the hormones involved.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.070
I promise to make it
all clear and accessible

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.190
regardless of whether or not

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.530
you have a scientific background or not.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.080
We are also going to discuss
very specific protocols

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.200
that you can apply,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.360
which leverage variables like temperature,

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.540
how cold, how to deliver
the cold, for instance,

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.160
whether or not you use a cold shower,

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.980
cold immersion, ice bath,
circulating water, or still water,

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.920
whether or not you're going
for walks outside in a t-shirt

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.490
when it's cold or whether or not

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.120
you're purposefully
using things like cryo,

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.400
if you have access to that or not.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.360
One thing I can promise you

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.540
is that by the end of today's episode,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.630
you will know a lot about the
biology of thermal regulation,

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.000
that is how your brain and
body regulates its temperature.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.380
You will also have a lot
of tools in your arsenal

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.170
that you can use and leverage

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.960
toward improving mental health,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.380
physical health, reducing
inflammation in the body,

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.210
improving athletic performance,

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.850
improving mental performance.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.580
I promise to spell out all
those protocols in detail

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.520
as I go along and to summarize
them again at the end.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.750
I'd like to make a point
now that I'm going to make

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.560
several additional times
during today's episode

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.180
and that is that temperature
is a very potent stimulus

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.750
for the brain and body.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.410
That also means that it
carries certain hazards

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.130
if it's not done correctly.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.040
Now, everyone shows up to the table,

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.380
meaning to protocols,

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.420
with a different
background of health status

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.190
and there's simply no way that I can know

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:20.790
what your health status is.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.290
So anytime you are going
to take on a new protocol,

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:26.730
that means a behavioral protocol
or a nutritional protocol

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.510
or a supplementation protocol,

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:31.610
you should absolutely consult
a board certified physician

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.110
before initiating that protocol.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.380
I don't just say this to protect us,

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.700
I also say this to protect you.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.500
If you'd like to see
our medical disclaimer,

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.700
you can go to our show
notes, it's described there.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.930
In fact, I encourage
you to please do that.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.240
And in general,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.500
when embarking on new
protocols in particular,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.460
if they involve strong stimuli
like changing temperature

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.730
or placing yourself into
unusual temperatures,

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:56.920
I would encourage you
to progress gradually.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.260
I would also encourage you to not look at

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:02.010
gradual progression as
the kind of weak version

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.080
of a protocol.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.600
In fact, today I'm going to discuss

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.940
a really beautiful peer reviewed study

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.440
that involved having people
do deliberate cold exposure

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.500
so they were immersing
themselves into water

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:13.850
up to about their neck

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:15.780
and the water was actually not that cold.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.570
It was only about 60 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.460
which for most people is pretty tolerable.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.120
So nowhere near the kinds
of extreme temperatures

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.320
that one could use in other protocols.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.080
And the interesting thing is despite

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.010
that fairly modest cold temperature,

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.910
by simply extending the duration of time

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.420
that people were in that water,

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.530
they experienced enormous
increases in neurochemicals

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:40.540
that ought to translate to
improvements in focus and mood.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.650
And indeed, that's what's been observed

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.570
in subsequent studies.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:46.680
So again, please see
our medical disclaimer

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.770
in our show notes, please
proceed with caution always,

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:54.650
please also understand that
the most potent stimulus

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.540
isn't always the one that you experience

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.270
as the most intense in the moment.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:03:59.910
In fact, I would encourage you to you find

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.440
the minimum threshold of stimulus

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.170
that will allow you to
drive the maximum benefit

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.030
from each protocol and indeed,

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:10.580
I will point out what those
thresholds ought to be today.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.450
I'll give you some simple formulas,

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.660
gauges or guides that you
can use in order to navigate

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.410
this extremely interesting and potent tool

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.520
that we call deliberate cold exposure.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.390
Before we talk about
deliberate cold exposure

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:24.910
and its many powerful applications,

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:26.210
I'd like to highlight a study

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:27.890
that I find particularly interesting,

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.380
that I think you will find

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.940
particularly interesting and useful.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.340
The title of this study
is brief aerobic exercise

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.860
immediately enhances
visual attentional control

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.480
and perceptual speed,

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:43.480
testing the mediated role
of feelings of energy.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:46.930
Now, the reason I like
this study is first of all,

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.980
it's a fairly large size sample group.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:52.320
They looked at 101 students.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.740
These were college-aged students
and they had two groups.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.740
One group did 15 minutes of
jogging at moderate intensity.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.600
So they did measure percent
heart rates, et cetera,

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.460
but this would be analogous
to zone two cardio,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.420
which I've discussed
on this podcast before.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:14.290
Zone two cardio is cardiovascular exercise

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:18.260
that places you at a level where
you can hold a conversation

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:20.100
with a little bit of strain,

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.480
meaning that you can get the words out,

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.440
but every once in a while
you have to catch your breath

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.020
whereas if you were to push
any harder by any mechanism

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.060
going faster or on a
steeper incline, et cetera,

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.840
that you would have a hard time
carrying out a conversation.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.840
So zone two cardio is a
common form of describing

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.660
that level of intensity that
they call moderate intensity.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.320
So one group did 15 minute of
jogging at moderate intensity,

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:47.820
which I'm translating to
roughly zone two cardio.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:52.040
The other group did 15 minutes
of relaxation concentration

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:55.320
that is somewhat akin to
mindfulness meditation.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.000
And then they were analyzed
for perceptual speed,

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.750
visual attentional control,
something called working memory,

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:02.583
which is your ability

00:06:02.583 --> 00:06:06.490
to keep certain batches
of information online.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:08.930
Just imagine someone telling
you their phone number,

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.800
and you have to remember
that sequence of numbers

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.980
in your head for some period
of time, that's working memory.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:15.920
And it depends very
heavily on the so-called

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.010
prefrontal cortical networks,

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.010
which are involved in planning and action.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:23.140
And they also looked at
people's feelings of energy

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:24.460
and they measured that subjectively,

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.100
how energetic people felt.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.010
Now the major takeaways from this study

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:30.230
that I'd like to emphasize are that

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:32.630
the 15 minutes of jogging group

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:35.320
experienced elevated levels of energy

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.930
for some period of time after
they ceased the exercise

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:41.810
whereas the group that
did mindfulness meditation

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.150
actually reported feeling more calm

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:46.770
and having less overall energy.

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.730
Now that's very subjective
and indeed they used

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:52.080
subjective measures to analyze energy,

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.060
but what gets interesting is
when they looked at performance

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:58.760
on these various cognitive
tasks and the two tasks

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:01.380
that they use were called
the trail making tests,

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:02.700
they have different versions of this,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:03.870
version A, version B,

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.730
I don't want to go into too much detail,

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:09.960
but version A essentially
involves having a page of numbers

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.900
that are distributed somewhat randomly.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.260
So one, two, three, four, five,
six, seven, eight and so on,

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:15.950
but distributed randomly across the page

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:17.580
and people have to use visual search

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:19.510
to circle those numbers in sequence.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:21.050
So this involves visual attention,

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:23.540
it involves some motor skills,

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:24.420
involves a number of things

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:27.240
that certainly require energy and focus.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:30.320
The second test was the
trail making test part B,

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:31.230
as I mentioned earlier,

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:34.040
and this involved also
circling numbers in sequence,

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.520
but interspersed between
those numbers were letters.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:37.910
So rather than just having

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:39.810
to circle off numbers in sequence,

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.620
they actually had to connect one,

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.167
then the letter A, then two,
then the letter B, et cetera

00:07:44.167 --> 00:07:47.440
and remember these are randomly
distributed across the page.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:50.120
The major takeaway from the
study is that the group that did

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:55.120
the 15 minutes of moderate
exercise prior to these two tests

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:58.770
showed significant decreases
in the amount of time required

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:01.380
to complete these tests accurately.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:05.330
That is interesting and indeed
surprising at least to me,

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:08.130
because there have been many
studies looking at the effects

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:11.200
of mindfulness meditation
on the ability to focus.

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:14.010
The key variable in the study
turned out to be energy.

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:17.600
This subjectively measured
feeling I should say

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.860
of having more energy and
thereby the ability to focus,

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:23.770
especially in these high
cognitive demand tasks.

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:25.470
Now the takeaway from
this study for all of us

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.330
I think is pretty straightforward.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:30.170
If you are going to sit
down to do some work

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.570
that requires focus and working memory

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:33.620
and cognitive attention

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.840
and especially if it's some
visual spatial control,

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.460
meaning you have to search
for things on a page,

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:39.850
you have to organize things on a page,

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:41.460
so some writing, arithmetic,

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.650
basically cognitive work of any kind,

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:48.700
15 minutes of moderate exercise done prior

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.590
to that work about could
be very beneficial for you.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:53.930
This does not mean that
mindfulness meditation

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:55.400
would not be a benefit to you.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.250
I wouldn't want you to conclude that,

00:08:57.250 --> 00:08:58.940
but if you had to choose between doing

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:00.890
15 minutes of mindfulness meditation

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:02.850
and doing 15 minutes of moderate exercise

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:05.680
prior to a cognitive work about,

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:09.300
I would say the 15 minutes
of moderate exercise would be

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:12.270
more valuable at least based
on the data in this paper.

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.007
In many previous podcasts,

00:09:14.007 --> 00:09:17.750
I have talked about the
powerful effects of doing things

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:21.890
like mindfulness meditation,
and other forms of NSDR,

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:23.000
non-sleep deep rest.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.840
So these could be 20 minute
naps or just lying there quietly

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:28.000
with your eyes closed or yoga nidra

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.510
or NSDR scripts are available on YouTube

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:34.660
and various other places
free of cost of any kind.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:36.530
You just go to YouTube, put in NSDR,

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:37.683
non-sleep deep rest.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.540
Those protocols have been
shown to be very beneficial

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:43.890
for enhancing neuroplasticity,

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:47.120
the changes in the brain
and body that encode

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:49.440
or shift the neural circuits that allow

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.700
for memory to change,

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:55.760
that allow for learning to
occur after a learning about.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.490
What I'm referring to today
in this particular study

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:00.830
is the use of moderate exercise

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:03.950
in order to increase
one's focus and attention

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:06.800
in order to trigger that neuroplasticity.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.223
So the simple sequence here
is get energetic and alert,

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:14.220
do that prior to the learning about,

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.370
engage in the cognitive
work or learning about,

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:18.330
and then mindfulness meditation,

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:20.700
NSDR and so forth should follow.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:23.160
And if you would like to access this paper

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.710
and like to look more at
the details in the paper,

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.280
we'll be sure to put a
link in the show notes.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.210
The first author is Legrand.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:31.770
And again, the title of this
paper is brief aerobic exercise

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.840
immediately enhances
visual potential control

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:34.980
and perceptual speed,

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:37.540
testing the mediating
role of feeling of energy.

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:40.100
And I also just want to
emphasize immediately.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.150
I think most people out
there are interested in tools

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:43.627
and protocols that work the first time

00:10:43.627 --> 00:10:45.020
and that work every time

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:47.760
and indeed, I think this
protocol fits that bill.

