00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:40.580
Let's talk about the use of
cold for health and performance.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:42.530
I confess I love this topic

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:45.630
because it takes me back
to my undergraduate years

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:49.570
when I worked in a laboratory
studying cold physiology,

00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:52.660
its effects on the brain
and its effects the body.

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:54.010
And over the years,

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:56.780
I've always kept track of
the literature in this area

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:59.830
and indeed there have been
some tremendous discoveries,

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:02.810
both in animal models, so in
rodents like mice and rats,

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:04.570
but also in humans

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:07.100
and today we're going to
talk about both categories

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:10.650
of studies and I'll be careful
to point out when discoveries

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:11.920
were made in animal models

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:14.173
and when they were made in humans.

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:19.790
A key point when thinking about
the use of cold as a tool,

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:23.760
and the key point is that
you have a baseline level

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.510
of temperature that is varying,

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:29.480
changing across the 24 hour cycle.

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:32.270
So any use of deliberate cold exposure

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:36.160
is going to be super
imposed on that rhythm,

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:40.420
that circadian rhythm,
meaning that 24 hour rhythm.

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:44.750
The basic contour of your
circadian rhythm in temperature

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:49.550
is that approximately two hours
before the time you wake up

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.920
is your so-called temperature minimum.

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:54.400
So your temperature minimum is a time

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:56.200
within the 24 hour cycle

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:59.900
when your body temperature
is at its lowest.

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:03.240
So if you normally wake up around 6:00 AM,

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:07.400
your temperature minimum
is probably about 4:00 AM.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:10.000
If you normally wake up at about 7:00 AM,

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:13.170
your temperature minimum
is probably about 5:00 AM.

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:16.210
It's not exactly two hours
before your wake up time,

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:19.250
it's approximately two hours
before your wake up time.

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:22.700
Now, as you go from
your temperature minimum

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:25.090
to the time in which
you're going to awake,

00:17:25.090 --> 00:17:27.970
your temperature is rising slightly.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.420
And then at the point where you wake up,

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:33.320
your temperature starts
to go up more sharply

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:35.910
and will continue to go up into the early

00:17:36.778 --> 00:17:38.600
and sometimes even into
the late afternoon.

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:41.350
And then sometime in the
late afternoon and evening,

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:44.750
your temperature will start to decline.

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:46.710
And indeed, as you approach sleep,

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:48.210
your body temperature will drop

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:50.760
by anywhere from one to three degrees.

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:53.980
And in fact that decrease
in core body temperature

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:56.580
is important if not
essential for getting into

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:59.010
and staying in deep sleep.

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:02.830
So temperature rises with
waking, that's easy to remember.

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:05.590
It tends to continue to
rise throughout the day

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:07.310
and in the late afternoon and evening,

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:10.410
your temperature WILL start
to go down and the drop

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:13.850
in temperature actually
helps you access sleep.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:18.310
That background or what we
call baseline circadian rhythm

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:21.160
in core body temperature
is important to remember

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:24.300
because it helps us frame both the effects

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:28.640
of deliberate cold
exposure and helps us frame

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:31.090
when you might want to use
deliberate cold exposure

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:33.600
in order to access specific states.

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:36.740
It also points to times
within the 24 hour cycle

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:39.830
when you might want to avoid
using deliberate cold exposure,

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:42.720
if your primary goal is to get to sleep.

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:45.590
So that's the circadian
rhythm in temperature.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:47.570
Now I just briefly want to touch on

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:51.090
thermal regulation at the
level of body and the brain.

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:54.193
And this will be very
surprising to many of you.

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:57.510
Let's do what's called
a Gedanken experiment,

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:58.960
which is a thought experiment.

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:01.930
Let's say I send you
out into the desert heat

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:05.650
for a jog or a run and
it's very hot outside,

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:10.650
102 or 103 degrees, and you start to move,

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:12.260
you start to sweat

00:19:12.260 --> 00:19:14.900
and of course your core
body temperature goes up.

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:18.890
Now, then I offer you a cold towel,

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:21.220
maybe a really, really cold towel

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:22.850
and this towel is saturated with water

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:24.517
so you could actually
squeeze the water out of that

00:19:24.517 --> 00:19:26.360
and cool your body off.

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:29.430
And our Gedanken experiment
is for me to say okay,

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:31.480
where are you going to place the towel?

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:33.820
How are you going to cool yourself off?

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:35.910
And I'm guessing that
most of you would think

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:37.590
that the best way to cool yourself off

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:39.550
would be to drape that
towel over your head,

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:42.040
maybe your neck, over your torso,

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:43.320
that it would feel really, really good,

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:45.370
and it would cool you off.

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:48.260
Well, that's exactly the wrong approach

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:49.330
if you want to cool off.

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:52.100
And in fact, if you were
to use that approach,

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:56.620
your body temperature would
continue to increase even more.

00:19:56.620 --> 00:19:59.300
Yes, even more than had you not placed

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:02.550
that cold towel on your
head or your torso.

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:04.500
And here is why.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:07.420
Thermal regulation meaning
your brain and body's ability

00:20:07.420 --> 00:20:10.960
to regulate your internal core temperature

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:13.390
is somewhat like a thermostat
and that thermostat

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:15.170
resides in your brain.

00:20:15.170 --> 00:20:16.650
So if you think about the thermostat

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:18.680
in your home or apartment,

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:21.400
if it's too warm in your home or apartment

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:24.980
and you were to take a bag of ice

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:27.640
and to put it on that thermostat,

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:29.090
what would the thermostat do?

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:32.670
It would register the
environment as artificially cool.

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:34.670
It would think that the environment

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:37.980
was actually much colder than it is.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:39.780
And so as a consequence,

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:41.040
it would trigger a mechanism

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:43.670
to further increase the
temperature in the room.

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:45.540
And you have such a thermostat as well.

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:48.550
It's called the medial preoptic
area of the hypothalamus.

00:20:48.550 --> 00:20:52.180
The hypothalamus is a small
region of brain tissue

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:54.110
about over the roof of your mouth

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:56.230
and a little bit in front of that.

00:20:56.230 --> 00:21:00.640
So it's basically right behind
your nose and over the roof

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:02.650
of your mouth and it's
a collection of neurons.

00:21:02.650 --> 00:21:04.540
Those neurons have a lot
of different functions

00:21:04.540 --> 00:21:07.530
that include things like
the control of aggression,

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:09.240
the control of sex behavior,

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:11.730
the control of temperature
regulation and so on.

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:15.850
The medial preoptic area
has connections with

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:17.290
the rest of the brain

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:19.090
or areas within the brain, I should say

00:21:19.090 --> 00:21:21.510
and with many areas within the body,

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:26.130
it receives input from
receptors in our skin

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:29.600
and inside our body that
register temperature

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:31.320
and it acts as a thermostat.

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:34.580
So if the surface of
your body is made cool,

00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:38.420
your medial preoptic
area will send signals

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:40.510
by way of hormones

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:42.600
and by way of chemicals that will serve

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:44.680
to heat your body up.

00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:47.280
So what this means is that
if you want to cool down,

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:52.160
the last thing you want to
do is to bring a cold surface

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:54.600
of any kind, towel or splashing water,

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.540
to the majority of your body surface.

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:59.107
It might be very, very surprising to you.

00:21:59.107 --> 00:22:00.930
And you might say, wait,
if I want to cool down,

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:02.980
I should jump into a cold lake
or something of that sort.

00:22:02.980 --> 00:22:05.530
That's a different thing altogether.

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:06.510
What I'll tell you,

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:08.320
and we'll get into this
in more depth later,

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:09.220
is that if you really

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:11.360
want to cool down quickly and efficiently,

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:14.810
you should leverage particular portals,

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:18.210
meaning particular sites on your body

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:21.700
where heat can leave
your body more readily

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:26.130
and where cooling can have
a dramatic and fast impact

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:27.190
on your core body temperature,

00:22:27.190 --> 00:22:29.440
can even save your life if
you're going hyperthermic.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:32.670
We're going to talk more about
the specific protocols to reduce

00:22:32.670 --> 00:22:34.640
core body temperature
for sake of performance

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:37.560
and avoiding hyperthermia
later in the episode.

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:40.620
Hyperthermia of course is a
very, very dangerous situation

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:43.270
because while your body can
drop in core temperature

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:45.860
somewhat and still be safe,

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:48.500
you can't really increase your
body temperature that much

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:50.050
before your brain starts to cook

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:52.080
and other organs start
to cook and by cook,

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:54.170
I mean the cells actually start to die.

00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:56.950
So you have to be very, very
careful with the use of heat.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:58.460
Heat stroke is no joke.

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:00.310
People die from heat stroke all the time.

00:23:00.310 --> 00:23:02.380
You really want to avoid that.

00:23:02.380 --> 00:23:06.640
One way to avoid that is to
cool the appropriate surfaces

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:10.050
of your body and the appropriate
surfaces in this case

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:11.910
are the upper cheeks

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:14.560
or I would say the upper half of the face,

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:16.520
the palms of your hands and
the bottoms of your feet.

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:18.600
I've talked about this
on the podcast before

00:23:18.600 --> 00:23:20.930
and in the guest episode
with Dr. Craig Heller,

00:23:20.930 --> 00:23:23.900
my colleague in the biology
department at Stanford,

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:26.510
but just very briefly, these surfaces,

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:28.370
the upper half of the face,

00:23:28.370 --> 00:23:31.120
the palms of the hands and
the bottoms of the feet

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:32.720
are what we call

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:37.720
glabrous skin surfaces,
G-L-A-B-R-O-U-S, glabrous.

00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:42.710
And those surfaces are unique
in that just below them,

00:23:42.710 --> 00:23:46.120
the vasculature is different
than elsewhere in the body.

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:48.480
Normally the passage of blood goes from

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:50.640
arteries to capillaries to veins,

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:54.150
but just beneath the
glabrous skin on the bottoms

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:56.280
of the feet, the hands in
the upper half of the face,

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:59.970
you have what are called
arterio-venous anastomoses.

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:03.020
These are portals of
blood that go directly

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:05.530
from arteries to veins and in doing so,

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:10.530
allow the body to dump heat
more readily, more quickly.

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:15.250
So as it turns out that if
you are to cool the palms

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:16.370
of the hands, the bottoms of the feet

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:17.470
and the upper half of the face,

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:20.690
you can more efficiently
reduce core body temperature

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:23.190
for sake of offsetting hyperthermia

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:25.420
and for improving athletic performance

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:27.300
and maybe even cognitive performance.

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:29.180
So we will return to
the specific protocols

00:24:29.180 --> 00:24:31.280
for doing that later in the episode,

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:34.140
I'll give you a lot of
details about how to do that,

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:35.680
how to do that without the use of

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:38.270
any fancy or expensive technology.

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:39.410
There are some technologies

00:24:39.410 --> 00:24:41.930
that are now commercially
available, for instance,

00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:43.040
the so-called CoolMitt

00:24:43.040 --> 00:24:45.190
that will allow you to do
that with maximum efficiency,

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:48.480
but I'll also give you some
at home methods to do this

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:51.523
either in the gym or on runs
or for sake of cognitive work.

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:54.270
So the two key themes again

00:24:54.270 --> 00:24:57.580
are understand that
baseline circadian rhythm

00:24:57.580 --> 00:25:01.050
in temperature, and understand
that the best way to cool

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:02.840
the body is going to be

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:07.430
by making sure that
something cold contacts

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:08.390
the bottoms of your feet,

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:10.530
the palms of your hands and
the upper half of the face.

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:11.470
Ideally all three

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:13.920
if your goal is to lower core
body temperature quickly,

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:17.150
and again, just cooling
off the back of your neck

00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:19.390
or the top of your head
or your torso with a towel

00:25:19.390 --> 00:25:21.400
is going to be the least efficient way

00:25:21.400 --> 00:25:24.700
to lower core body temperature
and might even increase

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:27.500
body temperature under certain conditions.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:28.870
With those two points in mind,

00:25:28.870 --> 00:25:31.300
we can start to think
about directed deliberate

00:25:31.300 --> 00:25:32.710
cold exposure protocols,

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:34.780
and there are a number
of different reasons

00:25:34.780 --> 00:25:36.830
to use deliberate cold exposure.

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:38.780
And I want to separate those out for you.

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:42.850
There are cold protocols
that have been tested

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:45.030
in peer reviewed studies that are designed

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:47.170
to improve mental performance.

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:49.860
They are designed to improve
things like resilience

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:50.693
or your grittiness,

00:25:50.693 --> 00:25:52.550
or your ability to move through challenge

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:56.310
or to regulate your mind
and your internal state

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:57.830
under conditions of stress

00:25:57.830 --> 00:26:01.320
and we can define stress
very specifically as times

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:03.680
when adrenaline also called epinephrine

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:09.290
and or norepinephrine
also called noradrenaline,

00:26:09.290 --> 00:26:10.600
are elevated in your body.

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:12.440
Forgive me for the noradrenaline,

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:15.280
norepinephrine, adrenaline,
epinephrine nomenclature,

00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:16.500
I didn't make that up.

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:17.900
It turns out that every once in a while,

00:26:17.900 --> 00:26:20.100
scientists disagree, imagine that,

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:21.790
and you'll get multiple scientists

00:26:21.790 --> 00:26:23.810
naming the same molecule different things.

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:26.650
So epinephrine and adrenaline
are the same thing.

00:26:26.650 --> 00:26:28.390
I will use them interchangeably.

00:26:28.390 --> 00:26:31.230
Norepinephrine and noradrenaline
are the same thing.

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:34.400
I will use those terms interchangeably.

00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:36.100
Noradrenaline and adrenaline

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:38.120
are often co-released
in the brain and body.

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:41.270
So they work as kind of a pair to increase

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:42.820
our level of agitation,

00:26:42.820 --> 00:26:47.450
our level of focus and our
desire and our ability to move.

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:50.470
They are often co-released
from different sites

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:52.890
in the brain and body with dopamine,

00:26:52.890 --> 00:26:56.710
a molecule that is commonly misunderstood

00:26:56.710 --> 00:26:58.040
as the molecule of pleasure,

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:00.350
but is actually the
molecule of motivation,

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:02.750
reward and pursuit.

00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:04.970
So dopamine, norepinephrine
and noradrenaline

00:27:04.970 --> 00:27:08.220
tend to be released together
under certain conditions

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:11.190
and today you'll learn how
deliberate cold exposure

00:27:11.190 --> 00:27:15.310
can be used to cause increases
in the release of several,

00:27:15.310 --> 00:27:16.930
if not, all of these in ways

00:27:16.930 --> 00:27:21.000
that can improve your levels
of attention and your mood.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:26.000
But the key point is that
your mental state is shifted

00:27:26.740 --> 00:27:29.840
when you are exposed to
certain forms of cold,

00:27:29.840 --> 00:27:32.100
and many people use
deliberate cold exposure

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:35.730
specifically to shift
their body state as a way

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:38.930
to train their mental state
so that they can better cope

00:27:38.930 --> 00:27:41.290
with stress in real life and by real life,

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:43.940
I mean when life presents
stressful events,

00:27:43.940 --> 00:27:45.800
and I will give you specific protocols

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:47.630
as to how you can do that, in other words,

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:49.680
how you can become more resilient

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:52.200
through the use of
deliberate cold exposure.

00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:55.420
Now, because of the ways in
which deliberate cold exposure

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:57.840
can increase this category of chemicals

00:27:57.840 --> 00:27:58.920
called the catecholamines,

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:01.670
that includes dopamine,
norepinephrine and epinephrine,

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:05.870
it can also be used to elevate mood

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:07.740
for long periods of time.

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:10.120
And I'm going to discuss
a specific protocol

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:13.070
that has been shown to
increase these chemicals

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:18.070
anywhere from 2.5X to
250%, to as high as 500%,

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:23.740
five times over baseline.

00:28:23.740 --> 00:28:26.330
Now you might be asking whether
or not it is a good thing

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:29.180
to raise chemicals like
norepinephrine and dopamine

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:31.170
to such a great degree,

00:28:31.170 --> 00:28:32.480
whether or not that's healthy for us,

00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:34.100
whether or not they can harm us.

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:36.750
But it turns out that these
elevations in norepinephrine

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:39.820
and dopamine are very long lasting in ways

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:43.090
that people report
feeling vast improvements

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:45.190
in mood and vast improvements

00:28:45.190 --> 00:28:48.760
in levels of cognitive
attention and energy.

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:50.920
So by my read of the literature,

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:55.230
these seem to be healthy
increases in our baseline levels

00:28:55.230 --> 00:28:57.760
of these chemicals in ways
that can really support us

00:28:57.760 --> 00:28:59.810
so I'll give you a protocol for that.

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:01.760
Now, those are some of the mental effects

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:03.230
of deliberate cold exposure,

00:29:03.230 --> 00:29:05.500
but deliberate cold exposure
has also been studied

00:29:05.500 --> 00:29:07.040
in animal models and in humans

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:09.860
in the context of increasing metabolism,

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:12.980
even in converting certain fat cells

00:29:12.980 --> 00:29:14.320
that we call white fat cells,

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:16.120
which are the ones where energy is stored,

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:17.470
they're the ones that
we typically think of

00:29:17.470 --> 00:29:21.560
as kind of blubbery fat,
to beige or brown fat,

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:23.330
which is thermogenic fat,

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:25.420
meaning that it can increase
core body temperature

00:29:25.420 --> 00:29:27.680
and serves kind of the furnace

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:31.040
by which we increase our core metabolism.

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:33.180
So with a very broad stroke,

00:29:33.180 --> 00:29:35.660
I can say that white fat is
generally the kind of fat

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:38.610
that people want less of
and beige fat and brown fat

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:40.850
is generally the kind of fat that

00:29:40.850 --> 00:29:42.810
if you're going to have fat cells and

00:29:42.810 --> 00:29:45.330
you certainly need fat
cells that you want more of.

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:47.600
They are thermogenic,
they help you stay lean.

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:50.130
They actually serve as a
reservoir for heating your body up

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:52.190
if you're ever confronted
with a cold challenge.

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:53.960
So we're going to talk
about how to use cold

00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:55.580
for metabolism as well.

00:29:55.580 --> 00:29:56.413
And of course,

00:29:56.413 --> 00:29:57.960
people are using deliberate cold exposure

00:29:57.960 --> 00:30:00.010
to reduce inflammation post exercise,

00:30:00.010 --> 00:30:02.710
to reduce inflammation generally.

00:30:02.710 --> 00:30:06.550
And people are also using
cold to enhance performance

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:08.560
in the context of strength training,

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:10.160
in the context of endurance training

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:12.480
and we'll talk about those data as well,

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:14.970
but where I'd like to start
is with mental performance

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:18.320
and I'd like to detail what happens

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:21.460
when we deliberately
expose ourselves to cold.

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:24.650
It's key to point out the word deliberate.

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:27.640
If I don't say otherwise,
then throughout this episode,

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:30.750
if I say cold exposure, I
mean deliberate cold exposure.

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:33.100
And the reason I point that
out is that as my colleague,

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:34.740
David Spiegel, and the
department of psychiatry

00:30:34.740 --> 00:30:36.170
at Stanford says,

00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:38.590
it's not just about the
state that we are in,

00:30:38.590 --> 00:30:40.530
it's about the state that
we are in and whether or not

00:30:40.530 --> 00:30:43.920
we had anything to with placing
ourselves into that state

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:45.920
and whether or not we did
that on purpose or not.

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:50.060
And what he really means
by that statement is that

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:53.110
there are important effects
of what we call mindset.

00:30:53.110 --> 00:30:56.380
Mindset was a topic discussed
in the guest episode

00:30:56.380 --> 00:30:58.670
with Ali Crumb some weeks ago.

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:01.270
If you haven't seen that
episode, I highly recommend it.

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:04.940
And the science of my mindset
tells us that if we are doing

00:31:04.940 --> 00:31:06.910
something deliberately and we believe

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:08.640
that it's going to be good for us,

00:31:08.640 --> 00:31:11.440
it actually can lead to a different set

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:15.230
of physiological effects than if something

00:31:15.230 --> 00:31:18.850
is happening to us against our
will or without our control.

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:20.650
Now, this is different
than placebo effects.

00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:23.900
Placebo effects are distinct
from mindset effects.

00:31:23.900 --> 00:31:25.350
If you want to learn more
about that distinction,

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.300
please see the episode with Ali Crumb.

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:30.360
But again, when I talk about
cold exposure in this episode,

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:33.280
I'm talking about
deliberate cold exposure,

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:36.120
meaning that you are
placing yourself into a

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:38.870
cold environment on
purpose in order to extract

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:40.933
a particular set of benefits.

00:31:42.490 --> 00:31:45.820
When we talk about deliberate
cold exposure, almost always,

00:31:45.820 --> 00:31:48.100
that means getting uncomfortable.

00:31:48.100 --> 00:31:50.080
And one of the most common questions I get

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:52.700
when discussing the use
of cold for sake of mental

00:31:52.700 --> 00:31:54.720
or physical performance,
metabolism, et cetera,

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:57.340
is how cold should it be?

00:31:57.340 --> 00:31:58.480
How cold should the water be?

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:00.940
How cold should the environment be?

00:32:00.940 --> 00:32:02.300
And I just will tell you now

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:03.760
and I'm going to say this again and again

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:04.593
throughout the episode,

00:32:04.593 --> 00:32:06.860
cause it will continue to be
true throughout the episode

00:32:06.860 --> 00:32:08.610
and long after the episode is over,

00:32:09.610 --> 00:32:12.870
how cold depends on your cold tolerance,

00:32:12.870 --> 00:32:14.320
your core metabolism,

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:18.070
and a number of other features
that there is simply no way

00:32:18.070 --> 00:32:20.480
I could know or have access to.

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:22.943
So I would like you to
use this rule of thumb.

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:27.380
If you are using deliberate cold exposure,

00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:30.820
the environment that
you place yourself into

00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:34.290
should place your mind
into a state of whoa,

00:32:34.290 --> 00:32:36.840
I would really like to get
out of this environment,

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:39.440
but I can stay in safely.

00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:41.260
Now that might seem a
little bit arbitrary,

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:43.610
but let's say you were
to get into a warm shower

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:45.160
and it would feel really, really nice

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:47.120
and you were to start
turning down the warm

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:48.400
and turning up the cold.

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:50.350
There would be some threshold at which

00:32:50.350 --> 00:32:52.600
it would feel uncomfortable to you.

00:32:52.600 --> 00:32:54.800
And if you were to continue
to make a little bit colder

00:32:54.800 --> 00:32:57.610
than that, you would really
want to get out of the shower,

00:32:57.610 --> 00:33:00.170
but you are confident
that you could stay in

00:33:00.170 --> 00:33:04.080
without risking your health,
without risking a heart attack.

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:05.870
Now that's very different
than jumping into

00:33:05.870 --> 00:33:09.200
a very, very cold lake or

00:33:09.200 --> 00:33:12.780
I've seen these images of
people that will cut holes into

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:16.040
frozen over lakes and they'll
get into that cold water.

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:17.240
If you are trained to do that

00:33:17.240 --> 00:33:19.170
and you have the right
conditions, et cetera,

00:33:19.170 --> 00:33:21.270
that can be done reasonably safely,

00:33:21.270 --> 00:33:23.770
but that's certainly not
what I would start with.

00:33:23.770 --> 00:33:25.740
And for many people,
that would be too cold

00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:28.670
and indeed some people
can go into cold shock

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:30.170
and can die as a consequence of getting

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:32.250
to that extremely cold water very quickly.

00:33:32.250 --> 00:33:33.450
Now that's not to scare you away

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:34.880
from deliberate cold exposure.

00:33:34.880 --> 00:33:38.300
It's just to say that there's
no simple prescriptive

00:33:38.300 --> 00:33:39.840
of how cold to make environment

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:43.250
in order to extract maximum benefit

00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:46.260
for mental or physical performance.

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:49.270
So the simple rule of thumb is going to be

00:33:49.270 --> 00:33:51.150
place yourself into an environment

00:33:51.150 --> 00:33:53.590
that is uncomfortably cold,

00:33:53.590 --> 00:33:55.330
but that you can stay in safely.

00:33:55.330 --> 00:33:57.980
And you'll have to experiment
a bit and that number,

00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:00.220
meaning that temperature,
will vary from day to day.

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:03.460
It will vary across the 24 hour cycle

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:05.290
because of that endogenous,

00:34:05.290 --> 00:34:06.920
meaning that internal rhythm,

00:34:06.920 --> 00:34:09.250
in temperature that I
talked about earlier.

00:34:09.250 --> 00:34:10.190
Low early in the day,

00:34:10.190 --> 00:34:12.490
rises into the afternoon, drops at night.

00:34:12.490 --> 00:34:14.900
You can actually do this
experiment if you like.

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:17.730
Try getting into a cold
shower at 11 o'clock at night

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:20.520
if you want versus try doing it

00:34:20.520 --> 00:34:22.020
in the middle of the afternoon.

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:24.137
It's quite a different experience

00:34:24.137 --> 00:34:25.620
and by quite a different experience,

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:29.630
I mean it requires quite a
different degree of resilience

00:34:29.630 --> 00:34:31.110
and leaning into the practice.

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:34.810
Your willpower will have
to be higher I suspect

00:34:35.650 --> 00:34:39.840
late in the day as it
compared to early in the day,

00:34:39.840 --> 00:34:42.730
but that will vary of course
between individuals as well.

00:34:42.730 --> 00:34:43.990
So the most common question I get

00:34:43.990 --> 00:34:45.310
about deliberate cold exposure

00:34:45.310 --> 00:34:48.110
is how cold should the water be?

00:34:48.110 --> 00:34:51.250
And we've answered that with
uncomfortably cold to the point

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:52.083
where you want to get out,

00:34:52.083 --> 00:34:53.810
but you can safely stay in.

00:34:53.810 --> 00:34:55.520
The second most common question I get

00:34:55.520 --> 00:34:57.880
about deliberate cold
exposure is whether or not

00:34:57.880 --> 00:34:59.500
cold showers are as good,

00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:02.500
better or worse than cold water immersion

00:35:02.500 --> 00:35:04.300
up to the neck, for instance.

00:35:04.300 --> 00:35:06.360
I also get a lot of questions
about whether or not

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:08.950
cryo chambers are better
than all the others,

00:35:08.950 --> 00:35:10.300
et cetera, et cetera.

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:12.020
I'm going to make all of that

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:14.970
very simple for you by
saying cold water immersion

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:18.270
up to the neck with your
feet and hands submerged also

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:20.093
is going to be the most effective.

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:23.740
Second best would be cold shower.

00:35:23.740 --> 00:35:26.050
Third best would be to go outside

00:35:26.050 --> 00:35:29.480
with a minimum amount of
clothing, but of course,

00:35:29.480 --> 00:35:32.100
clothing that is culturally appropriate

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:35.580
and that would allow you to
experience cold to the point

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:38.470
where you would almost want
to shiver or start shivering.

00:35:38.470 --> 00:35:40.060
Now there are a number of different,

00:35:40.060 --> 00:35:42.410
important constraints
that are going to dictate

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:44.580
whether or not you use
one form of cold exposure

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:45.420
or the other.

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:47.010
For instance, some
people don't have access

00:35:47.010 --> 00:35:48.040
to cold water immersion.

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:53.040
They don't have access to ice
baths or cold water tanks,

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:55.220
cold ocean or cold lakes, et cetera.

00:35:55.220 --> 00:35:58.800
In that case, showers would
be the next best solution.

00:35:58.800 --> 00:36:01.210
I do want to emphasize that
there have been very few,

00:36:01.210 --> 00:36:04.410
if any, studies of cold showers,

00:36:04.410 --> 00:36:07.250
and you can imagine why
this would be the case.

00:36:07.250 --> 00:36:08.083
In a laboratory,

00:36:08.083 --> 00:36:11.010
you want to control for as
many variables as possible.

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:16.010
So placing people into a cold
water immersion or an ice bath

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:18.370
up to the neck and insisting
they keep their hands

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:21.270
and feet under is very easy to control.

00:36:21.270 --> 00:36:23.620
It means that everyone can
do essentially the same thing

00:36:23.620 --> 00:36:25.160
whereas with cold showers,

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:27.320
people are different sized bodies.

00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:28.620
Some people are going
to put their head under.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:31.290
Some people are going to lean forward.

00:36:31.290 --> 00:36:34.020
Measuring the amount of cold
water exposure on the body

00:36:34.020 --> 00:36:35.230
is very hard to do

00:36:35.230 --> 00:36:38.270
and so there aren't a lot
of studies of cold showers,

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:39.430
but of course,

00:36:39.430 --> 00:36:41.720
a lot of people don't have
access to cold water immersion

00:36:41.720 --> 00:36:43.420
so they have to use cold showers.

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:45.430
And if you don't have
access to both, of course,

00:36:45.430 --> 00:36:49.550
then going outside on a cold
day can be of a benefit.

00:36:49.550 --> 00:36:53.440
But I will point out
that the heat transfer

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:57.020
from your body into water is much higher,

00:36:57.020 --> 00:37:00.760
four times greater, if not even greater,

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:02.440
depending on the temperature of the water,

00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:04.410
in water as opposed to in air.

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:05.930
So it's going to be much more efficient

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:07.810
to do cold water immersion
than anything else,

00:37:07.810 --> 00:37:10.650
cold showers after that
and put yourself into

00:37:10.650 --> 00:37:13.440
a cold environment would
be the third best thing.

00:37:13.440 --> 00:37:15.030
I'm not going to get into cryo chambers

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:18.270
because they carry quite
a high degree of cost.

00:37:18.270 --> 00:37:20.660
And again, there aren't
many studies of them.

00:37:20.660 --> 00:37:23.100
So if you have access to cryo chambers,

00:37:23.100 --> 00:37:24.780
I'm sure that the cryo chamber facility

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:26.380
has told you about all
these incredible benefits

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:29.080
and I don't doubt that some
of those benefits truly exist,

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:31.380
but most people just
don't have the resources

00:37:31.380 --> 00:37:32.420
or the access to those

00:37:32.420 --> 00:37:33.670
so we're going to leave cryo chambers

00:37:33.670 --> 00:37:34.980
out of today's discussion.

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:37.120
And of course I realize
there's a fourth category

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:38.440
of cold exposure out there.

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:39.900
People are wearing ice vests.

00:37:39.900 --> 00:37:41.920
Believe it or not, those
exist, ice underwear.

00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:43.453
Yes, those exist.

00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:46.790
You can look for them
on Amazon if you like.

00:37:46.790 --> 00:37:49.380
They are putting cold
packs in their armpits

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:51.680
or in their groin or elsewhere in order to

00:37:51.680 --> 00:37:54.520
stimulate some of the effects of cold

00:37:54.520 --> 00:37:56.820
on mental and physical performance.

00:37:56.820 --> 00:37:59.970
I'm not going to address those
in too much detail today.

00:37:59.970 --> 00:38:02.460
They can be efficient in certain ways,

00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:04.940
but as you'll learn about
later in the episode,

00:38:04.940 --> 00:38:07.500
cooling the palms, the upper face

00:38:07.500 --> 00:38:08.650
and the bottoms of the feet

00:38:08.650 --> 00:38:10.450
is going to be far more efficient

00:38:10.450 --> 00:38:13.450
and unfortunately I think most
of the people that are using

00:38:13.450 --> 00:38:15.410
ice packs to increase
their core metabolism

00:38:15.410 --> 00:38:18.470
are not aware of the glabrous skin cooling

00:38:18.470 --> 00:38:20.860
and how it can be a very,
very potent stimulus

00:38:20.860 --> 00:38:23.130
so we'll return to that later.

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:24.400
Unless I say otherwise,

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:26.877
I'm mainly going to be focusing
on cold water immersion

00:38:26.877 --> 00:38:28.050
and cold showers.

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:30.840
So let's talk about protocols
for enhancing mental health

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:33.583
and performance using
deliberate cold exposure.

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:38.820
What happens when we get into
cold is that we experience

00:38:38.820 --> 00:38:41.480
an increase in norepinephrine,

00:38:41.480 --> 00:38:44.550
in noradrenaline release
and in adrenaline release.

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:47.220
The fact that cold
exposure, deliberate or no,

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:50.190
increases norepinephrine and epinephrine

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:52.530
in our brain and body means that it is

00:38:52.530 --> 00:38:54.670
a very reliable stimulus for increasing

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:56.590
norepinephrine and epinephrine.

00:38:56.590 --> 00:38:58.440
That's sort of an obvious statement,

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:02.070
but that obvious
statement can be leveraged

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:05.580
to systematically build up
what we call resilience.

00:39:05.580 --> 00:39:08.710
Now, when we experience
a stressor in life,

00:39:08.710 --> 00:39:10.850
whether or not it's something bad happens

00:39:10.850 --> 00:39:14.270
in our relationship or something
bad happens in the world

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:16.390
and we feel stress,

00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:18.500
that stress is the
consequence of increases

00:39:18.500 --> 00:39:22.010
in norepinephrine and epinephrine
in our brain and body.

00:39:22.010 --> 00:39:23.700
Very similar, if not identical,

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:24.760
to the kinds of increases

00:39:24.760 --> 00:39:27.230
that come from deliberate cold exposure.

00:39:27.230 --> 00:39:29.990
So deliberate cold
exposure is an opportunity

00:39:29.990 --> 00:39:33.510
to deliberately stress our body and yet,

00:39:33.510 --> 00:39:34.560
because it's deliberate

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:36.050
and because we can take certain steps,

00:39:36.050 --> 00:39:38.390
which I'll describe in a moment,

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:41.880
we can learn to maintain mental clarity,

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:45.730
we can learn to maintain
calm while our body

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:47.540
is in a state of stress.

00:39:47.540 --> 00:39:49.840
And that can be immensely useful

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:53.320
when encountering stressors
in other parts of life.

00:39:53.320 --> 00:39:55.580
And that's what we call
resilience or grit,

00:39:55.580 --> 00:39:57.520
our ability or mental toughness,

00:39:57.520 --> 00:40:01.610
our ability to lean into
challenge or to tolerate challenge

00:40:01.610 --> 00:40:04.200
while keeping our heads
straight, so to speak.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:07.730
So one simple protocol
for increasing resilience

00:40:07.730 --> 00:40:10.450
is to pick a temperature
that's uncomfortable

00:40:10.450 --> 00:40:12.570
of shower or cold immersion,

00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:15.870
and then to get in for a
certain duration of time

00:40:15.870 --> 00:40:17.240
and then to get out.

00:40:17.240 --> 00:40:18.270
Now, it's important to understand

00:40:18.270 --> 00:40:20.190
that people will
experience different levels

00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:22.360
of norepinephrine and adrenaline release

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:24.200
when getting into cold water.

00:40:24.200 --> 00:40:28.010
Some people, because they
dread the cold so much,

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:29.777
will actually experience norepinephrine

00:40:29.777 --> 00:40:31.080
and epinephrine increases

00:40:31.080 --> 00:40:33.610
even before they get into the cold water

00:40:33.610 --> 00:40:35.150
or under the cold shower.

00:40:35.150 --> 00:40:36.270
Now you may have experienced this.

00:40:36.270 --> 00:40:37.930
I've certainly experienced this.

00:40:37.930 --> 00:40:39.450
I'm dreading it, I don't want to do it

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:41.860
and I have to force myself to do it.

00:40:41.860 --> 00:40:45.610
And indeed epinephrine and
norepinephrine and its surges

00:40:45.610 --> 00:40:48.920
can be thought of as sort of
walls that we have to confront

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:49.960
and go over.

00:40:49.960 --> 00:40:52.060
And I'd like you to
conceptualize them that way,

00:40:52.060 --> 00:40:54.610
because it allows us to build protocols

00:40:54.610 --> 00:40:58.120
that can be very
objective and can allow us

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:01.280
to monitor our progress in
terms of building resilience.

00:41:01.280 --> 00:41:04.170
So one option is to simply say, okay,

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:05.320
I'm going to force myself

00:41:05.320 --> 00:41:07.750
to get into the cold
shower for one minute.

00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:09.390
How cold, again, uncomfortably cold,

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:10.460
but you can stay in safely

00:41:10.460 --> 00:41:13.720
or I'm going to get into
the ice bath for one minute.

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:16.860
Ice baths are very cold inevitably.

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:19.480
And what is also inevitable is that

00:41:19.480 --> 00:41:20.910
when you get into the cold,

00:41:20.910 --> 00:41:22.400
you will experience a surge

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:24.130
in epinephrine and norepinephrine.

00:41:24.130 --> 00:41:26.710
That's non-negotiable
because it's mediated

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:29.290
by cold receptors on
the surface of your body

00:41:29.290 --> 00:41:32.100
and your skin and the way
that they trigger the release

00:41:32.100 --> 00:41:33.610
of norepinephrine and epinephrine,

00:41:33.610 --> 00:41:35.300
not just from the adrenals,

00:41:35.300 --> 00:41:37.270
from the adrenal glands
above your kidneys,

00:41:37.270 --> 00:41:38.820
but also from regions of your brain,

00:41:38.820 --> 00:41:39.990
like the locus coeruleus,

00:41:39.990 --> 00:41:42.940
which cause increases in
the tension and alertness,

00:41:42.940 --> 00:41:45.727
and from other locations in
your body where epinephrine

00:41:45.727 --> 00:41:47.150
and norepinephrine are released.

00:41:47.150 --> 00:41:51.800
In other words, cold is
a non-negotiable stimulus

00:41:51.800 --> 00:41:55.140
for increasing epinephrine
and norepinephrine.

00:41:55.140 --> 00:41:58.010
Even if you are the
toughest person in the world

00:41:58.010 --> 00:41:59.503
and you love the cold,

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:02.560
that increase in epinephrine
and norepinephrine

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:04.070
is going to happen.

00:42:04.070 --> 00:42:06.750
So the way to think about
norepinephrine and epinephrine

00:42:06.750 --> 00:42:09.250
in this context of
building mental resilience

00:42:09.250 --> 00:42:11.480
is that you have two options.

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:15.370
You can either try to
extend the duration of time

00:42:15.370 --> 00:42:17.480
that you are in the
deliberate cold exposure.

00:42:17.480 --> 00:42:21.010
So going from one minute to
75 seconds to two minutes

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:23.440
and so on over a period of days

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:25.837
or one way to approach this

00:42:25.837 --> 00:42:27.870
and the way that I particularly favor

00:42:27.870 --> 00:42:30.747
is to take the context of the day

00:42:30.747 --> 00:42:32.610
and the moment into account,

00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:36.300
meaning we have different
levels of grit and resilience

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:38.730
on different days and
depending on the landscape

00:42:38.730 --> 00:42:39.780
of our life at the time,

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:42.120
even the time of day that
we're doing these protocols

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:46.303
and start to be able to sense
the release of epinephrine,

00:42:47.809 --> 00:42:50.410
and norepinephrine in our
brain and body and see those

00:42:50.410 --> 00:42:52.520
as walls that we want to climb over

00:42:52.520 --> 00:42:55.320
in order to build resilience
and to start counting

00:42:55.320 --> 00:42:57.950
the number of walls that we traverse

00:42:57.950 --> 00:42:59.790
and the distance between those walls

00:42:59.790 --> 00:43:01.730
as we do deliberate cold exposure.

00:43:01.730 --> 00:43:05.290
Let me give you an example
of the timed protocol

00:43:05.290 --> 00:43:06.890
because that one is very straightforward,

00:43:06.890 --> 00:43:09.600
although I do not think it is as powerful

00:43:09.600 --> 00:43:11.050
for building mental resilience.

00:43:11.050 --> 00:43:12.920
The time protocol would be Monday,

00:43:12.920 --> 00:43:15.350
I do one minute of
deliberate cold exposure

00:43:15.350 --> 00:43:16.190
at a given temperature.

00:43:16.190 --> 00:43:19.340
Wednesday, I extend
that by 50% and Friday,

00:43:19.340 --> 00:43:22.380
I do deliberate cold
exposure for twice as long

00:43:22.380 --> 00:43:23.850
as I did it on Monday.

00:43:23.850 --> 00:43:26.240
And if I were to continue that every week,

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:27.100
Monday, Wednesday, Friday,

00:43:27.100 --> 00:43:29.530
I would continue to either
increase the duration

00:43:29.530 --> 00:43:34.160
or I would lower the temperature
and reduce the duration,

00:43:34.160 --> 00:43:36.520
this kind of thing, very much
like sets and reps in the gym.

00:43:36.520 --> 00:43:38.710
Now that option is very objective.

00:43:38.710 --> 00:43:40.300
You could even log it in a book

00:43:40.300 --> 00:43:44.973
and as you develop the ability
to stay in cold temperatures,

00:43:46.160 --> 00:43:48.200
even progressively colder
and colder temperatures

00:43:48.200 --> 00:43:50.230
for longer and longer periods of time,

00:43:50.230 --> 00:43:52.470
you will become more resilient.

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:53.660
What do I mean by that?

00:43:53.660 --> 00:43:55.640
Well, my operational
definition of resilience

00:43:55.640 --> 00:43:59.160
is that you are able to resist
escape from the stressor,

00:43:59.160 --> 00:44:02.910
the cold, by virtue of your willpower,

00:44:02.910 --> 00:44:05.800
which is really your prefrontal cortex

00:44:05.800 --> 00:44:08.830
causing top down control on your reflexes

00:44:08.830 --> 00:44:10.830
and your limbic system
and your hypothalamus,

00:44:10.830 --> 00:44:11.870
which are basically telling you

00:44:11.870 --> 00:44:13.960
to get out of that cold water,

00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:17.540
get out of that cold
environment and in doing so,

00:44:17.540 --> 00:44:19.070
you are basically getting better

00:44:19.070 --> 00:44:21.890
at controlling your behavior
when your brain and body

00:44:21.890 --> 00:44:24.040
are flooded with
norepinephrine and epinephrine.

00:44:24.040 --> 00:44:26.630
That's a very reductionist
way to explain resilience

00:44:26.630 --> 00:44:28.140
or grit or mental toughness,

00:44:28.140 --> 00:44:30.530
but it's a reductionist
way of explaining it

00:44:30.530 --> 00:44:32.550
that is very closely
tied to the biology end

00:44:32.550 --> 00:44:33.670
of the psychology.

00:44:33.670 --> 00:44:37.710
And it is a fact that
norepinephrine and epinephrine

00:44:37.710 --> 00:44:39.200
release in the brain and body

00:44:39.200 --> 00:44:43.550
are the generic universal
code for stressor.

00:44:43.550 --> 00:44:45.520
There is no unique chemical signature

00:44:45.520 --> 00:44:48.430
for different forms of
stressors, that is the only one,

00:44:48.430 --> 00:44:49.263
although of course there are

00:44:49.263 --> 00:44:50.860
other chemicals involved as well.

00:44:50.860 --> 00:44:52.780
So you could go for time and you could try

00:44:52.780 --> 00:44:55.470
and reduce the temperature
and increase the time

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:57.480
over a period of days or weeks.

00:44:57.480 --> 00:45:00.050
Now that's an attractive
way to approach things,

00:45:00.050 --> 00:45:03.790
but the problem is that you
don't have an infinite amount

00:45:03.790 --> 00:45:06.040
of room with which to lower temperature,

00:45:06.040 --> 00:45:08.140
because eventually you
will get into temperatures

00:45:08.140 --> 00:45:10.900
that are either so cold
that they are dangerous

00:45:10.900 --> 00:45:13.390
or you have to stay in cold temperatures

00:45:13.390 --> 00:45:16.980
for such long periods that
it becomes impractical

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:18.970
because presumably you also have to

00:45:18.970 --> 00:45:20.490
take care of other aspects of your life,

00:45:20.490 --> 00:45:23.060
you can't just sit all
day in the ice bath.

00:45:23.060 --> 00:45:24.270
Now for that reason,

00:45:24.270 --> 00:45:27.640
I favor a protocol in which
you build mental resilience

00:45:27.640 --> 00:45:29.490
and mental toughness through

00:45:29.490 --> 00:45:31.090
two different types of protocols.

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:33.420
The first one involves counting walls.

00:45:33.420 --> 00:45:34.430
Now, what do I mean by walls?

00:45:34.430 --> 00:45:37.210
I mean the sensation of, no,

00:45:37.210 --> 00:45:41.320
I don't want to do this and
the idea or the sensation

00:45:41.320 --> 00:45:43.710
in your brain and body that
you actually want to leave

00:45:43.710 --> 00:45:45.560
that environment and go warm up.

00:45:45.560 --> 00:45:46.720
Now again, for some people,

00:45:46.720 --> 00:45:49.160
that will be even before
getting into the ice bath

00:45:49.160 --> 00:45:50.110
or cold shower.

00:45:50.110 --> 00:45:52.740
So if you are feeling very resistant

00:45:52.740 --> 00:45:54.040
to getting into the ice bath

00:45:54.040 --> 00:45:56.340
or cold shower and you manage to do that,

00:45:56.340 --> 00:45:59.203
that's going over what
I would call one wall.

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:01.690
Then for some period of time,

00:46:01.690 --> 00:46:05.600
you might actually feel
comfortable in the ice bath,

00:46:05.600 --> 00:46:07.800
cold water or cold shower.

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:09.560
And you feel like you could stay there

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:10.450
for some period of time,

00:46:10.450 --> 00:46:12.300
that you could stay there
for a minute or two minutes,

00:46:12.300 --> 00:46:15.890
but inevitably, the next wall will arrive.

00:46:15.890 --> 00:46:17.870
And I would encourage
you to pay attention to

00:46:17.870 --> 00:46:19.330
when that next wall arrives

00:46:19.330 --> 00:46:21.000
and actually having an awareness,

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:24.380
that so-called interceptive
awareness as we call it,

00:46:24.380 --> 00:46:27.690
of when that next surge
adrenaline epinephrine comes

00:46:27.690 --> 00:46:30.370
or whether or not it
reaches a certain threshold

00:46:30.370 --> 00:46:32.440
in your brain and body that
you feel you want to get out

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:36.270
and you're able to stay in for
even just 10 seconds longer,

00:46:36.270 --> 00:46:39.290
that means you've
traversed yet another wall.

00:46:39.290 --> 00:46:42.190
And if you continue to stay
in that cold environment,

00:46:42.190 --> 00:46:43.907
you'll find that the next wall will come

00:46:43.907 --> 00:46:45.070
and the next wall will come.

00:46:45.070 --> 00:46:48.300
Now eventually of course,
you will get very, very numb

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:49.400
depending on how cold it is

00:46:49.400 --> 00:46:51.220
and you could also place
yourself into danger.

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:54.050
So you have to maintain cognitive control,

00:46:54.050 --> 00:46:56.300
counting these walls,
traversing these walls,

00:46:56.300 --> 00:46:59.160
but getting out at some point, of course.

00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:03.890
So my favorite protocol for
building mental toughness

00:47:03.890 --> 00:47:06.240
AKA grit, AKA resilience,

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:08.370
is to take into account that some days,

00:47:08.370 --> 00:47:10.150
just getting into the ice bath

00:47:10.150 --> 00:47:12.680
or cold shower represents a
wall, some days it doesn't.

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:15.740
Some days you get in and you
feel you could go 10 minutes,

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:17.090
other days you get in and you feel like

00:47:17.090 --> 00:47:20.460
you could only go a minute and
setting a designated number

00:47:20.460 --> 00:47:23.010
of walls before you start the protocol

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:24.680
is going to be very beneficial here.

00:47:24.680 --> 00:47:27.090
So you say, as long as I can do it safely,

00:47:27.090 --> 00:47:29.400
I'm going to do three walls today.

00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:30.530
The first wall is getting in.

00:47:30.530 --> 00:47:32.800
The second wall will
arrive when it arrives

00:47:32.800 --> 00:47:35.410
and the third wall will
arrive when it arrives

00:47:35.410 --> 00:47:37.130
and I'll get over that
wall and then I'll get out.

00:47:37.130 --> 00:47:38.730
The next day, you might do five walls.

00:47:38.730 --> 00:47:41.960
The next day, you might
do three walls again,

00:47:41.960 --> 00:47:43.530
but you might lower the temperature.

00:47:43.530 --> 00:47:45.920
This gives you tremendous flexibility

00:47:45.920 --> 00:47:48.840
and indeed it gives you much more latitude

00:47:48.840 --> 00:47:52.520
to be able to use the same
temperatures in different ways,

00:47:52.520 --> 00:47:54.740
or to reduce the temperature
only a little bit

00:47:54.740 --> 00:47:56.800
and still get a lot of stimulus,

00:47:56.800 --> 00:48:00.170
meaning a lot of results
out of a given protocol.

00:48:00.170 --> 00:48:02.900
Whereas people who are just
going for temperature and time

00:48:02.900 --> 00:48:04.950
eventually become cold adapted.

00:48:04.950 --> 00:48:07.800
They get very, very good
at doing three minutes

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:10.760
or six minutes or even 10
minutes at a given temperature

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:12.140
and so then they feel like they have

00:48:12.140 --> 00:48:14.530
to lower the temperature
even more and even more

00:48:14.530 --> 00:48:16.290
and eventually they just bought them out.

00:48:16.290 --> 00:48:17.320
There's nowhere else to go.

00:48:17.320 --> 00:48:21.140
There's no way to get
improvements out of the protocol.

00:48:21.140 --> 00:48:24.280
At least not in terms
of mental resilience.

00:48:24.280 --> 00:48:26.610
Of course, there's still
the positive effects

00:48:26.610 --> 00:48:28.630
on inflammation and metabolism, et cetera,

00:48:28.630 --> 00:48:30.840
that we'll talk about in a little bit.

00:48:30.840 --> 00:48:33.540
But the key thing here
is to design protocols

00:48:33.540 --> 00:48:35.630
that are going to work for you over time

00:48:35.630 --> 00:48:37.410
and for you very, very hardy,

00:48:37.410 --> 00:48:39.550
very, very tough guys and gals out there

00:48:39.550 --> 00:48:40.680
that can get right into

00:48:40.680 --> 00:48:43.120
an ice bath or a very, very cold immersion

00:48:43.120 --> 00:48:45.530
and you can just grind it
out for six or 10 minutes,

00:48:45.530 --> 00:48:48.730
or you can even do that
by remaining peaceful,

00:48:48.730 --> 00:48:51.100
well more points to you, but guess what?

00:48:51.100 --> 00:48:53.270
That's the equivalent of
already having loaded up

00:48:53.270 --> 00:48:56.360
the barbell with 600 pounds
and done your 10 reps.

00:48:56.360 --> 00:48:59.000
There's not a whole
lot more variable space

00:48:59.000 --> 00:49:02.330
with which to get benefits
from that stimulus.

00:49:02.330 --> 00:49:03.440
And in the weight room,

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:05.330
people understand that you
can adjust, for instance,

00:49:05.330 --> 00:49:06.510
the speed of the movement,

00:49:06.510 --> 00:49:08.010
or you can start combining that movement

00:49:08.010 --> 00:49:09.660
with pre-exhaustion, et cetera.

00:49:09.660 --> 00:49:11.100
With cold exposure,

00:49:11.100 --> 00:49:13.620
you don't have as much
variable space to play with.

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:15.710
So if your goal is to build resilience,

00:49:15.710 --> 00:49:18.560
either go for time as a
function of temperature,

00:49:18.560 --> 00:49:23.250
or what I suggest is to
start recognizing these walls

00:49:23.250 --> 00:49:25.900
as an experience of resistance in you

00:49:25.900 --> 00:49:28.850
and going over those walls,
set a certain number of walls

00:49:28.850 --> 00:49:31.840
that you're going to go over
on a given day and do that

00:49:31.840 --> 00:49:34.680
at a given temperature,
and then to mix it up.

00:49:34.680 --> 00:49:38.450
And ideally you might even
throw in one more wall

00:49:38.450 --> 00:49:39.283
at the end,

00:49:39.283 --> 00:49:41.110
if you're really feeling bold and brave

00:49:41.110 --> 00:49:43.460
because that's going to
build out further resilience.

00:49:43.460 --> 00:49:45.500
But if you want cold
exposure to work for you

00:49:45.500 --> 00:49:46.930
for sake of building up resilience

00:49:46.930 --> 00:49:48.510
and mental toughness over time,

00:49:48.510 --> 00:49:51.380
you're going to want to
vary this parameter space

00:49:51.380 --> 00:49:52.750
in some sort of way.

00:49:52.750 --> 00:49:56.100
And you don't have to be
super systematic about it.

00:49:56.100 --> 00:49:58.350
That's the beauty of this kind of approach

00:49:58.350 --> 00:49:59.930
because you're relying on the fact

00:49:59.930 --> 00:50:02.900
that those walls really represent times

00:50:02.900 --> 00:50:05.410
in which you are forcing
your top down control,

00:50:05.410 --> 00:50:08.150
your prefrontal cortex to
clamp down on your reflex

00:50:08.150 --> 00:50:11.050
and you're learning behavioral
control in the context

00:50:11.050 --> 00:50:12.980
of your body having elevated levels

00:50:12.980 --> 00:50:15.550
of these catecholamines,
norepinephrine and epinephrine.

00:50:15.550 --> 00:50:17.500
And that translates to real life

00:50:17.500 --> 00:50:21.560
in a much more realistic way I
believe because in real life,

00:50:21.560 --> 00:50:24.330
you're not really engaging in stressors

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:26.270
for a given amount of time

00:50:26.270 --> 00:50:28.040
that you know how long it's going to last

00:50:28.040 --> 00:50:29.460
and you know the context, no.

00:50:29.460 --> 00:50:31.830
Most stressors arrive
in the form of surprises

00:50:31.830 --> 00:50:34.850
we don't like, text messages
that deliver bad news,

00:50:34.850 --> 00:50:37.530
information about the
outside world or real world

00:50:37.530 --> 00:50:40.010
and online interactions
that send our system

00:50:40.010 --> 00:50:42.850
into a state of increased
norepinephrine and epinephrine.

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:44.660
And if you start to
think of those as walls

00:50:44.660 --> 00:50:46.100
that you can tolerate and climb over

00:50:46.100 --> 00:50:48.500
while staying and calm and clear of mind,

00:50:48.500 --> 00:50:52.180
then you can really
imagine how the ice bath

00:50:52.180 --> 00:50:55.000
and other forms of cold
exposure are really serving

00:50:55.000 --> 00:50:58.070
to train you up for real life stressors.

00:50:58.070 --> 00:51:00.250
The next question that I always get is

00:51:00.250 --> 00:51:02.220
what should my mental state be

00:51:02.220 --> 00:51:04.940
while I'm exposing myself
to this uncomfortable,

00:51:04.940 --> 00:51:07.680
yet safe condition of cold?

00:51:07.680 --> 00:51:09.590
Well, you have two options

00:51:09.590 --> 00:51:11.490
and there are probably
other options as well.

00:51:11.490 --> 00:51:14.010
One is to try and calm yourself

00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:17.680
to remain as mentally still as possible.

00:51:17.680 --> 00:51:21.150
The other is to lean into that challenge

00:51:21.150 --> 00:51:23.420
and so to grind it out and here,

00:51:23.420 --> 00:51:25.750
I have to say that this is
a lot like teaching someone

00:51:25.750 --> 00:51:27.410
to drive on a gravel road.

00:51:27.410 --> 00:51:29.900
For any of you that have
driven on a gravel road,

00:51:29.900 --> 00:51:32.870
you know that there is no optimal speed

00:51:32.870 --> 00:51:33.990
for all gravel roads.

00:51:33.990 --> 00:51:35.937
It depends on the density
of the gravel, et cetera,

00:51:35.937 --> 00:51:37.760
and the vehicle, et cetera.

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:40.140
So for instance, on some gravel roads,

00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:42.990
when you start to drive and
the dust starts to kick up,

00:51:42.990 --> 00:51:44.700
your best option is to drive fast

00:51:44.700 --> 00:51:46.740
and put that dust cloud behind you.

00:51:46.740 --> 00:51:49.120
On other gravel roads,
if you try and do that,

00:51:49.120 --> 00:51:50.800
the dust actually kicks
up around the vehicle

00:51:50.800 --> 00:51:52.020
and it makes it hard to see

00:51:52.020 --> 00:51:53.960
and sometimes you have to slow down.

00:51:53.960 --> 00:51:55.810
The same thing is true for getting through

00:51:55.810 --> 00:51:57.520
deliberate cold exposure.

00:51:57.520 --> 00:51:59.530
Sometimes it's easier to calm yourself.

00:51:59.530 --> 00:52:01.730
One way to do that is
through double inhales

00:52:01.730 --> 00:52:04.000
through the nose and extended
exhales through the mouth,

00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:06.770
or simply by trying to
control your breathing

00:52:06.770 --> 00:52:09.740
and reduce the pace of
your breath and increase

00:52:09.740 --> 00:52:11.233
the volume of your breathing.

00:52:12.370 --> 00:52:15.420
I have to say that everyone
experiences a shortening

00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:18.340
of breath when they get into
uncomfortably cold water,

00:52:18.340 --> 00:52:21.620
that is a universal
physiological response.

00:52:21.620 --> 00:52:26.210
Everyone also experiences
a 30 to 80% decrease

00:52:26.210 --> 00:52:29.430
in cognitive function, in
particular, the frontal cortex.

00:52:29.430 --> 00:52:31.440
The metabolism of your
frontal cortex goes down,

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:34.440
the metabolism meaning the
activity of brain areas

00:52:34.440 --> 00:52:36.970
associated with stress
and panic goes way up.

00:52:36.970 --> 00:52:41.400
And so anchoring your mind
in cognitive activities

00:52:41.400 --> 00:52:43.220
as you get into the cold can be very,

00:52:43.220 --> 00:52:46.860
very helpful for
maintaining clarity of mind.

00:52:46.860 --> 00:52:48.820
In fact, one thing that
I sometimes recommend

00:52:48.820 --> 00:52:50.690
is that people try and engage

00:52:50.690 --> 00:52:53.720
in some sort of cognitive
exercise while in the cold,

00:52:53.720 --> 00:52:55.610
not as a form of distraction,

00:52:55.610 --> 00:52:57.640
but as a way to maintain
clarity of thinking

00:52:57.640 --> 00:52:59.890
and to learn how to do that
when the body is flooded

00:52:59.890 --> 00:53:02.290
with all these chemicals
that make us stressed.

00:53:02.290 --> 00:53:03.123
So for instance,

00:53:03.123 --> 00:53:06.050
you could do math problems and
not two plus two equals four,

00:53:06.050 --> 00:53:08.250
not three times three equals nine,

00:53:08.250 --> 00:53:10.740
but things that require
a little bit more focus

00:53:10.740 --> 00:53:13.380
and attention working memory and so forth.

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:16.470
You could also start to have thoughts

00:53:16.470 --> 00:53:20.600
that you deliberately impose
a full sentence structure on.

00:53:20.600 --> 00:53:22.140
That's actually quite tough.

00:53:22.140 --> 00:53:25.320
You could try and recall
specific bouts of information

00:53:25.320 --> 00:53:26.153
that are challenging.

00:53:26.153 --> 00:53:29.280
This is teaching your
mind how to stay online,

00:53:29.280 --> 00:53:30.113
or rather I should say,

00:53:30.113 --> 00:53:32.490
this is you teaching
your prefrontal cortex,

00:53:32.490 --> 00:53:35.880
how to stay engaged while you
have high levels of stress

00:53:35.880 --> 00:53:36.880
in your body.

00:53:36.880 --> 00:53:39.290
Years ago, I had a friend who works

00:53:39.290 --> 00:53:42.490
in the neuroscience world,
research neuroscientist,

00:53:42.490 --> 00:53:45.720
who was obsessed with
this very bizarre sport

00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:47.700
that I don't necessarily recommend at all,

00:53:47.700 --> 00:53:50.630
which is the combination
of boxing and chess.

00:53:50.630 --> 00:53:51.840
You may have seen this on YouTube

00:53:51.840 --> 00:53:53.280
where people will box around,

00:53:53.280 --> 00:53:56.540
legitimate boxing around,
they're sparring all out often.

00:53:56.540 --> 00:53:58.220
And then at the end of the round,

00:53:58.220 --> 00:53:59.310
instead of resting in the corner,

00:53:59.310 --> 00:54:01.030
they actually sit down and play chess,

00:54:01.030 --> 00:54:03.100
and then they go back to
boxing and back to chess.

00:54:03.100 --> 00:54:05.590
Again, not a sport that I recommend,

00:54:05.590 --> 00:54:08.110
but the reason he was
obsessed with this is because

00:54:08.110 --> 00:54:11.410
he studies the impact of stress
on cognitive performance.

00:54:11.410 --> 00:54:14.370
And what that particular
very bizarre sport was doing

00:54:14.370 --> 00:54:15.630
was toggling back and forth

00:54:15.630 --> 00:54:17.610
between different states of mind.

00:54:17.610 --> 00:54:22.090
Now it's used both to
increase cognitive clarity

00:54:22.090 --> 00:54:23.630
for the fighter when they box,

00:54:23.630 --> 00:54:25.460
because staying calm and clear thinking

00:54:25.460 --> 00:54:27.750
is very important to
winning boxing matches.

00:54:27.750 --> 00:54:29.810
Believe it or not, it's
not an all outrage.

00:54:29.810 --> 00:54:32.220
It's a very calculated
game of mental chess

00:54:32.220 --> 00:54:34.570
and physical chess
that's quite high stakes

00:54:34.570 --> 00:54:35.520
as you can imagine.

00:54:36.530 --> 00:54:40.730
It's also used in some circles
as a way to teach people

00:54:40.730 --> 00:54:42.670
how to engage in cognitive performance

00:54:42.670 --> 00:54:44.390
when their body is simply
filled to a stress.

00:54:44.390 --> 00:54:46.870
So in the boxing chess example,

00:54:46.870 --> 00:54:50.400
the replacement for the cold
water is actually the boxing,

00:54:50.400 --> 00:54:52.310
it's the thing that's
supposed to induce the stress

00:54:52.310 --> 00:54:54.590
cause getting hit is
stressful and the risk

00:54:54.590 --> 00:54:57.000
of getting hit is
stressful for most people.

00:54:57.000 --> 00:55:00.740
So again, if you think about
deliberate cold exposure

00:55:00.740 --> 00:55:03.400
as a way of just
systematically and reliably

00:55:03.400 --> 00:55:05.450
inducing epinephrine and
norepinephrine release

00:55:05.450 --> 00:55:07.040
and delivering stress,

00:55:07.040 --> 00:55:09.750
well then this idea of
maintaining cognitive clarity

00:55:09.750 --> 00:55:11.640
and actually engaging in cognitive tasks

00:55:11.640 --> 00:55:14.290
while in the ice bath or cold shower

00:55:14.290 --> 00:55:15.650
can actually be very beneficial.

00:55:15.650 --> 00:55:16.980
Even though it might
sound a little bit silly,

00:55:16.980 --> 00:55:18.670
you are really training up your ability

00:55:18.670 --> 00:55:20.430
to keep your brain working

00:55:20.430 --> 00:55:24.200
when the reflex is to shut
down the parts of your brain

00:55:24.200 --> 00:55:26.340
that are involved in deliberate
planning and thinking.

00:55:26.340 --> 00:55:29.330
Now another important aspect
of deliberate cold exposure

00:55:29.330 --> 00:55:32.290
that I rarely if ever hear discussed,

00:55:32.290 --> 00:55:35.510
but is vitally important is whether or not

00:55:35.510 --> 00:55:37.710
you move around or not.

00:55:37.710 --> 00:55:38.900
And here's the reason.

00:55:38.900 --> 00:55:43.300
When you get into cold
water and you remain there

00:55:43.300 --> 00:55:44.280
for some period of time,

00:55:44.280 --> 00:55:47.710
your body is generating
heat and that heat generates

00:55:47.710 --> 00:55:49.380
what's called a thermal layer

00:55:49.380 --> 00:55:51.283
that surrounds your entire body.

00:55:52.270 --> 00:55:54.410
So if you stay still,

00:55:54.410 --> 00:55:57.270
you are actually warmer
than if you move around,

00:55:57.270 --> 00:55:58.470
you can try this the next time

00:55:58.470 --> 00:55:59.960
you're doing your
deliberate cold exposure.

00:55:59.960 --> 00:56:01.220
If you're submerged up to the neck,

00:56:01.220 --> 00:56:03.680
sit there for about 10, 30 seconds

00:56:03.680 --> 00:56:05.510
and be very, very still of body.

00:56:05.510 --> 00:56:07.820
In fact, this is the way
that most people start

00:56:07.820 --> 00:56:09.130
to do deliberate cold exposure.

00:56:09.130 --> 00:56:10.460
They give this very stoic look.

00:56:10.460 --> 00:56:12.000
They don't blink, they look very peaceful.

00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:13.810
Some of them even look tough

00:56:13.810 --> 00:56:17.800
or they make a very even A, emotional face

00:56:17.800 --> 00:56:20.190
and so it looks like they're really tough,

00:56:20.190 --> 00:56:22.410
but they are so still
that believe it or not,

00:56:22.410 --> 00:56:26.560
they're not providing
the most potent stimulus.

00:56:26.560 --> 00:56:30.690
If they or you were to
move around in that water,

00:56:30.690 --> 00:56:32.730
what would happen is you'd
break up the thermal layer

00:56:32.730 --> 00:56:36.000
and that you actually
experience that as much colder.

00:56:36.000 --> 00:56:39.140
So if you really want to
push the resilience aspect,

00:56:39.140 --> 00:56:42.070
or for instance, if you want
to use a given temperature

00:56:42.070 --> 00:56:43.300
that you're comfortable in,

00:56:43.300 --> 00:56:45.540
but that you want to increase the stimulus

00:56:45.540 --> 00:56:47.390
and you want to get some more benefit

00:56:47.390 --> 00:56:49.190
for mental resilience training,

00:56:49.190 --> 00:56:51.600
well then get into the cold water,

00:56:51.600 --> 00:56:53.850
move your body around continuously,

00:56:53.850 --> 00:56:55.370
but try and keep your mind still,

00:56:55.370 --> 00:56:57.590
or even do some sort of cognitive task.

00:56:57.590 --> 00:56:59.200
So as you're starting to realize,

00:56:59.200 --> 00:57:00.360
there are a bunch of different variables

00:57:00.360 --> 00:57:02.240
that you can play with while maintaining

00:57:02.240 --> 00:57:05.160
the same temperature of
water and in doing so,

00:57:05.160 --> 00:57:08.650
really keep you in the zone of what should

00:57:08.650 --> 00:57:10.850
and absolutely has to be safe for you

00:57:10.850 --> 00:57:13.160
without having to just
continually drop the temperature

00:57:13.160 --> 00:57:15.750
from say 60 degrees to 55 to 40 to 33

00:57:15.750 --> 00:57:16.670
because as I mentioned before,

00:57:16.670 --> 00:57:18.310
eventually you're going to bottom out.

00:57:18.310 --> 00:57:20.750
So if you're one of those
people that likes to look tough

00:57:20.750 --> 00:57:22.930
or really relaxed while
you're in the ice bath

00:57:22.930 --> 00:57:24.120
or cold water immersion,

00:57:24.120 --> 00:57:26.553
just realize that you're
actually cheating yourself out

00:57:26.553 --> 00:57:28.370
of part of the stimulus.

00:57:28.370 --> 00:57:31.470
Keep those limbs moving and of course,

00:57:31.470 --> 00:57:33.640
limbs under the water, feet and hands

00:57:33.640 --> 00:57:35.770
is going to be a more potent stimulus

00:57:35.770 --> 00:57:38.450
than hands and feet out for reasons

00:57:38.450 --> 00:57:40.210
that should be obvious based
on what we talked about

00:57:40.210 --> 00:57:42.540
in terms of glabrous skin cooling.

00:57:42.540 --> 00:57:45.570
Keep those submerged, move your body,

00:57:45.570 --> 00:57:48.080
maybe move your knees up
and down, pedal your feet

00:57:48.080 --> 00:57:50.120
and trust me, it's going
to feel a lot colder

00:57:50.120 --> 00:57:52.300
than were you to remain stone still.

00:57:52.300 --> 00:57:53.500
Another very common question

00:57:53.500 --> 00:57:56.760
is how often to do
deliberate cold exposure.

00:57:56.760 --> 00:57:59.370
It's tough to make a
recommendation on that

00:57:59.370 --> 00:58:01.770
based on any peer reviewed study

00:58:01.770 --> 00:58:04.740
although there are a few in
humans that point to a threshold

00:58:04.740 --> 00:58:07.550
of 11 minutes total per week.

00:58:07.550 --> 00:58:09.440
So that's total throughout the week

00:58:09.440 --> 00:58:11.650
divided into two or four sessions

00:58:11.650 --> 00:58:13.580
of two or three minutes or so.

00:58:13.580 --> 00:58:16.380
Now that 11 minute cutoff
is not a strict threshold

00:58:16.380 --> 00:58:20.150
and is actually geared more
towards increases in metabolism,

00:58:20.150 --> 00:58:22.980
we'll get into this a little
bit later in the episode,

00:58:22.980 --> 00:58:25.640
but I think the 11 minute threshold,

00:58:25.640 --> 00:58:28.260
meaning 11 minutes total
of deliberate cold exposure

00:58:28.260 --> 00:58:31.460
per week is a pretty good number to use

00:58:31.460 --> 00:58:34.970
if you need a number in
order to keep you consistent.

00:58:34.970 --> 00:58:36.430
But as we talked about earlier,

00:58:36.430 --> 00:58:39.530
some of you are going
to be in the ice bath

00:58:39.530 --> 00:58:42.070
or cold immersion or cold
shower for one minute,

00:58:42.070 --> 00:58:44.170
others of you will be
in there for 10 minutes,

00:58:44.170 --> 00:58:47.590
depending on how frequent
and how high, if you will,

00:58:47.590 --> 00:58:50.180
those walls of adrenaline are coming.

00:58:50.180 --> 00:58:52.020
So for some of you,

00:58:52.020 --> 00:58:54.300
getting into a cold shower
for three minutes total

00:58:54.300 --> 00:58:57.530
for the whole week will
represent a tremendous

00:58:57.530 --> 00:59:00.080
achievement in terms of
willpower and overcoming

00:59:00.080 --> 00:59:02.380
the resistance to doing
that, overcoming those walls.

00:59:02.380 --> 00:59:05.000
For others of you, three
minutes is nothing.

00:59:05.000 --> 00:59:06.920
So what do I recommend,

00:59:06.920 --> 00:59:11.110
I recommend that you get at
least 11 minutes total per week,

00:59:11.110 --> 00:59:13.280
but at the point where
11 minutes total per week

00:59:13.280 --> 00:59:14.180
is very easy for you,

00:59:14.180 --> 00:59:15.810
where is no longer representing

00:59:15.810 --> 00:59:17.210
a significant mental challenge,

00:59:17.210 --> 00:59:19.130
meaning you're not experiencing
many of these walls,

00:59:19.130 --> 00:59:21.790
you're excited to get into
the cold shower immersion,

00:59:21.790 --> 00:59:24.610
you're going through it easily,
you're cruising basically.

00:59:24.610 --> 00:59:27.850
Then I would say either
lower the temperature safely,

00:59:27.850 --> 00:59:31.430
of course, extend the
duration safely, of course,

00:59:31.430 --> 00:59:33.880
or increase the frequency
so that you're doing this

00:59:33.880 --> 00:59:36.690
perhaps every day or
maybe five days a week

00:59:36.690 --> 00:59:37.550
or three days a week.

00:59:37.550 --> 00:59:39.370
I personally get tremendous benefit

00:59:39.370 --> 00:59:42.480
from doing deliberate cold
exposure three times a week

00:59:42.480 --> 00:59:45.600
and using the walls method
that I described earlier

00:59:45.600 --> 00:59:47.480
as my gauge for how long to stay in

00:59:47.480 --> 00:59:50.360
and typically that means that
I'm staying in for anywhere

00:59:50.360 --> 00:59:53.770
from two minutes to
six minutes per session

00:59:53.770 --> 00:59:56.800
and that averages out to about

00:59:56.800 --> 00:59:59.630
11 to 15 minutes total per week.

00:59:59.630 --> 01:00:02.900
So again, I do not think that
you need to be super strict

01:00:02.900 --> 01:00:04.240
about these guidelines.

01:00:04.240 --> 01:00:07.140
It's most important when
embracing a protocol A,

01:00:07.140 --> 01:00:08.260
that you do it safely,

01:00:08.260 --> 01:00:11.380
but secondarily that
you do it consistently.

01:00:11.380 --> 01:00:13.170
So find what you can do consistently

01:00:13.170 --> 01:00:14.460
and then vary the parameters

01:00:14.460 --> 01:00:16.380
that will allow you to continue to do

01:00:16.380 --> 01:00:18.400
deliberate cold exposure consistently,

01:00:18.400 --> 01:00:20.520
regardless of whether or not
you have access to a shower

01:00:20.520 --> 01:00:22.410
or cold immersion, et cetera.

01:00:22.410 --> 01:00:24.430
So we've been talking about mental effects

01:00:24.430 --> 01:00:26.300
and the use of deliberate cold exposure

01:00:26.300 --> 01:00:28.090
for sake of building resilience,

01:00:28.090 --> 01:00:30.660
which I do believe can
be tremendously powerful.

01:00:30.660 --> 01:00:35.070
Look, it's no coincidence
that the screening

01:00:35.070 --> 01:00:37.050
and the training for Navy Seals

01:00:37.050 --> 01:00:40.110
involves a lot of exposure to cold water.

01:00:40.110 --> 01:00:41.820
One could argue that it is deliberate

01:00:41.820 --> 01:00:43.770
because they elect to go to buds,

01:00:43.770 --> 01:00:46.440
but when they get into
the cold water at buds

01:00:46.440 --> 01:00:49.420
is dictated by the
instructors and the reason

01:00:49.420 --> 01:00:52.300
they use cold water
exposure as the stressor

01:00:52.300 --> 01:00:55.230
is that it does offer considerable leeway

01:00:55.230 --> 01:00:58.050
in terms of duration and temperature,

01:00:58.050 --> 01:01:00.820
in terms of how you can
use it as a stressor

01:01:00.820 --> 01:01:04.010
whereas things like heat don't
offer much variable space

01:01:04.010 --> 01:01:05.160
as we say.

01:01:05.160 --> 01:01:08.690
There isn't a lot of room
beyond which you start injuring

01:01:08.690 --> 01:01:10.760
or even killing people by using heat.

01:01:10.760 --> 01:01:12.570
So there are a lot of forms
of stressors out there,

01:01:12.570 --> 01:01:14.540
but cold is one that we can titrate,

01:01:14.540 --> 01:01:16.470
that we can adjust in
ways that can allow us

01:01:16.470 --> 01:01:21.470
to continually build up and
or maintain mental toughness.

01:01:21.890 --> 01:01:25.630
Now, deliberate cold exposure
also has many effects

01:01:25.630 --> 01:01:28.800
on chemicals other than
norepinephrine and epinephrine,

01:01:28.800 --> 01:01:32.050
most notably the neuromodulator dopamine,

01:01:32.050 --> 01:01:34.450
which is involved in elevating our mood,

01:01:34.450 --> 01:01:38.170
making us feel energized and
enhancing our ability to focus.

01:01:38.170 --> 01:01:41.210
And that has a lot to do
with how dopamine engages us

01:01:41.210 --> 01:01:43.120
in motivated states,

01:01:43.120 --> 01:01:45.240
tends to narrow our
thinking in our behavior

01:01:45.240 --> 01:01:48.010
into a particular trench
of goal-directed behavior.

01:01:48.010 --> 01:01:50.590
If you want to learn more about dopamine,

01:01:50.590 --> 01:01:52.410
you can learn a lot about dopamine

01:01:52.410 --> 01:01:55.330
in our episode about dopamine,
it's at hubermanlab.com.

01:01:55.330 --> 01:01:58.420
You can find it, it's a
two and a half hour plus

01:01:58.420 --> 01:02:00.990
kind of deep dive into
all things dopamine,

01:02:00.990 --> 01:02:02.943
focus, motivation, et cetera.

01:02:04.860 --> 01:02:08.750
Deliberate cold exposure
has a very powerful effect

01:02:08.750 --> 01:02:11.100
on the release of dopamine
in our brain and body.

01:02:11.100 --> 01:02:14.360
And this is one of the main
reasons why people continue

01:02:14.360 --> 01:02:16.113
to do deliberate cold exposure.

01:02:17.070 --> 01:02:19.210
Basically it makes us feel good

01:02:19.210 --> 01:02:21.300
and it continues to make us feel good

01:02:21.300 --> 01:02:23.650
even after we get out
of the cold environment.

01:02:23.650 --> 01:02:25.490
In fact, some people would say

01:02:25.490 --> 01:02:27.020
they don't feel good in
the cold environment,

01:02:27.020 --> 01:02:27.930
it's all stress for them,

01:02:27.930 --> 01:02:30.180
but afterwards they feel great.

01:02:30.180 --> 01:02:32.330
One of our previous
guests, Dr. Anna Lembke,

01:02:32.330 --> 01:02:34.390
who's a medical doctor
at Stanford University

01:02:34.390 --> 01:02:37.320
School of Medicine, she's
a close colleague of mine,

01:02:37.320 --> 01:02:40.430
described the use dopamine
in her book, Dopamine Nation,

01:02:40.430 --> 01:02:42.370
an incredible book about
addiction and dopamine

01:02:42.370 --> 01:02:43.470
I should mention.

01:02:43.470 --> 01:02:48.470
And the use of dopamine
elicited by cold water exposure

01:02:49.520 --> 01:02:50.640
by one of her patients.

01:02:50.640 --> 01:02:53.860
What I'm referring to is the
fact that one of her patients

01:02:53.860 --> 01:02:57.130
helped themselves get
and stay sober off drugs

01:02:57.130 --> 01:03:01.040
by using deliberate cold
exposure to increase dopamine.

01:03:01.040 --> 01:03:03.130
So a healthier form of dopamine release

01:03:03.130 --> 01:03:07.060
than they were engaged in
prior to getting sober.

01:03:07.060 --> 01:03:09.930
Now, the basis for dopamine release

01:03:09.930 --> 01:03:11.490
in response to cold exposure

01:03:11.490 --> 01:03:13.370
is that the catecholamines,
norepinephrine,

01:03:13.370 --> 01:03:16.590
epinephrine and dopamine
tend to be co-released

01:03:16.590 --> 01:03:18.700
by the same sorts of stimuli,

01:03:18.700 --> 01:03:21.410
but most stressors and
in particular things

01:03:21.410 --> 01:03:24.850
that evoke stress or our
feelings of stress internally

01:03:24.850 --> 01:03:28.020
that we don't like do
not increase dopamine.

01:03:28.020 --> 01:03:30.560
They only increase
norepinephrine and epinephrine,

01:03:30.560 --> 01:03:32.240
but deliberate cold exposure

01:03:32.240 --> 01:03:36.050
seems to cause a dramatic
increase in dopamine.

01:03:36.050 --> 01:03:37.860
And this has actually been substantiated

01:03:37.860 --> 01:03:40.180
in a really beautiful study entitled

01:03:40.180 --> 01:03:41.730
human physiological responses

01:03:41.730 --> 01:03:44.670
to immersion into water
of different temperatures.

01:03:44.670 --> 01:03:46.990
The first author is Sramek.

01:03:46.990 --> 01:03:50.090
I'm almost certainly
pronouncing that poorly

01:03:50.090 --> 01:03:54.230
and if not incorrectly, S-R-A-M-E-K.

01:03:54.230 --> 01:03:55.820
This was published in the European Journal

01:03:55.820 --> 01:03:58.460
of Applied Physiology in the year 2000,

01:03:58.460 --> 01:04:00.700
really a beautiful
study, I love this study.

01:04:00.700 --> 01:04:03.740
They took people and they
had them sit in chairs

01:04:03.740 --> 01:04:06.750
underwater but their head was
out and so they were immersed

01:04:06.750 --> 01:04:11.750
up to the neck in either of
three different temperatures.

01:04:12.194 --> 01:04:16.260
32 degrees Celsius, which
is 89 degrees Fahrenheit,

01:04:16.260 --> 01:04:19.370
20 degrees Celsius, which
is 68 degrees Fahrenheit,

01:04:19.370 --> 01:04:23.760
or 14 degrees Celsius, which
is 57.2 degrees Fahrenheit.

01:04:23.760 --> 01:04:27.420
So not super cold, but then what they did

01:04:27.420 --> 01:04:30.010
is they measured people's core
body temperature throughout.

01:04:30.010 --> 01:04:31.697
They measured their metabolism

01:04:31.697 --> 01:04:33.750
and they looked at serum levels

01:04:33.750 --> 01:04:36.820
of things like norepinephrine,
epinephrine, dopamine,

01:04:36.820 --> 01:04:39.410
and cortisol, serum
meaning within the blood.

01:04:39.410 --> 01:04:42.283
So a really nice and quite thorough study.

01:04:43.270 --> 01:04:46.270
There were not a huge number
of subjects in the study,

01:04:46.270 --> 01:04:48.500
but nonetheless, it was
a very thorough study

01:04:48.500 --> 01:04:52.630
in terms of the number of
variables that they explored.

01:04:52.630 --> 01:04:54.050
So I just want to briefly highlight

01:04:54.050 --> 01:04:57.760
some of what they saw or what
they observed in this study.

01:04:57.760 --> 01:05:01.680
First of all, all the
groups were in the water

01:05:01.680 --> 01:05:04.520
of a given temperature for one hour,

01:05:04.520 --> 01:05:06.260
which is much longer than most

01:05:06.260 --> 01:05:08.120
of the deliberate cold exposure protocols

01:05:08.120 --> 01:05:09.500
that anyone is using at home.

01:05:09.500 --> 01:05:12.240
I mean, maybe you're taking
one hour long cold showers,

01:05:12.240 --> 01:05:14.460
maybe you're getting into
the ice bath for an hour

01:05:14.460 --> 01:05:15.540
although I don't recommend that.

01:05:15.540 --> 01:05:18.840
I think you'd probably get
badly hypothermic or maybe

01:05:18.840 --> 01:05:21.470
you're getting into a cold water immersion

01:05:21.470 --> 01:05:22.530
for some period of time,

01:05:22.530 --> 01:05:25.010
but I have a hard time imagining
that it would be an hour

01:05:25.010 --> 01:05:27.160
and I don't suggest
that if it's very cold.

01:05:27.160 --> 01:05:29.420
So this study focused on actually somewhat

01:05:29.420 --> 01:05:31.840
moderately cool temperatures,

01:05:31.840 --> 01:05:33.990
not what I think most
people would consider

01:05:33.990 --> 01:05:35.680
very, very cold temperatures,

01:05:35.680 --> 01:05:38.410
but extended the duration
for quite a while.

01:05:38.410 --> 01:05:41.240
So again, 32 degree Celsius,

01:05:41.240 --> 01:05:44.100
20 degree Celsius or 14 degree Celsius.

01:05:44.100 --> 01:05:45.730
Here's what they observed.

01:05:45.730 --> 01:05:48.780
The group that was immersed up to the neck

01:05:48.780 --> 01:05:51.960
in 32 degrees Celsius, that
is 89 degrees Fahrenheit,

01:05:51.960 --> 01:05:55.420
water did not experience
a shift in metabolism

01:05:55.420 --> 01:05:58.330
nor a significant increase in dopamine,

01:05:58.330 --> 01:06:01.070
norepinephrine or these
other catecholamines.

01:06:01.070 --> 01:06:03.670
The group that was in 20 degree Celsius,

01:06:03.670 --> 01:06:06.400
meaning 68 degree Fahrenheit,

01:06:06.400 --> 01:06:10.570
water for an hour
experienced a 93% increase

01:06:10.570 --> 01:06:13.380
in metabolic rate, which is remarkable

01:06:13.380 --> 01:06:15.960
given that the water wasn't that cold

01:06:15.960 --> 01:06:18.540
and yet an hour is a pretty
long time to be in there.

01:06:18.540 --> 01:06:21.340
And again, it speaks
to the dramatic effect

01:06:21.340 --> 01:06:24.650
of heat transfer that water
has, which I mentioned earlier,

01:06:24.650 --> 01:06:27.390
as opposed to being out
in the air at 68 degrees,

01:06:27.390 --> 01:06:29.610
it would certainly not cause that increase

01:06:29.610 --> 01:06:30.583
in metabolic rate.

01:06:31.570 --> 01:06:35.020
The group that was at 14 degrees Celsius,

01:06:35.020 --> 01:06:37.840
meaning 57.2 degrees Fahrenheit,

01:06:37.840 --> 01:06:42.830
water for an hour experienced
a 350% increase in metabolism.

01:06:42.830 --> 01:06:45.290
So huge increases in metabolism.

01:06:45.290 --> 01:06:48.720
Now the most interesting data to me,

01:06:48.720 --> 01:06:50.560
at least in terms of mental effects

01:06:50.560 --> 01:06:52.450
of deliberate cold exposure,

01:06:52.450 --> 01:06:57.450
were that the plasma or serum
levels of norepinephrine

01:06:58.200 --> 01:07:01.660
in the blood increased 530%.

01:07:01.660 --> 01:07:04.380
These are huge increases in norepinephrine

01:07:04.380 --> 01:07:07.270
so it suggests that this
is a stressful stimulus

01:07:07.270 --> 01:07:10.070
at least neurochemically
speaking, stressful,

01:07:10.070 --> 01:07:12.350
despite the fact that
it's not super super cold,

01:07:12.350 --> 01:07:14.870
although 57.2 degrees Fahrenheit,

01:07:14.870 --> 01:07:19.870
14 degrees Celsius it's
not a warm environment,

01:07:20.410 --> 01:07:22.610
but it's not a ultra,
ultra cold environment,

01:07:22.610 --> 01:07:24.960
but an hour is a very
long time to be in there.

01:07:26.600 --> 01:07:31.600
The subjects also
experienced a 250% increase

01:07:31.710 --> 01:07:34.100
in dopamine concentrations,

01:07:34.100 --> 01:07:38.130
which while not 530% as
it was with norepinephrine

01:07:38.130 --> 01:07:40.670
is still a very large increase

01:07:40.670 --> 01:07:42.550
in baseline levels of dopamine.

01:07:42.550 --> 01:07:45.460
And what was interesting is
that those increases in dopamine

01:07:45.460 --> 01:07:49.250
persisted for a very long
period of time afterwards,

01:07:49.250 --> 01:07:51.530
even out to two hours.

01:07:51.530 --> 01:07:54.440
And they stopped the
study after 120 minutes

01:07:54.440 --> 01:07:58.230
of getting out of the
cold, but nonetheless,

01:07:58.230 --> 01:08:01.830
these increases in norepinephrine
are huge and long lasting

01:08:01.830 --> 01:08:03.370
and these increases in dopamine

01:08:03.370 --> 01:08:05.840
are very large and long lasting.

01:08:05.840 --> 01:08:08.910
And I do believe that these
documented effects in humans

01:08:08.910 --> 01:08:13.060
explain much of the enhancement
of attention and of feelings

01:08:13.060 --> 01:08:16.430
of wellbeing and mood that
people typically experience

01:08:16.430 --> 01:08:19.120
after doing deliberate cold exposure.

01:08:19.120 --> 01:08:21.780
And the reason I say that is that

01:08:21.780 --> 01:08:23.630
if you were to go back to the episode

01:08:23.630 --> 01:08:24.770
that I did on dopamine,

01:08:24.770 --> 01:08:26.220
or you were to go back to the episode

01:08:26.220 --> 01:08:29.770
that I did with Dr. Anna Lembke
on addiction and dopamine,

01:08:29.770 --> 01:08:32.880
what you would find is
that increases in dopamine

01:08:32.880 --> 01:08:35.880
of the sort evoked by
deliberate cold exposure

01:08:35.880 --> 01:08:38.140
are actually very similar
to the kinds of increases

01:08:38.140 --> 01:08:41.440
in dopamine that are elicited
by things like nicotine

01:08:41.440 --> 01:08:43.210
or from other behaviors

01:08:43.210 --> 01:08:45.960
that are known to be
addictive and bad for us

01:08:45.960 --> 01:08:48.090
because they lead to
other effects on the brain

01:08:48.090 --> 01:08:50.290
and body that we simply don't want.

01:08:50.290 --> 01:08:53.860
And yet deliberate cold
exposure provided is done safely

01:08:53.860 --> 01:08:57.470
can create similar if not
greater increases in dopamine

01:08:58.610 --> 01:09:01.890
that are not just fleeting,
that don't just occur during,

01:09:01.890 --> 01:09:05.220
say the consumption of some
deleterious drug or activity,

01:09:05.220 --> 01:09:07.820
but that are very long lasting
and that can be leveraged

01:09:07.820 --> 01:09:11.860
toward activities other than
deliberate cold exposure.

01:09:11.860 --> 01:09:13.140
So I want to emphasize this.

01:09:13.140 --> 01:09:14.150
I'm not suggesting

01:09:14.150 --> 01:09:16.840
that people do deliberate cold
exposure for an hour a day.

01:09:16.840 --> 01:09:20.330
And unfortunately there
are not many studies yet

01:09:20.330 --> 01:09:23.830
exploring how shorter colder temperature

01:09:23.830 --> 01:09:27.110
environment exposure, say
one minute or three minutes

01:09:27.110 --> 01:09:32.110
or six minutes at 55
degrees or at 50 degrees,

01:09:32.170 --> 01:09:34.700
whether or not that leads to similar,

01:09:34.700 --> 01:09:38.820
greater or reduced levels of
dopamine in the brain and body.

01:09:38.820 --> 01:09:42.620
And yet almost everybody who
does deliberate cold exposure

01:09:42.620 --> 01:09:44.520
will say yeah, it was stressful.

01:09:44.520 --> 01:09:47.400
I didn't enjoy it, or I
eventually grew to like it,

01:09:47.400 --> 01:09:49.670
but that I always feel better afterwards

01:09:49.670 --> 01:09:52.710
and then that feeling last
a very long period of time.

01:09:52.710 --> 01:09:54.620
And I think it's almost certain

01:09:54.620 --> 01:09:56.860
that those experiences that people report

01:09:56.860 --> 01:09:58.850
relate to these increases in dopamine

01:09:58.850 --> 01:10:02.050
and in concert with the
increases in norepinephrine

01:10:02.050 --> 01:10:04.530
also explain the other effect
that's commonly reported,

01:10:04.530 --> 01:10:06.980
which is an enhancement in mental acuity

01:10:06.980 --> 01:10:09.190
and the ability to focus.

01:10:09.190 --> 01:10:11.770
Now, here we can extrapolate to the study

01:10:11.770 --> 01:10:14.900
that I discussed at the
early part of the episode,

01:10:14.900 --> 01:10:16.370
where I was talking about the use of

01:10:16.370 --> 01:10:18.150
short 15 minute exercise,

01:10:18.150 --> 01:10:19.800
kind of moderate intensity exercise

01:10:19.800 --> 01:10:22.780
and how that was shown to
increase levels of energy

01:10:22.780 --> 01:10:23.800
and mental acuity

01:10:23.800 --> 01:10:26.500
in these working memory,
visual attention tasks.

01:10:26.500 --> 01:10:29.350
And there, again, we
have to assume somewhat

01:10:29.350 --> 01:10:31.490
because they weren't doing
neurochemical measurements,

01:10:31.490 --> 01:10:35.270
but we can reasonably assume
that those improvements

01:10:35.270 --> 01:10:37.520
in cognitive performance were due

01:10:37.520 --> 01:10:39.760
at least in part to the
increase in catecholamines

01:10:39.760 --> 01:10:43.750
known to a company, moderate
intensity zone two cardio.

01:10:43.750 --> 01:10:45.830
So what you're starting
to see here is a theme.

01:10:45.830 --> 01:10:49.280
The theme is that virtually any stimulus

01:10:49.280 --> 01:10:51.130
that delivers more norepinephrine,

01:10:51.130 --> 01:10:53.210
epinephrine and dopamine to our system

01:10:53.210 --> 01:10:55.680
will sharpen our mental
acuity and elevate our mood

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:58.310
and will do so for some period of time.

01:10:58.310 --> 01:11:00.520
Deliberate cold exposure, it turns out,

01:11:00.520 --> 01:11:03.520
is a very potent way to
increase these catecholamines,

01:11:03.520 --> 01:11:06.740
this category of chemicals
and thereby to improve mood,

01:11:06.740 --> 01:11:09.190
mental acuity and levels of alertness.

01:11:09.190 --> 01:11:13.070
And as we'll next see, it
not only has that effect,

01:11:13.070 --> 01:11:15.170
which can be very
beneficial for many people

01:11:15.170 --> 01:11:16.940
in a bunch of different circumstances,

01:11:16.940 --> 01:11:21.000
but it also has the positive
effects that many people seek

01:11:21.000 --> 01:11:24.350
in terms of metabolism, in
lowering inflammation in the body

01:11:24.350 --> 01:11:26.620
and other physiological effects as well.

01:11:26.620 --> 01:11:27.453
And forgive me,

01:11:27.453 --> 01:11:30.120
I was almost ready to move on to effects

01:11:30.120 --> 01:11:32.780
of deliberate cold exposure on metabolism

01:11:32.780 --> 01:11:34.330
and inflammation and so forth,

01:11:34.330 --> 01:11:36.690
but I neglected to point
out one of the other

01:11:36.690 --> 01:11:39.950
very interesting aspects of the study

01:11:39.950 --> 01:11:41.720
showing deliberate cold exposure

01:11:41.720 --> 01:11:44.210
can increase in
norepinephrine and dopamine,

01:11:44.210 --> 01:11:48.180
which is that they observed
no significant increases

01:11:48.180 --> 01:11:50.300
in the stress hormone cortisol

01:11:50.300 --> 01:11:53.670
and that is both surprising,
interesting, and important

01:11:53.670 --> 01:11:57.500
because what it means is
that the quality of stress

01:11:57.500 --> 01:12:00.780
that deliberate cold exposure
is creating in the body

01:12:00.780 --> 01:12:04.560
is likely to be one of
what we call use stress.

01:12:04.560 --> 01:12:07.420
Hans Selye, the great physiologist,

01:12:07.420 --> 01:12:10.830
won a Nobel Prize for
distinguishing between distress,

01:12:10.830 --> 01:12:13.230
which is stress in the brain and body

01:12:13.230 --> 01:12:16.600
that causes the release
of things like cortisol

01:12:16.600 --> 01:12:18.010
along with the other catecholamines,

01:12:18.010 --> 01:12:20.780
and that we experience as
negative happening to us

01:12:20.780 --> 01:12:22.910
and can lead to negative health outcomes.

01:12:22.910 --> 01:12:25.440
And he distinguished that from eustress,

01:12:25.440 --> 01:12:27.920
which was stress that we now understand

01:12:27.920 --> 01:12:30.590
is associated with increases
in things like norepinephrine

01:12:30.590 --> 01:12:34.010
and dopamine, but no
increases or minimal increases

01:12:34.010 --> 01:12:37.600
in cortisol, and that can lead
to positive health outcomes.

01:12:37.600 --> 01:12:41.330
So it appears that deliberate
cold exposure can create

01:12:41.330 --> 01:12:44.830
what we call or what Hans
Selye called eustress.

01:12:44.830 --> 01:12:46.990
In other words, it can create
a condition in the brain

01:12:46.990 --> 01:12:49.900
and body in which we
are stressing ourselves,

01:12:49.900 --> 01:12:51.240
we are training up resilience,

01:12:51.240 --> 01:12:54.180
and yet we are creating
a neurochemical milieu

01:12:54.180 --> 01:12:56.420
that actually has many health benefits.

01:12:56.420 --> 01:12:57.630
Now I'd like to shift our attention

01:12:57.630 --> 01:13:00.830
to the effects of deliberate
cold exposure on metabolism.

01:13:00.830 --> 01:13:03.350
And I'd like to start by detailing a study

01:13:03.350 --> 01:13:05.180
that was performed on humans

01:13:05.180 --> 01:13:07.790
and published just at
the end of last year.

01:13:07.790 --> 01:13:09.290
The title of the study is altered

01:13:09.290 --> 01:13:10.890
brown fat thermoregulation,

01:13:10.890 --> 01:13:13.670
and enhanced cold induced thermogenesis

01:13:13.670 --> 01:13:16.020
in young, healthy winter swimming men.

01:13:16.020 --> 01:13:18.370
And I should point out
that while the study

01:13:18.370 --> 01:13:20.630
was only performed on male subjects,

01:13:20.630 --> 01:13:22.880
there's no reason to
think that the effects

01:13:22.880 --> 01:13:25.190
that they discovered
would only pertain to men.

01:13:25.190 --> 01:13:27.160
I would hope that they would
also do a study on women

01:13:27.160 --> 01:13:28.840
at some point in the future,

01:13:28.840 --> 01:13:30.900
but the effects that they describe

01:13:30.900 --> 01:13:34.740
are very basic core
physiological processes.

01:13:34.740 --> 01:13:37.260
What they did is they looked
at at deliberate cold exposure

01:13:37.260 --> 01:13:38.760
in this group of young men,

01:13:38.760 --> 01:13:43.000
and they used that 11
minute threshold per week.

01:13:43.000 --> 01:13:46.820
So in other words, they had
them get into cold water

01:13:46.820 --> 01:13:50.200
for approximately 11 minutes per week.

01:13:50.200 --> 01:13:53.640
And again, that's 11
minutes total per week.

01:13:53.640 --> 01:13:56.030
They divided that into two sessions

01:13:56.030 --> 01:13:58.800
although in speaking with the
first author of this study,

01:13:58.800 --> 01:13:59.990
Dr. Susanna Søberg,

01:13:59.990 --> 01:14:02.350
I learned that it
probably is not important

01:14:02.350 --> 01:14:03.540
that it be two sessions,

01:14:03.540 --> 01:14:06.310
it could be three or even four sessions,

01:14:06.310 --> 01:14:08.963
as long as it reaches
that 11 minute threshold.

01:14:09.910 --> 01:14:11.210
What they discovered was

01:14:11.210 --> 01:14:14.860
that by going into
these cold environments,

01:14:14.860 --> 01:14:18.010
in this case cold water
immersion up to the neck,

01:14:18.010 --> 01:14:21.290
for 11 minutes total per week,

01:14:21.290 --> 01:14:23.830
that these men experienced increases

01:14:23.830 --> 01:14:26.700
in so-called brown fat thermogenesis,

01:14:26.700 --> 01:14:28.750
I'll talk more about
what that is in a moment,

01:14:28.750 --> 01:14:30.840
and increases in core body temperature

01:14:30.840 --> 01:14:34.950
that translate to increases
in core body metabolism.

01:14:34.950 --> 01:14:37.860
Now, the overall increases
in core body metabolism

01:14:37.860 --> 01:14:42.860
that they experienced
were not extremely large.

01:14:42.890 --> 01:14:44.640
They were statistically significant,

01:14:44.640 --> 01:14:45.990
but they weren't extremely large.

01:14:45.990 --> 01:14:50.430
However, the changes in brown fat stores

01:14:50.430 --> 01:14:53.150
are perhaps what's most
interesting about this study

01:14:53.150 --> 01:14:54.470
and I'll tell you why.

01:14:54.470 --> 01:14:57.620
The metabolic increases of
deliberate cold exposure

01:14:57.620 --> 01:15:00.330
are both acute meaning
happening in the short term.

01:15:00.330 --> 01:15:02.950
When you get into the cold
and immediately after,

01:15:02.950 --> 01:15:06.470
one does experience an
increase in core metabolism,

01:15:06.470 --> 01:15:08.420
you burn some calories, in other words,

01:15:09.730 --> 01:15:14.560
and while those might not be
very significant increases,

01:15:14.560 --> 01:15:17.770
or I should say they can be
statistically significant,

01:15:17.770 --> 01:15:20.740
but they are not enormously large numbers

01:15:20.740 --> 01:15:22.670
of calories burned,

01:15:22.670 --> 01:15:27.240
the longer lasting effects
of deliberate cold exposure

01:15:27.240 --> 01:15:30.710
on metabolism seem to take
place by changes that occur

01:15:30.710 --> 01:15:34.130
in the types of fat that
we store in our body

01:15:34.130 --> 01:15:36.700
and the way that that fat
impacts our metabolism

01:15:36.700 --> 01:15:40.173
at other times throughout
the 24 hour cycle.

01:15:41.820 --> 01:15:44.690
This actually has a
somewhat anecdotal basis,

01:15:44.690 --> 01:15:46.930
in particular in Scandinavia.

01:15:46.930 --> 01:15:49.470
I don't speak Swedish nor I speak Danish,

01:15:49.470 --> 01:15:51.200
nor do I speak Norwegian,

01:15:51.200 --> 01:15:53.000
but I do have Danish relatives

01:15:53.000 --> 01:15:55.440
and they were able to help me decipher

01:15:55.440 --> 01:15:58.060
a common Swedish saying,

01:15:58.060 --> 01:16:01.160
which essentially
translates to the fact that

01:16:01.160 --> 01:16:04.343
in preparation for the summer, they say,

01:16:05.360 --> 01:16:09.030
one should expose themselves
to warm environments

01:16:09.030 --> 01:16:12.280
so that one is comfortable
in warm environments

01:16:12.280 --> 01:16:13.170
in the summer.

01:16:13.170 --> 01:16:16.500
That's one half of this
traditional Swedish

01:16:16.500 --> 01:16:17.970
and also Danish saying.

01:16:17.970 --> 01:16:22.420
The other half of this
traditional Danish-Swedish saying

01:16:22.420 --> 01:16:24.830
is that in preparation for winter,

01:16:24.830 --> 01:16:28.800
in order to not feel too
cold in cold environments,

01:16:28.800 --> 01:16:31.330
one should prepare for those in the fall

01:16:31.330 --> 01:16:34.270
by not wearing a jacket
and exposing one's self

01:16:34.270 --> 01:16:36.240
to cold environments.

01:16:36.240 --> 01:16:40.170
Now, of course, this is just
anecdotal cultural lore,

01:16:40.170 --> 01:16:42.250
but it actually has a physiological basis,

01:16:42.250 --> 01:16:46.430
which is by exposing
oneself to cold environments

01:16:46.430 --> 01:16:50.080
on a repeated basis in
anticipation of exposure

01:16:50.080 --> 01:16:52.140
to more extreme cold environments,

01:16:52.140 --> 01:16:54.000
one can feel more comfortable

01:16:54.000 --> 01:16:56.040
in those extreme cold environments.

01:16:56.040 --> 01:16:58.210
And that's exactly what they observed

01:16:58.210 --> 01:17:00.430
in this study by Søberg et al.

01:17:00.430 --> 01:17:03.980
The men felt more
comfortable in extreme cold

01:17:03.980 --> 01:17:07.410
if they had trained through
deliberate cold exposure,

01:17:07.410 --> 01:17:09.920
which might not seem surprising at all,

01:17:09.920 --> 01:17:12.310
but based on what we talked about earlier,

01:17:12.310 --> 01:17:16.070
whereby deliberate cold
exposure evokes this discomfort

01:17:16.070 --> 01:17:18.670
and this experience of
norepinephrine release,

01:17:18.670 --> 01:17:21.990
at least in the short term,
then you would say, well,

01:17:21.990 --> 01:17:24.110
shouldn't that deliberate
cold exposure also

01:17:24.110 --> 01:17:25.280
make them feel uncomfortable

01:17:25.280 --> 01:17:26.720
like they really want to get out?

01:17:26.720 --> 01:17:28.280
Well, that is true

01:17:28.280 --> 01:17:31.460
in the beginning of a deliberate
cold exposure protocol,

01:17:31.460 --> 01:17:33.510
meaning in the first week
or in the second week

01:17:33.510 --> 01:17:35.060
or the third week, but what one finds

01:17:35.060 --> 01:17:36.870
and what you will find if you do

01:17:36.870 --> 01:17:41.190
deliberate cold exposure
consistently is that you will then

01:17:41.190 --> 01:17:44.600
become more comfortable
at cold temperatures

01:17:44.600 --> 01:17:46.750
away from the deliberate cold exposure.

01:17:46.750 --> 01:17:49.210
So whereas you might have
previously been the person

01:17:49.210 --> 01:17:51.210
who is always cold in the
room with air conditioning,

01:17:51.210 --> 01:17:54.030
or always seeking a sweater,
always wanting to bundle up,

01:17:54.030 --> 01:17:56.060
you will be more comfortable
in those cold environments.

01:17:56.060 --> 01:18:00.020
And the reason for that
is well substantiated

01:18:00.020 --> 01:18:03.290
from this study and from animal studies

01:18:03.290 --> 01:18:06.260
whereby deliberate cold exposure

01:18:06.260 --> 01:18:09.560
converts one particular kind of fat cell,

01:18:09.560 --> 01:18:11.120
the white fat cell,

01:18:11.120 --> 01:18:15.320
which is a very low metabolic output cell.

01:18:15.320 --> 01:18:17.450
It's basically a storage site for energy

01:18:17.450 --> 01:18:21.290
in the body fat cells to a
different type of fat cell,

01:18:21.290 --> 01:18:24.440
which is the beige fat cell called beige

01:18:24.440 --> 01:18:27.030
because it's actually
beige or slightly brown

01:18:27.030 --> 01:18:30.470
under the microscope, or
even to brown fat cells,

01:18:30.470 --> 01:18:32.950
which are very dark under
the microscope and dark

01:18:32.950 --> 01:18:35.090
because they contain mitochondria

01:18:35.960 --> 01:18:40.530
and are very metabolically
and thermogenetically active.

01:18:40.530 --> 01:18:43.890
In other words, white fat
doesn't burn many calories.

01:18:43.890 --> 01:18:45.560
It's basically a storage site.

01:18:45.560 --> 01:18:47.650
It's a bank account for energy.

01:18:47.650 --> 01:18:50.020
It's filled with lipids,

01:18:50.020 --> 01:18:53.530
and those lipids can be used
if the body needs energy

01:18:53.530 --> 01:18:55.720
and if it goes into a caloric deficit.

01:18:55.720 --> 01:18:59.700
Beige fat and brown fat
acts as sort of a furnace

01:18:59.700 --> 01:19:03.430
or the sort of fat that
you would find in a kindle,

01:19:03.430 --> 01:19:06.130
a fuel that can increase
core body temperature.

01:19:06.130 --> 01:19:09.550
So beige fat and brown fat is very good

01:19:09.550 --> 01:19:13.360
at raising our metabolism
and helps burn white fat.

01:19:13.360 --> 01:19:14.193
Now of course,

01:19:14.193 --> 01:19:18.380
it does that only in the
context of a caloric deficit,

01:19:18.380 --> 01:19:22.010
but it can actually help
create that caloric deficit.

01:19:22.010 --> 01:19:24.610
Having more beige fat and brown fat

01:19:24.610 --> 01:19:27.880
can increase your overall core
metabolism, in other words,

01:19:27.880 --> 01:19:30.330
the number of calories
that you burn per day,

01:19:30.330 --> 01:19:31.730
and therefore the number of calories

01:19:31.730 --> 01:19:34.733
that you need to either
maintain or to lose weight.

01:19:35.860 --> 01:19:39.180
The simple translation of
this is that getting into

01:19:39.180 --> 01:19:43.080
cold water for a total of
11 minutes, perhaps more,

01:19:43.080 --> 01:19:45.020
but at least 11 minutes per week

01:19:45.020 --> 01:19:47.750
divided into two or four sessions

01:19:47.750 --> 01:19:50.550
can increase your core metabolism in part

01:19:50.550 --> 01:19:54.240
by increasing your beige
and brown fat stores.

01:19:54.240 --> 01:19:57.440
And we know how that works
at least in animal models

01:19:57.440 --> 01:19:59.310
and there's now reason to suspect

01:19:59.310 --> 01:20:02.350
that the exact same mechanisms
are occurring in humans.

01:20:02.350 --> 01:20:05.920
The primary way in which
deliberate cold exposure converts

01:20:05.920 --> 01:20:08.540
white fat cells into
these more metabolically

01:20:08.540 --> 01:20:11.730
thermogenetically active
metabolism increasing

01:20:11.730 --> 01:20:16.730
beige and brown fat cells
is because norepinephrine

01:20:18.280 --> 01:20:22.290
released when we get into
the cold binds to receptors

01:20:22.290 --> 01:20:25.870
on the surface of white
fat cells and activates

01:20:25.870 --> 01:20:30.040
downstream pathways such as UCP1,

01:20:30.040 --> 01:20:32.670
so this is an uncoupling protein one,

01:20:32.670 --> 01:20:35.550
that acts on the mitochondrial
metabolism of cells

01:20:35.550 --> 01:20:39.110
and increases the mitochondrial
output of those cells

01:20:39.110 --> 01:20:41.380
and the mitochondrial
density of those cells.

01:20:41.380 --> 01:20:42.260
In other words,

01:20:42.260 --> 01:20:46.180
it takes a cell that has
a kind of a weak engine

01:20:46.180 --> 01:20:48.910
or no engine for generating energy.

01:20:48.910 --> 01:20:51.040
Although every cell has some mitochondria,

01:20:51.040 --> 01:20:53.650
it takes cells that have
very few mitochondria

01:20:53.650 --> 01:20:56.360
and increases the engine size.

01:20:56.360 --> 01:21:00.010
It kind of stokes the furnace
of those particular cells

01:21:00.010 --> 01:21:03.510
and actually can change gene
expression in those cells.

01:21:03.510 --> 01:21:04.680
So that's what's really interesting.

01:21:04.680 --> 01:21:08.460
Deliberate cold exposure causes
increases in norepinephrine.

01:21:08.460 --> 01:21:12.720
which bind to receptors on the
surfaces of white fat cells,

01:21:12.720 --> 01:21:16.340
which triggers the release
of things like UCP1.

01:21:16.340 --> 01:21:19.600
It also causes the release
of things like P-part gamma,

01:21:19.600 --> 01:21:22.100
and co-factor PGC1.

01:21:22.100 --> 01:21:23.570
I'm going to refer you to a review

01:21:23.570 --> 01:21:25.480
if you want to learn more about these.

01:21:25.480 --> 01:21:26.870
For those of you that
don't want to learn more,

01:21:26.870 --> 01:21:29.640
all you need to know is that
the downstream of all that

01:21:29.640 --> 01:21:32.440
are increases in
mitochondria and metabolism

01:21:32.440 --> 01:21:36.290
and actual genetic changes
in the white fat cells

01:21:36.290 --> 01:21:39.520
that convert them into
beige and brown fat cells.

01:21:39.520 --> 01:21:41.430
This is especially important for adults

01:21:41.430 --> 01:21:46.430
because babies and young
children actually don't have

01:21:46.690 --> 01:21:47.780
the ability to shiver

01:21:47.780 --> 01:21:51.570
or they have a less
robust capacity to shiver.

01:21:51.570 --> 01:21:53.460
Very small babies really can't shiver

01:21:53.460 --> 01:21:56.410
so they have a lot of brown
fat in order to keep them warm.

01:21:57.480 --> 01:22:00.380
Young children eventually
develop the ability to shiver

01:22:01.230 --> 01:22:02.950
and maintain these brown fat stores,

01:22:02.950 --> 01:22:06.020
mainly around the clavicles,
the heart, the upper spine,

01:22:06.020 --> 01:22:07.690
and in the upper back.

01:22:07.690 --> 01:22:10.880
And it's no coincidence that
kids can often run around

01:22:10.880 --> 01:22:12.580
with a minimal of clothing
and be comfortable

01:22:12.580 --> 01:22:15.273
in environments that
adults would be cold in.

01:22:16.970 --> 01:22:21.050
As life goes on, we tend to
lose beige and brown fat,

01:22:21.050 --> 01:22:23.720
but this mechanism that I'm referring to

01:22:23.720 --> 01:22:25.700
points to the plasticity of white fat,

01:22:25.700 --> 01:22:27.930
meaning the ability for
white fat to actually convert

01:22:27.930 --> 01:22:31.000
its identity into this
metabolically thermogenetically

01:22:31.000 --> 01:22:33.950
enhancing form of beige and brown fat.

01:22:33.950 --> 01:22:36.560
So deliberate cold
exposure is a terrific way

01:22:36.560 --> 01:22:38.470
to increase your core metabolism

01:22:38.470 --> 01:22:40.300
and oftentimes critics will say, well,

01:22:40.300 --> 01:22:42.580
the increase in metabolism
isn't that significant

01:22:42.580 --> 01:22:44.470
although I do want to point out again,

01:22:44.470 --> 01:22:49.470
the 93% and 350% increases in metabolism

01:22:49.560 --> 01:22:51.080
from that previous study.

01:22:51.080 --> 01:22:53.770
But critics then will say, well,

01:22:53.770 --> 01:22:58.770
that doesn't really translate
to that big of a caloric burn

01:22:59.040 --> 01:23:00.980
during the deliberate cold exposure.

01:23:00.980 --> 01:23:02.700
But to that, you should say, ah,

01:23:02.700 --> 01:23:04.870
but that's only limiting your optics

01:23:04.870 --> 01:23:06.330
to just a portion of the effects

01:23:06.330 --> 01:23:09.130
of deliberate cold exposure
because deliberate cold exposure

01:23:09.130 --> 01:23:12.860
can also convert white fat
to beige fat and brown fat

01:23:12.860 --> 01:23:17.040
and lead to these more lasting
increases in metabolism.

01:23:17.040 --> 01:23:19.737
So for any of you interested
in increasing your metabolism

01:23:19.737 --> 01:23:22.520
and or being comfortable
in cold environments

01:23:22.520 --> 01:23:25.250
and or being comfortable in terms of

01:23:25.250 --> 01:23:27.550
being able to combat stress mentally,

01:23:27.550 --> 01:23:31.000
deliberate cold exposure, I
do believe is a powerful tool.

01:23:31.000 --> 01:23:34.390
And there is simply no reason
why you couldn't and shouldn't

01:23:34.390 --> 01:23:36.990
use the same protocols
that I described earlier

01:23:36.990 --> 01:23:39.970
for building resilience
to increase metabolism.

01:23:39.970 --> 01:23:43.450
Provided you're hitting that
11 minute per week threshold,

01:23:43.450 --> 01:23:47.170
you ought to be stimulating
both mechanism increases

01:23:47.170 --> 01:23:51.850
in resilience and increases
in core metabolism.

01:23:51.850 --> 01:23:52.683
As I mentioned earlier,

01:23:52.683 --> 01:23:55.580
most of the detailed studies
on the conversion of white fat

01:23:55.580 --> 01:23:57.860
to beige fat and brown fat
through the use of cold

01:23:57.860 --> 01:23:59.560
have been done in animal models,

01:23:59.560 --> 01:24:01.500
but the human data are starting to emerge.

01:24:01.500 --> 01:24:04.260
And if you'd like to do the
deep dive into these mechanisms,

01:24:04.260 --> 01:24:07.240
things like UCP1, P-part gamma, et cetera,

01:24:07.240 --> 01:24:09.680
there's a beautiful review
that was published recently

01:24:09.680 --> 01:24:10.620
in the journal cell,

01:24:10.620 --> 01:24:12.160
which is one of the three apex journals,

01:24:12.160 --> 01:24:13.710
nature, science, cell.

01:24:13.710 --> 01:24:17.220
And the title of that paper
is adipose tissue plasticity

01:24:17.220 --> 01:24:18.170
in health and disease.

01:24:18.170 --> 01:24:19.460
I love this review.

01:24:19.460 --> 01:24:22.820
It has beautiful diagrams
detailing all of the pathways

01:24:22.820 --> 01:24:26.500
from cold to norepinephrine through UCP1

01:24:26.500 --> 01:24:28.140
downstream of things like cyclic AMP.

01:24:28.140 --> 01:24:30.280
If none of those names
mean anything to you,

01:24:30.280 --> 01:24:31.130
don't worry about it.

01:24:31.130 --> 01:24:32.870
You certainly don't need
to know these mechanisms

01:24:32.870 --> 01:24:35.810
to benefit from deliberate
cold exposure protocols.

01:24:35.810 --> 01:24:38.190
If those names do mean something to you,

01:24:38.190 --> 01:24:41.200
or you are interested in
exploring the downstream effects

01:24:41.200 --> 01:24:43.630
of deliberate cold
exposure and something else

01:24:43.630 --> 01:24:45.620
that's really nice that's
covered in this paper

01:24:45.620 --> 01:24:48.100
is how deliberate cold exposure interacts

01:24:48.100 --> 01:24:51.770
with fasted states and fed states.

01:24:51.770 --> 01:24:54.330
I think you'll also find
this review very interesting.

01:24:54.330 --> 01:24:56.370
I don't want to go too
deeply into fasted states

01:24:56.370 --> 01:24:58.140
and fed states right now,

01:24:58.140 --> 01:25:00.490
suffice to say that when we are fasted,

01:25:00.490 --> 01:25:03.070
meaning when we have an eaten
for some period of time,

01:25:03.070 --> 01:25:06.080
our baseline levels of norepinephrine

01:25:06.080 --> 01:25:09.000
and epinephrine are already elevated.

01:25:09.000 --> 01:25:11.390
And so cold exposure at those times

01:25:11.390 --> 01:25:14.900
ought to have an even
greater effect on metabolism

01:25:14.900 --> 01:25:16.510
and resilience and so on.

01:25:16.510 --> 01:25:20.440
So for you fasters or your
intermittent fasters out there,

01:25:20.440 --> 01:25:21.890
if you really want to get fancy,

01:25:21.890 --> 01:25:24.130
you can do your deliberate cold exposure

01:25:24.130 --> 01:25:25.630
when you are fasted.

01:25:25.630 --> 01:25:27.150
I certainly wouldn't recommend doing it

01:25:27.150 --> 01:25:29.500
with a very full stomach in any case.

01:25:29.500 --> 01:25:33.010
And as I mentioned before on this podcast,

01:25:33.010 --> 01:25:36.240
intermittent fasting is, but one way,

01:25:36.240 --> 01:25:37.580
and certainly there are other ways

01:25:37.580 --> 01:25:40.720
to limit total caloric intake
for sake of maintaining

01:25:40.720 --> 01:25:43.150
or losing weight if that's your goal.

01:25:43.150 --> 01:25:45.510
I know many people are using and benefit

01:25:45.510 --> 01:25:47.440
from intermittent fasting, however,

01:25:47.440 --> 01:25:49.700
and so it certainly can be combined

01:25:49.700 --> 01:25:51.540
with deliberate cold exposures in order

01:25:51.540 --> 01:25:53.040
to get even greater increases

01:25:53.040 --> 01:25:54.890
in norepinephrine and epinephrine.

01:25:54.890 --> 01:25:57.140
So for those of you that
are primarily interested

01:25:57.140 --> 01:25:58.610
in using deliberate cold exposure

01:25:58.610 --> 01:26:02.260
to increase dopamine levels
in your brain and body,

01:26:02.260 --> 01:26:04.550
you can also do a combined protocol

01:26:05.520 --> 01:26:09.170
whereby you ingest
caffeine 60 to 120 minutes

01:26:09.170 --> 01:26:11.770
before the deliberate cold exposure.

01:26:11.770 --> 01:26:14.700
This is based on a study
that I've talked about before

01:26:14.700 --> 01:26:18.040
entitled caffeine increases
striatal dopamine D-2,

01:26:18.040 --> 01:26:20.440
D-3 receptor availability
in the human brain.

01:26:20.440 --> 01:26:23.790
And as the title suggests,
this study was done on humans,

01:26:23.790 --> 01:26:27.580
looking at the density and or efficacy

01:26:27.580 --> 01:26:29.050
of these dopamine receptors

01:26:29.050 --> 01:26:30.830
in an area of the brain
called the striatum,

01:26:30.830 --> 01:26:32.540
which is involved in planning in action

01:26:32.540 --> 01:26:34.350
and also suppressing planning in action.

01:26:34.350 --> 01:26:37.630
It's involved very closely
with whether or not

01:26:37.630 --> 01:26:41.120
we can engage in behavior
and withhold behavior,

01:26:41.120 --> 01:26:44.270
the so-called go and no
go ways in the brain.

01:26:44.270 --> 01:26:45.627
Dopamine plays a critical role in that

01:26:45.627 --> 01:26:48.373
and many other things
as well as you now know.

01:26:49.270 --> 01:26:52.670
So why would you want to ingest
caffeine 60 to 120 minutes

01:26:52.670 --> 01:26:55.050
before deliberate cold exposure?

01:26:55.050 --> 01:26:57.720
Well, as I talked about earlier,

01:26:57.720 --> 01:27:00.300
dopamine can increase quite substantially

01:27:00.300 --> 01:27:02.210
in response to deliberate cold exposure,

01:27:02.210 --> 01:27:04.050
but dopamine on its own
doesn't do anything,

01:27:04.050 --> 01:27:06.020
it has to bind to receptors

01:27:06.020 --> 01:27:08.720
and this paper shows quite definitively

01:27:08.720 --> 01:27:10.370
that ingesting caffeine in this case,

01:27:10.370 --> 01:27:12.400
it was 300 milligram dose of caffeine,

01:27:12.400 --> 01:27:14.200
which is about the dose of caffeine

01:27:14.200 --> 01:27:15.480
in two or three cups of coffee,

01:27:15.480 --> 01:27:17.600
it depends on the strength
of the coffee, of course,

01:27:17.600 --> 01:27:19.900
but it's not an outrageous
amount of caffeine,

01:27:21.150 --> 01:27:23.850
that increases the density and or efficacy

01:27:23.850 --> 01:27:24.683
of these receptors,

01:27:24.683 --> 01:27:25.860
which you would allow that dopamine

01:27:25.860 --> 01:27:28.860
to have its greatest effect.

01:27:28.860 --> 01:27:31.160
And for those of you that want
to get really, really fancy,

01:27:31.160 --> 01:27:32.620
I suppose you could do this fasted

01:27:32.620 --> 01:27:35.047
so you get the further
increase in norepinephrine,

01:27:35.047 --> 01:27:37.240
then you get the dopamine
increase from the cold exposure,

01:27:37.240 --> 01:27:38.920
the binding of the dopamine.

01:27:38.920 --> 01:27:42.140
Although I do want to point
out that at some point,

01:27:42.140 --> 01:27:44.110
you start layering
together enough protocols

01:27:44.110 --> 01:27:45.710
that you would to be
spending your entire day

01:27:45.710 --> 01:27:47.120
trying to get this dopamine pulse

01:27:47.120 --> 01:27:49.600
and I would hope that you
would have other activities

01:27:49.600 --> 01:27:50.810
that you would engage in,

01:27:50.810 --> 01:27:53.810
but if you're getting up in
the morning and you're fasted

01:27:53.810 --> 01:27:55.070
because you haven't eaten all night

01:27:55.070 --> 01:27:57.380
and you have a cup of coffee,
and then 60 minutes later,

01:27:57.380 --> 01:28:00.540
you take your cold shower
or two hours later,

01:28:00.540 --> 01:28:02.930
you do your cold immersion
or your cold shower,

01:28:02.930 --> 01:28:04.760
you would be layering together

01:28:04.760 --> 01:28:06.950
these different mechanisms
of dopamine receptors,

01:28:06.950 --> 01:28:09.210
epinephrine and so forth in
a way that at least to me,

01:28:09.210 --> 01:28:12.500
doesn't seem incompatible
with having some other life

01:28:12.500 --> 01:28:15.090
like going to school and having
relationships, et cetera.

01:28:15.090 --> 01:28:16.770
And this increase in dopamine,

01:28:16.770 --> 01:28:20.780
particularly in the striatum
is not a trivial one.

01:28:20.780 --> 01:28:23.210
I do want to point out as the authors do

01:28:23.210 --> 01:28:24.900
that preclinical studies have shown

01:28:24.900 --> 01:28:27.380
that increases in striatal dopamine

01:28:27.380 --> 01:28:29.430
induced by things like modafinil,

01:28:29.430 --> 01:28:33.320
which is used to treat
ADHD and treat narcolepsy,

01:28:33.320 --> 01:28:36.180
is necessary for their
wake promoting actions.

01:28:36.180 --> 01:28:38.670
What this really says is that
just having elevated levels

01:28:38.670 --> 01:28:41.230
of dopamine from a drug
or from an ice bath,

01:28:41.230 --> 01:28:43.310
or what have you is not sufficient

01:28:43.310 --> 01:28:44.610
to get the effects of dopamine,

01:28:44.610 --> 01:28:47.410
you really need the
receptors to be available

01:28:47.410 --> 01:28:49.090
and you need those
receptors to be available

01:28:49.090 --> 01:28:50.480
in the appropriate density

01:28:50.480 --> 01:28:52.640
and you need those
receptors to be available

01:28:52.640 --> 01:28:55.350
in the appropriate density in
the striatum in particular.

01:28:55.350 --> 01:28:57.540
So I think there are a
number of reasons why

01:28:57.540 --> 01:29:00.600
if it's compatible with the
other aspects of your health,

01:29:00.600 --> 01:29:02.320
cause of course always
you have to consider this

01:29:02.320 --> 01:29:03.990
on a background of cardiovascular health

01:29:03.990 --> 01:29:05.200
and blood pressure, et cetera,

01:29:05.200 --> 01:29:07.290
that ingesting a cup or two of coffee

01:29:07.290 --> 01:29:11.290
an hour before your ice
bath may be fasted as well

01:29:11.290 --> 01:29:13.550
could be quite beneficial
for increasing dopamine

01:29:13.550 --> 01:29:15.690
over quite extended periods of time.

01:29:15.690 --> 01:29:18.290
A couple of key points that
you'll want to pay attention to

01:29:18.290 --> 01:29:21.300
in thinking about deliberate
cold exposure and metabolism.

01:29:21.300 --> 01:29:22.300
In the Søberg study,

01:29:22.300 --> 01:29:27.100
they also explored the use of
sauna and how to use sauna,

01:29:27.100 --> 01:29:30.450
meaning deliberate heat
in conjunction with cold.

01:29:30.450 --> 01:29:33.490
We are going to do an entire
episode about the use of heat

01:29:33.490 --> 01:29:34.700
for health and performance.

01:29:34.700 --> 01:29:38.800
So that is not the focus now, however,

01:29:38.800 --> 01:29:40.200
it does raise an important point

01:29:40.200 --> 01:29:42.150
that we do need to address at this moment,

01:29:42.150 --> 01:29:45.000
which is if you are using sauna

01:29:45.000 --> 01:29:47.340
or if you are taking warm showers

01:29:47.340 --> 01:29:50.090
or if you are simply using
deliberate cold exposure

01:29:50.090 --> 01:29:51.190
of any kind,

01:29:51.190 --> 01:29:54.060
should you get into the heat afterward

01:29:54.060 --> 01:29:55.840
or before or not at all?

01:29:55.840 --> 01:29:59.260
And this is where we can
point to the so-called

01:29:59.260 --> 01:30:02.380
Søberg principle, at least I
call it the Søberg principle,

01:30:02.380 --> 01:30:05.330
the Søberg principle named after
first author of this study,

01:30:05.330 --> 01:30:08.083
I referred to earlier, Dr. Susanna Søberg.

01:30:09.170 --> 01:30:12.980
In science, it is appropriate
to take a key piece of data

01:30:12.980 --> 01:30:14.510
and call it a principle

01:30:14.510 --> 01:30:16.540
if in fact it translates
to something larger,

01:30:16.540 --> 01:30:18.200
which I believe it does.

01:30:18.200 --> 01:30:20.410
It is generally not appropriate for people

01:30:20.410 --> 01:30:22.020
to name a principle after themselves

01:30:22.020 --> 01:30:25.430
although there are a few
scientists that have done that.

01:30:25.430 --> 01:30:27.050
So I have named it the Søberg principle,

01:30:27.050 --> 01:30:30.310
but I did that to give
it appropriate credit

01:30:30.310 --> 01:30:32.070
to Dr. Susanna Søberg,

01:30:32.070 --> 01:30:36.290
who discovered that and pointed
out quite appropriately,

01:30:36.290 --> 01:30:40.430
that to achieve the greatest
increases in metabolism

01:30:40.430 --> 01:30:42.760
through deliberate cold exposure,

01:30:42.760 --> 01:30:47.490
you want to force yourself
to reheat on your own

01:30:47.490 --> 01:30:49.280
after the deliberate cold exposure,

01:30:49.280 --> 01:30:52.160
meaning you wouldn't want
to go from the cold shower

01:30:52.160 --> 01:30:54.870
to a hot shower or from
the cold shower to a sauna.

01:30:54.870 --> 01:30:57.170
Rather if you were going
to start with a hot shower

01:30:57.170 --> 01:30:58.770
or you're going to start with a sauna

01:30:58.770 --> 01:31:01.030
that you would end with the cold,

01:31:01.030 --> 01:31:03.830
and then you would reheat naturally.

01:31:03.830 --> 01:31:06.930
Now I personally take a cold
shower a few times a week

01:31:06.930 --> 01:31:08.330
or do cold immersion

01:31:08.330 --> 01:31:10.730
and because I'm not specifically focused

01:31:10.730 --> 01:31:13.920
on increasing metabolism,
although I probably should be,

01:31:13.920 --> 01:31:16.490
that's not what I'm using it for now,

01:31:16.490 --> 01:31:18.840
I will take a hot shower
afterwards and in doing so,

01:31:18.840 --> 01:31:19.920
I'm short circuiting

01:31:19.920 --> 01:31:22.810
some of the further metabolic
increases that I would achieve

01:31:22.810 --> 01:31:25.210
were I to just end with the cold.

01:31:25.210 --> 01:31:27.750
So the Søberg principle is

01:31:27.750 --> 01:31:31.550
if you want to increase your
metabolism, end with cold,

01:31:31.550 --> 01:31:34.600
and we can take this
a step further and say

01:31:34.600 --> 01:31:37.010
that if you want to use
deliberate cold exposure

01:31:37.010 --> 01:31:40.980
to increase metabolism,
that you should make sure

01:31:40.980 --> 01:31:43.640
that you get to the
point where you shiver.

01:31:43.640 --> 01:31:45.500
And the reason in for
this is that there are

01:31:45.500 --> 01:31:47.110
a series of studies, but in particular,

01:31:47.110 --> 01:31:49.000
one study published in the journal Nature,

01:31:49.000 --> 01:31:51.770
excellent journal in the year 2018,

01:31:51.770 --> 01:31:56.770
showing that deliberate cold
exposure that evokes shivering

01:31:57.810 --> 01:32:00.840
from the muscles causes
the release of a molecule

01:32:00.840 --> 01:32:03.480
called succinate from the muscles

01:32:03.480 --> 01:32:05.410
and that succinate plays

01:32:05.410 --> 01:32:08.860
a key role in activating
brown fat thermogenesis,

01:32:08.860 --> 01:32:10.990
which you now have heard
about and understand

01:32:10.990 --> 01:32:13.140
as critical to the increases in metabolism

01:32:13.140 --> 01:32:15.890
caused by deliberate cold exposure.

01:32:15.890 --> 01:32:17.250
So what this means is if you want to

01:32:17.250 --> 01:32:19.160
increase your metabolism, end on cold,

01:32:19.160 --> 01:32:22.300
that's a Søberg principle
and as best you can,

01:32:22.300 --> 01:32:24.900
try and get to the point
where you are shivering

01:32:24.900 --> 01:32:27.400
either when you are in the cold exposure

01:32:27.400 --> 01:32:29.290
or immediately afterwards.

01:32:29.290 --> 01:32:32.620
Now one efficient way to do
this is to, for instance,

01:32:32.620 --> 01:32:36.120
you could get into the
cold shower for a minute

01:32:36.120 --> 01:32:38.970
or two minutes or three
minutes, uncomfortably cold,

01:32:38.970 --> 01:32:40.120
but safe to stay in.

01:32:40.120 --> 01:32:42.500
Remember that's our general rule of thumb.

01:32:42.500 --> 01:32:45.470
Then turn off the water and stand there,

01:32:45.470 --> 01:32:47.860
make sure that you're not holding yourself

01:32:47.860 --> 01:32:48.693
close to your body,

01:32:48.693 --> 01:32:50.810
you're not hugging yourself
to try and keep yourself warm,

01:32:50.810 --> 01:32:53.960
but rather your limbs are
extended at your sides.

01:32:53.960 --> 01:32:56.900
And then if that fails to induce shiver

01:32:56.900 --> 01:32:59.710
than to turn on the cold water again,

01:32:59.710 --> 01:33:00.950
and then turn it off again,

01:33:00.950 --> 01:33:03.030
so alternating perhaps a
minute to three minutes

01:33:03.030 --> 01:33:05.560
of cold exposure followed
by a minute to three minutes

01:33:05.560 --> 01:33:07.690
of drying out in air

01:33:07.690 --> 01:33:10.650
and going back into the
cold exposure, et cetera.

01:33:10.650 --> 01:33:12.170
I can tell you this from experience,

01:33:12.170 --> 01:33:14.163
this is a pretty brutal protocol.

01:33:15.190 --> 01:33:18.730
If you have never tried
getting into an ice bath

01:33:18.730 --> 01:33:21.630
or cold water immersion
or shower for one minute

01:33:21.630 --> 01:33:23.370
and then getting out and
trying to stand there

01:33:23.370 --> 01:33:27.250
with your arms extended in cool
or cold air for one minute,

01:33:27.250 --> 01:33:28.810
and then getting back into the cold shower

01:33:28.810 --> 01:33:33.470
or water immersion, you are
in for an experience because

01:33:33.470 --> 01:33:36.110
even for those of you that
are pretty shiver resistant,

01:33:36.110 --> 01:33:38.270
you'll find that it is much, much harder

01:33:38.270 --> 01:33:41.920
to get out of that cold water
and stand there arms extended

01:33:41.920 --> 01:33:43.820
and drying off by evaporation,

01:33:43.820 --> 01:33:46.640
which further draws heat from the body

01:33:46.640 --> 01:33:48.300
than it is to wrap yourself in a towel,

01:33:48.300 --> 01:33:50.210
get in a warm shower or a sauna.

01:33:50.210 --> 01:33:54.090
So there's certainly no
requirement to end on cold.

01:33:54.090 --> 01:33:57.650
There's certainly no
requirement to induce shiver,

01:33:57.650 --> 01:33:59.340
but if your primary goal

01:33:59.340 --> 01:34:02.760
is to induce increases in metabolism,

01:34:02.760 --> 01:34:05.180
both in the short term
and in the long term,

01:34:05.180 --> 01:34:06.690
following the cold exposure,

01:34:06.690 --> 01:34:08.010
well then you'll want to end on cold

01:34:08.010 --> 01:34:10.570
and you'll want to find a way to shiver

01:34:10.570 --> 01:34:12.830
provided that the level of cold

01:34:12.830 --> 01:34:14.130
that you're exposing yourself to

01:34:14.130 --> 01:34:15.880
is still safe for you overall.

01:34:15.880 --> 01:34:16.713
So up until now,

01:34:16.713 --> 01:34:18.840
I've been talking about
deliberate cold exposure

01:34:18.840 --> 01:34:21.980
as a potent stimulus for the
release of norepinephrine

01:34:21.980 --> 01:34:24.430
in the brain and body and indeed it is,

01:34:24.430 --> 01:34:27.940
but the way I've been describing
it has been in the context

01:34:27.940 --> 01:34:31.870
of circulating plasma
levels of norepinephrine,

01:34:31.870 --> 01:34:33.720
meaning circulating within the blood.

01:34:34.660 --> 01:34:35.780
What I haven't mentioned,

01:34:35.780 --> 01:34:40.780
but is absolutely true is
that the fat cells themselves

01:34:41.740 --> 01:34:44.740
actually receive input from neurons.

01:34:44.740 --> 01:34:47.540
So there are neurons that
release norepinephrine

01:34:47.540 --> 01:34:51.100
in response to cold,
directly into the fat.

01:34:51.100 --> 01:34:55.100
So I want to give you this
picture of how the architecture

01:34:55.100 --> 01:34:55.933
of all this works,

01:34:55.933 --> 01:34:58.020
because I think it can help you navigate

01:34:58.020 --> 01:35:01.810
and indeed build better deliberate
cold exposure protocols.

01:35:01.810 --> 01:35:05.360
Your adrenal glands release
norepinephrine and epinephrine.

01:35:05.360 --> 01:35:08.940
Your brain has sites within
it like the locus coeruleus

01:35:08.940 --> 01:35:12.050
that release norepinephrine
and epinephrine,

01:35:12.050 --> 01:35:16.230
but there are also neurons
within your skin that sense cold

01:35:16.230 --> 01:35:19.410
and other neurons that can
directly release norepinephrine

01:35:19.410 --> 01:35:22.680
into the fat stores and
cause those white fat cells

01:35:22.680 --> 01:35:24.420
to convert to beige and brown fat.

01:35:24.420 --> 01:35:26.960
And I think this particular
aspect of our physiology

01:35:26.960 --> 01:35:29.770
is often overlooked in studies.

01:35:29.770 --> 01:35:31.060
And when people say, oh,

01:35:31.060 --> 01:35:33.630
well the increases in
metabolism aren't that great,

01:35:33.630 --> 01:35:35.370
the circulating levels of norepinephrine,

01:35:35.370 --> 01:35:39.220
those are very large, but
they're very transient and so on,

01:35:39.220 --> 01:35:43.000
that fails to understand that neurons

01:35:43.000 --> 01:35:46.870
that actually sense cold are
in a position to communicate

01:35:46.870 --> 01:35:49.970
via other neurons
directly to the fat cells

01:35:49.970 --> 01:35:51.900
and release norepinephrine
into those fat cells,

01:35:51.900 --> 01:35:53.570
which as I pointed out earlier,

01:35:53.570 --> 01:35:55.570
set off a huge set of immediate

01:35:55.570 --> 01:35:58.913
and long term cascades of
even gene expression changes.

01:35:59.890 --> 01:36:02.070
So the picture that I'd like
you to have in your mind

01:36:02.070 --> 01:36:04.450
is that when you get into the cold, yes,

01:36:04.450 --> 01:36:07.790
of course you experience
that as a experience of

01:36:07.790 --> 01:36:09.590
I don't want to do this,
I'm going to overcome this,

01:36:09.590 --> 01:36:11.330
I'm going to climb over these mental walls

01:36:11.330 --> 01:36:14.050
that represent adrenaline
release in my brain and body,

01:36:14.050 --> 01:36:18.120
but also that your fat
cells are receiving signals,

01:36:18.120 --> 01:36:20.837
norepinephrine signals that
are changing those fat cells

01:36:20.837 --> 01:36:22.900
and the way that they metabolize energy.

01:36:22.900 --> 01:36:24.670
Now I'd like to shift
our attention to the use

01:36:24.670 --> 01:36:26.290
of deliberate cold exposure

01:36:26.290 --> 01:36:28.780
for sake of physical performance.

01:36:28.780 --> 01:36:30.450
And there are a lot of opinions out there

01:36:30.450 --> 01:36:32.480
about the use of deliberate cold,

01:36:32.480 --> 01:36:34.030
whether or not it should
be done for instance,

01:36:34.030 --> 01:36:36.200
before or after exercise,

01:36:36.200 --> 01:36:39.760
whether or not if done immediately
after strength training

01:36:39.760 --> 01:36:40.840
or hypertrophy training,

01:36:40.840 --> 01:36:43.490
meaning training designed to grow muscles

01:36:43.490 --> 01:36:44.380
or make them stronger,

01:36:44.380 --> 01:36:46.180
whether or not it can inhibit that process

01:36:46.180 --> 01:36:47.640
and so on and so forth.

01:36:47.640 --> 01:36:50.690
I think today in looking
over the literature

01:36:50.690 --> 01:36:52.750
and trying to bring forward the simplest

01:36:52.750 --> 01:36:53.630
and most straightforward,

01:36:53.630 --> 01:36:56.030
and yet scientifically grounded protocols,

01:36:56.030 --> 01:36:59.280
we can set up some general
guidelines that will allow most,

01:36:59.280 --> 01:37:01.520
if not, all of you to
still extract the benefits

01:37:01.520 --> 01:37:05.080
of deliberate cold exposure
on physical performance

01:37:05.080 --> 01:37:08.410
without getting too neurotic
about the exact timing,

01:37:08.410 --> 01:37:10.710
but for sake of discussion

01:37:10.710 --> 01:37:13.270
and because it's a prominent theme

01:37:13.270 --> 01:37:14.920
in many online communities,

01:37:14.920 --> 01:37:18.180
let's just start with
the big one out there,

01:37:18.180 --> 01:37:22.000
meaning the question of whether
or not doing an ice bath

01:37:22.000 --> 01:37:25.070
or doing deliberate cold
exposure or taking a cold shower

01:37:25.070 --> 01:37:28.310
after strength slash hypertrophy training,

01:37:28.310 --> 01:37:30.720
meaning training designed
to increase strength

01:37:30.720 --> 01:37:34.150
and or I should say the size of muscles

01:37:34.150 --> 01:37:37.840
will somehow short circuit
or diminish that process,

01:37:37.840 --> 01:37:39.890
whether or not it will reduce or eliminate

01:37:39.890 --> 01:37:41.700
those strength gains
and hypertrophy gains.

01:37:41.700 --> 01:37:45.080
And the short answer that
I was able to arrive at

01:37:45.080 --> 01:37:48.200
on the basis of a review
article that I'll talk about

01:37:48.200 --> 01:37:51.170
in a moment and some
other studies as well,

01:37:51.170 --> 01:37:56.170
is that if your main goal
is hypertrophy and strength,

01:37:56.330 --> 01:38:00.500
it is probably best to
avoid cold water immersion

01:38:00.500 --> 01:38:04.370
and ice bath immersion in the four hours

01:38:04.370 --> 01:38:06.890
immediately following that strength

01:38:06.890 --> 01:38:08.690
and or hypertrophy training.

01:38:08.690 --> 01:38:12.910
Again, if your main goal
is to achieve hypertrophy

01:38:12.910 --> 01:38:15.940
or strength or some combination of those,

01:38:15.940 --> 01:38:20.200
probably best to avoid cold
water immersion up to the neck

01:38:20.200 --> 01:38:23.070
or ice bath immersion up to the neck

01:38:23.070 --> 01:38:26.120
immediately after strength
and hypertrophy training

01:38:26.120 --> 01:38:30.163
and extending out to about
four hours after that training.

01:38:31.150 --> 01:38:32.950
If you're really neurotic about this,

01:38:32.950 --> 01:38:35.520
then perhaps you'd want to
move the cold water exposure

01:38:35.520 --> 01:38:37.170
to a different day entirely,

01:38:37.170 --> 01:38:39.840
but it all depends on how
neurotically attached you are

01:38:39.840 --> 01:38:42.380
to getting every last bit
of strength and hypertrophy.

01:38:42.380 --> 01:38:44.090
And if that's your goal, terrific,

01:38:44.090 --> 01:38:47.770
well then probably moving
the cold exposure four hours

01:38:47.770 --> 01:38:49.080
or more away from that training

01:38:49.080 --> 01:38:50.740
is going to be necessary for you.

01:38:50.740 --> 01:38:53.730
Now you'll notice I did
not talk about cold showers

01:38:53.730 --> 01:38:55.880
and the reason I did not
talk about cold showers

01:38:55.880 --> 01:38:59.380
is that there simply are
not very many studies

01:38:59.380 --> 01:39:02.010
of deliberate cold exposure
through cold showers

01:39:02.010 --> 01:39:03.080
for the reasons I talked about

01:39:03.080 --> 01:39:05.130
at the beginning of the episode.

01:39:05.130 --> 01:39:08.400
It's hard for me to imagine
that taking a brief cold shower

01:39:08.400 --> 01:39:11.070
after a strength or
hypertrophy training session

01:39:11.070 --> 01:39:13.700
would completely reverse or
short circuit the effects

01:39:13.700 --> 01:39:15.510
of that strength and hypertrophy training.

01:39:15.510 --> 01:39:17.900
But again, if you're neurotically attached

01:39:17.900 --> 01:39:21.140
to getting every last bit
of strength and hypertrophy

01:39:21.140 --> 01:39:23.730
out of your training
sessions, then by all means,

01:39:23.730 --> 01:39:26.280
err on the side of caution
and wait four hours or more

01:39:26.280 --> 01:39:29.650
to do your cold shower just
as you would wait four hours

01:39:29.650 --> 01:39:31.620
or more to do your cold water immersion.

01:39:31.620 --> 01:39:33.950
Now there are nice data
pointing to the fact

01:39:33.950 --> 01:39:37.600
that doing cold water
immersion after a hard run,

01:39:37.600 --> 01:39:39.100
so endurance training,

01:39:39.100 --> 01:39:41.690
or even sprint and interval training

01:39:41.690 --> 01:39:45.690
or after a weight workout
where your main focus

01:39:45.690 --> 01:39:47.560
is on performance of those movements

01:39:47.560 --> 01:39:49.230
or after a skill training workout

01:39:49.230 --> 01:39:52.300
where your main focus on
performance of those movements,

01:39:52.300 --> 01:39:54.580
that there's no reason to think that that

01:39:54.580 --> 01:39:57.410
cold water immersion or
ice bath or cold shower

01:39:57.410 --> 01:40:01.580
would inhibit the progress or the stimulus

01:40:01.580 --> 01:40:03.400
that would lead to progress

01:40:03.400 --> 01:40:05.290
that occurred during
that training session.

01:40:05.290 --> 01:40:07.490
In other words, I don't see any reason

01:40:07.490 --> 01:40:10.600
based on the literature to
avoid deliberate cold exposure

01:40:10.600 --> 01:40:12.080
immediately after training,

01:40:12.080 --> 01:40:15.920
again unless your goal is
hypertrophy and strength.

01:40:15.920 --> 01:40:16.753
And in fact,

01:40:16.753 --> 01:40:19.220
there's a very nice
review recently published

01:40:19.220 --> 01:40:21.610
on deliberate cold exposure

01:40:21.610 --> 01:40:24.750
and how it can impact
physical performance,

01:40:24.750 --> 01:40:27.780
whether or not it's done before or after,

01:40:27.780 --> 01:40:30.300
different types of training and so forth.

01:40:30.300 --> 01:40:33.550
The paper is entitled impact
of cold water immersion

01:40:33.550 --> 01:40:35.310
compared with passive recovery,

01:40:35.310 --> 01:40:37.820
following a single about
of strenuous exercise

01:40:37.820 --> 01:40:41.320
on athletic performance in
physically active participants,

01:40:41.320 --> 01:40:45.120
a systematic review with
meta-analysis and meta regression.

01:40:45.120 --> 01:40:49.570
So this is a meta-analysis of 52 studies

01:40:49.570 --> 01:40:51.930
that looked at a tremendous
number of variables

01:40:51.930 --> 01:40:55.250
and contexts, as you would
expect in a meta-analysis

01:40:55.250 --> 01:40:56.850
of 52 studies.

01:40:56.850 --> 01:40:58.410
I'm going to read you the
conclusions of the study

01:40:58.410 --> 01:40:59.930
and I will provide a link.

01:40:59.930 --> 01:41:01.610
We certainly don't have
the time to go through

01:41:01.610 --> 01:41:02.740
all the details of the study.

01:41:02.740 --> 01:41:05.150
I will highlight a few specific outcomes

01:41:05.150 --> 01:41:07.100
that I found particularly interesting,

01:41:07.100 --> 01:41:10.440
but here I am paraphrasing
their conclusions,

01:41:10.440 --> 01:41:15.130
that cold water immersion, I
want to emphasize immersion,

01:41:15.130 --> 01:41:18.120
not cold showers, but cold water immersion

01:41:18.120 --> 01:41:20.080
they say was an effective recovery tool

01:41:20.080 --> 01:41:22.340
after high intensity exercise.

01:41:22.340 --> 01:41:23.880
They observed positive outcomes,

01:41:23.880 --> 01:41:27.410
meaning improvements in certain variables,

01:41:27.410 --> 01:41:30.310
for muscular power, muscular soreness,

01:41:30.310 --> 01:41:31.610
meaning reduced muscular soreness,

01:41:31.610 --> 01:41:33.320
increased muscular power,

01:41:33.320 --> 01:41:36.760
perceived recovery after
24 hours of exercise.

01:41:36.760 --> 01:41:39.360
However, there were
certain forms of exercise

01:41:39.360 --> 01:41:43.370
that were not benefited
by cold water immersion,

01:41:43.370 --> 01:41:45.830
such as eccentric exercise,

01:41:45.830 --> 01:41:49.260
exercise focusing only
on the lowering component

01:41:49.260 --> 01:41:52.750
or the so-called eccentric
component of resistance exercise.

01:41:52.750 --> 01:41:56.670
They saw some very entry
dose response relationships

01:41:56.670 --> 01:42:00.470
for things like endurance
training, meaning the longer

01:42:00.470 --> 01:42:02.840
the cold exposure post-endurance training,

01:42:02.840 --> 01:42:05.330
the more improvement in
endurance performance,

01:42:05.330 --> 01:42:08.790
reductions in circulating creatine kinases

01:42:08.790 --> 01:42:11.300
and things that relate to muscle damage

01:42:11.300 --> 01:42:12.400
under certain conditions.

01:42:12.400 --> 01:42:14.280
At some point in the future, by the way,

01:42:14.280 --> 01:42:17.230
we'll do an entire episode on
creatine and creatine kinase,

01:42:17.230 --> 01:42:19.740
which are important not
just for muscular function,

01:42:19.740 --> 01:42:21.620
but also for brain function.

01:42:21.620 --> 01:42:25.320
But the basic takeaway was
that cold water immersion

01:42:25.320 --> 01:42:27.750
performed after high intensity exercise

01:42:27.750 --> 01:42:30.760
was beneficial from a number
of different standpoints

01:42:30.760 --> 01:42:34.430
and indicated that shorter duration,

01:42:34.430 --> 01:42:37.210
cold exposure and lower temperatures

01:42:37.210 --> 01:42:40.580
can improve the efficacy
of cold water exposure

01:42:40.580 --> 01:42:42.970
if used after high intensity exercise.

01:42:42.970 --> 01:42:45.450
There I'm directly pulling
from their conclusions.

01:42:45.450 --> 01:42:47.920
So what this says is
that it's not just those

01:42:47.920 --> 01:42:52.460
longer duration, 30, 45
minute and 60 minute protocols

01:42:52.460 --> 01:42:54.910
of cold water immersion
that we discussed earlier,

01:42:54.910 --> 01:42:58.130
but also shorter duration
of one minute, three minute,

01:42:58.130 --> 01:43:02.510
five minute exposures
to lower temperatures.

01:43:02.510 --> 01:43:04.790
Temperatures that would
make you psychologically

01:43:04.790 --> 01:43:07.730
want to get out as soon
as you possibly can,

01:43:07.730 --> 01:43:10.030
but again that you can safely stay in

01:43:10.030 --> 01:43:12.810
done after training
really have been shown to

01:43:12.810 --> 01:43:15.970
improve outcomes in terms
of reducing soreness

01:43:15.970 --> 01:43:18.750
and improving training efficacy,

01:43:18.750 --> 01:43:22.480
meaning your ability to get
back into training more quickly

01:43:22.480 --> 01:43:25.770
and thereby deliver more training stimuli

01:43:25.770 --> 01:43:29.110
to a given muscle or in your
endurance training protocol.

01:43:29.110 --> 01:43:29.943
Translate to English,

01:43:29.943 --> 01:43:33.020
what this means is that taking
a cold shower or getting into

01:43:33.020 --> 01:43:35.810
an ice bath or some other
form of cold water immersion

01:43:35.810 --> 01:43:39.160
within the immediate minutes
or even the immediate hours

01:43:39.160 --> 01:43:41.960
following your training has
been shown to be beneficial.

01:43:43.120 --> 01:43:45.860
I'm sure a number of you
have questions for instance,

01:43:45.860 --> 01:43:49.070
how long should you be
in that cold exposure?

01:43:49.070 --> 01:43:52.750
Is it the same as the 11 minute
threshold described earlier?

01:43:52.750 --> 01:43:53.680
To be honest with you,

01:43:53.680 --> 01:43:56.340
there are not enough
studies to really point

01:43:56.340 --> 01:43:59.900
to the critical threshold for eliminating

01:43:59.900 --> 01:44:01.840
or reducing delayed onset muscle soreness

01:44:01.840 --> 01:44:05.400
or for getting maximal results from power

01:44:05.400 --> 01:44:06.600
and endurance training,

01:44:06.600 --> 01:44:09.340
but this study does make
a couple of key points

01:44:09.340 --> 01:44:11.220
and here I will just paraphrase.

01:44:11.220 --> 01:44:13.670
For instance, that cold water immersion

01:44:13.670 --> 01:44:16.900
is more likely to positively influence

01:44:16.900 --> 01:44:18.700
muscular power performance,

01:44:18.700 --> 01:44:22.730
to reduce muscle soreness, to
reduce serum creatine kinase,

01:44:22.730 --> 01:44:24.960
and to improve perceived recovery

01:44:24.960 --> 01:44:26.810
after high intensity exercise,

01:44:26.810 --> 01:44:29.290
as compared with passive recovery.

01:44:29.290 --> 01:44:33.040
This can be translated to cold
water exposure after training

01:44:33.040 --> 01:44:35.480
is beneficial and probably better

01:44:35.480 --> 01:44:38.670
than passive recovery from
a number of standpoints.

01:44:38.670 --> 01:44:41.970
In addition, they say that
dose response relationships

01:44:42.910 --> 01:44:47.160
meaning the amount and the degree of cold

01:44:47.160 --> 01:44:49.910
that people were exposed to
and how often they did that

01:44:51.270 --> 01:44:53.770
in particular in lower
temperature cold immersion.

01:44:53.770 --> 01:44:57.050
So these would be the sorts
of cold immersion protocols

01:44:57.050 --> 01:44:59.570
that are one minute or two
minutes, three minutes,

01:44:59.570 --> 01:45:00.860
maybe five minutes,

01:45:00.860 --> 01:45:02.830
but that one couldn't stay in there longer

01:45:02.830 --> 01:45:03.820
because it feels stressful

01:45:03.820 --> 01:45:05.610
and one wants to get out.

01:45:05.610 --> 01:45:08.280
Maybe more effective after
high intensity exercise

01:45:08.280 --> 01:45:11.410
for removal of serum creatine kinase

01:45:11.410 --> 01:45:13.460
as well that these shorter duration

01:45:13.460 --> 01:45:16.130
cold water immersion approaches
may be more effective

01:45:16.130 --> 01:45:19.860
after high intensity
endurance performance as well.

01:45:19.860 --> 01:45:22.920
So all of this can be
translated to say that

01:45:22.920 --> 01:45:27.200
unless your main goal is
hypertrophy and strength,

01:45:27.200 --> 01:45:30.877
that cold exposure, ideally cold immersion

01:45:30.877 --> 01:45:32.590
and cold water ice bath,

01:45:32.590 --> 01:45:34.280
but if you don't have access to that,

01:45:34.280 --> 01:45:37.220
then cold showers is likely
going to be beneficial

01:45:37.220 --> 01:45:39.900
if done immediately
after or in the minutes

01:45:39.900 --> 01:45:42.500
or hours after your training,

01:45:42.500 --> 01:45:44.310
especially high intensity training.

01:45:44.310 --> 01:45:46.570
One particularly nice thing
about this meta-analysis

01:45:46.570 --> 01:45:48.920
is that it included some studies that

01:45:48.920 --> 01:45:50.770
involve the use of cooling packs.

01:45:50.770 --> 01:45:55.430
So again, vests that can
essentially ice packs

01:45:55.430 --> 01:45:59.040
and indeed even cryotherapy
chambers and so on.

01:45:59.040 --> 01:46:00.370
There's a nice table in the study

01:46:00.370 --> 01:46:02.220
if you want to get really detailed and go

01:46:02.220 --> 01:46:03.810
and look specifically at those studies,

01:46:03.810 --> 01:46:04.850
I invite you to do that.

01:46:04.850 --> 01:46:07.260
We'll put a link to this
study in the caption

01:46:07.260 --> 01:46:09.320
for this episode, but all in all,

01:46:09.320 --> 01:46:12.990
what this study shows is
that deliberate cold exposure

01:46:12.990 --> 01:46:17.000
can be very useful for recovery
likely through reductions

01:46:17.000 --> 01:46:20.690
in inflammation, in muscle
and connective tissue.

01:46:20.690 --> 01:46:22.940
And while this study did
not look specifically

01:46:22.940 --> 01:46:24.990
at the mechanisms of reduced inflammation

01:46:25.900 --> 01:46:27.890
caused by deliberate cold exposure,

01:46:27.890 --> 01:46:30.520
those mechanisms are somewhat known.

01:46:30.520 --> 01:46:32.730
There are a number of studies
that have pointed to the fact

01:46:32.730 --> 01:46:34.870
that deliberate cold and cold generally

01:46:34.870 --> 01:46:37.400
can reduce inflammatory cytokines,

01:46:37.400 --> 01:46:39.560
such as IL-6, interleukin six.

01:46:39.560 --> 01:46:43.160
It can increase
anti-inflammatory cytokines

01:46:43.160 --> 01:46:45.293
such as interleukin 10 and so on.

01:46:46.310 --> 01:46:48.040
Without getting into all those details,

01:46:48.040 --> 01:46:50.590
I think it's sufficient to say that

01:46:50.590 --> 01:46:52.180
if you are somebody who experiences

01:46:52.180 --> 01:46:54.300
a lot of delayed onset muscle soreness,

01:46:54.300 --> 01:46:56.060
taking a cold shower after your training

01:46:56.060 --> 01:46:58.370
or getting into a cold
immersion after your training,

01:46:58.370 --> 01:47:01.060
even if it's a few hours
later ought to help.

01:47:01.060 --> 01:47:04.610
And if you are doing
particularly intense training,

01:47:04.610 --> 01:47:07.460
then you probably want
to ratchet up the number

01:47:07.460 --> 01:47:10.220
of cold exposure sessions
that you're doing

01:47:10.220 --> 01:47:12.420
even if those have to
be done on separate days

01:47:12.420 --> 01:47:13.253
from your training,

01:47:13.253 --> 01:47:15.910
because a lot of the
inflammatory effects of training,

01:47:15.910 --> 01:47:17.920
endurance and strength
training are actually occurring

01:47:17.920 --> 01:47:20.720
some hours away from
the training stimulus.

01:47:20.720 --> 01:47:22.380
So it's not just that inflammation goes up

01:47:22.380 --> 01:47:25.100
radically during training,
which it often can,

01:47:25.100 --> 01:47:27.260
but that it can occur even in the days

01:47:27.260 --> 01:47:28.480
and even weeks afterwards,

01:47:28.480 --> 01:47:30.440
depending how intense
and how long duration

01:47:30.440 --> 01:47:31.340
that training is.

01:47:31.340 --> 01:47:33.870
So deliberate cold
exposure is very powerful

01:47:33.870 --> 01:47:36.440
as an anti-inflammatory tool.

01:47:36.440 --> 01:47:38.600
Now I'd like to emphasize
the topic that we touched on

01:47:38.600 --> 01:47:40.430
at the beginning of the episode,

01:47:40.430 --> 01:47:43.140
which are those glabrous skin surfaces,

01:47:43.140 --> 01:47:44.420
the hands, the upper face,

01:47:44.420 --> 01:47:47.870
and the bottoms of the
feet through which heat

01:47:47.870 --> 01:47:50.460
is especially good at leaving the body.

01:47:50.460 --> 01:47:54.860
And another way of putting
that is that one can cool

01:47:54.860 --> 01:47:56.640
the body much more efficiently

01:47:56.640 --> 01:47:58.920
through the glabrous skin surfaces.

01:47:58.920 --> 01:48:01.530
Now, if you want to
understand all of the science

01:48:01.530 --> 01:48:05.900
behind this and all of
the various applications,

01:48:05.900 --> 01:48:07.840
I invite you to please
listen to the episode

01:48:07.840 --> 01:48:10.320
that I did with Dr. Craig Heller, again,

01:48:10.320 --> 01:48:12.253
in the biology department at Stanford.

01:48:13.710 --> 01:48:14.760
For sake of this episode,

01:48:14.760 --> 01:48:17.100
I'm just going to detail
a couple of findings

01:48:17.100 --> 01:48:18.410
from his laboratory.

01:48:18.410 --> 01:48:21.430
The first one, dealing with
exercise induced hyperthermia,

01:48:21.430 --> 01:48:22.840
because I think this is very interesting

01:48:22.840 --> 01:48:24.620
and it can even save lives

01:48:24.620 --> 01:48:26.470
if you understand the way this works.

01:48:28.040 --> 01:48:30.730
There's a particular paper
that focuses on this,

01:48:30.730 --> 01:48:32.350
and we will put a link to this as well.

01:48:32.350 --> 01:48:34.200
The title of this paper
is novel application

01:48:34.200 --> 01:48:35.590
of chemical cold packs for treatment

01:48:35.590 --> 01:48:37.640
of exercise induced hyperthermia,

01:48:37.640 --> 01:48:39.600
a randomized control trial.

01:48:39.600 --> 01:48:42.200
This is a pretty brutal study,

01:48:42.200 --> 01:48:44.393
brutal for the subjects that is.

01:48:45.230 --> 01:48:49.490
This study involved was having
subjects walk on a treadmill

01:48:49.490 --> 01:48:51.320
at a pretty significant
incline and anywhere

01:48:51.320 --> 01:48:56.300
from nine to 17% wearing a
substantial amount of clothing

01:48:56.300 --> 01:48:58.240
that was not well ventilated

01:48:58.240 --> 01:49:00.730
and the room was kept
to 40 degrees Celsius,

01:49:00.730 --> 01:49:03.240
which is 104 degrees Fahrenheit.

01:49:03.240 --> 01:49:06.730
This is definitely not
something to do at home.

01:49:06.730 --> 01:49:09.210
This study was designed
to induce hyperthermia,

01:49:09.210 --> 01:49:11.650
which as I mentioned earlier,
can be quite dangerous.

01:49:11.650 --> 01:49:14.430
And they compared two types of cooling.

01:49:14.430 --> 01:49:16.240
In the first form of cooling

01:49:16.240 --> 01:49:18.240
that they call traditional cooling,

01:49:18.240 --> 01:49:21.540
they had ice packs on their neck,

01:49:21.540 --> 01:49:23.920
in their armpits and in their groin.

01:49:23.920 --> 01:49:25.550
And in the other group,

01:49:25.550 --> 01:49:27.870
there was the so-called
glabrous skin cooling.

01:49:27.870 --> 01:49:29.620
So the palms, the soles of the feet,

01:49:29.620 --> 01:49:32.730
which were actually so they
were cooling inside the boots

01:49:33.580 --> 01:49:38.203
or inside of gloves and on
the upper portion of the face.

01:49:39.090 --> 01:49:41.270
And the basic takeaway of this study

01:49:41.270 --> 01:49:43.283
is that by cooling the glabrous skin,

01:49:44.280 --> 01:49:47.310
the subjects were able
to sustain this walking

01:49:47.310 --> 01:49:50.930
on these inclined
treadmills for much longer

01:49:50.930 --> 01:49:54.240
than were the people who
received traditional cooling.

01:49:54.240 --> 01:49:56.420
And also the return to
baseline temperature

01:49:56.420 --> 01:49:59.860
was much faster in the
glabrous skin cooling group.

01:49:59.860 --> 01:50:01.890
So how this translates to the real world

01:50:01.890 --> 01:50:04.070
is that if ever you are hyperthermic

01:50:04.070 --> 01:50:05.620
or someone else's hyperthermic,

01:50:06.810 --> 01:50:10.430
one way to cool them
down quickly is to cool

01:50:10.430 --> 01:50:14.550
these palmer glabrous,
soles of the feet glabrous

01:50:14.550 --> 01:50:18.060
and upper portion of the face
glabrous portions of the body

01:50:18.060 --> 01:50:22.360
using cool rags, using ice
packs or using any number

01:50:22.360 --> 01:50:25.543
of different cold objects or temperatures.

01:50:26.470 --> 01:50:30.150
One key thing, if you're going
to use glabrous skin cooling,

01:50:30.150 --> 01:50:33.190
is that whatever you use
to cool those surfaces

01:50:33.190 --> 01:50:36.093
cannot be so cold that it
causes vasoconstriction.

01:50:37.120 --> 01:50:38.970
Because as I mentioned earlier,

01:50:38.970 --> 01:50:41.700
the arterio-venous anastomoses

01:50:41.700 --> 01:50:43.970
these portals of arteries
directly to veins

01:50:43.970 --> 01:50:47.803
that exist only in these
glabrous skin surfaces,

01:50:49.640 --> 01:50:51.610
the way that they're able to cool the body

01:50:51.610 --> 01:50:53.950
and essentially pass cool into the body

01:50:53.950 --> 01:50:55.110
although that's not
really what they're doing,

01:50:55.110 --> 01:50:57.450
they're actually extracting
heat from the body

01:50:57.450 --> 01:51:00.570
to be technical, they're
extracting heat from the body,

01:51:00.570 --> 01:51:04.150
the only way they can do
that is if those veins

01:51:04.150 --> 01:51:06.780
don't collapse and veins will collapse

01:51:06.780 --> 01:51:08.830
if they are made very, very cold.

01:51:08.830 --> 01:51:11.580
So if you want to use
glabrous skin cooling

01:51:11.580 --> 01:51:12.890
to offset hyperthermia

01:51:12.890 --> 01:51:14.470
or for the other forms of performance,

01:51:14.470 --> 01:51:16.220
which we'll talk about in a moment,

01:51:16.220 --> 01:51:19.690
you need to use a cool object or surface

01:51:19.690 --> 01:51:22.270
that is not so cold that
it causes vasoconstriction.

01:51:22.270 --> 01:51:24.760
And this can be a little
bit tough to dial in,

01:51:24.760 --> 01:51:27.960
meaning it can be tough to
identify such an object.

01:51:27.960 --> 01:51:28.850
And for that reason,

01:51:28.850 --> 01:51:30.870
Dr. Heller and some of his
colleagues have developed

01:51:30.870 --> 01:51:32.540
a commercial product called the CoolMitt.

01:51:32.540 --> 01:51:34.590
You can actually go to
their website, coolmitt.com.

01:51:34.590 --> 01:51:37.830
I don't have any financial or
other relationship to them.

01:51:37.830 --> 01:51:39.560
I know they've been
developing this technology

01:51:39.560 --> 01:51:40.470
for some period of time.

01:51:40.470 --> 01:51:43.580
It involves a glove that
you put your hand into,

01:51:43.580 --> 01:51:47.800
it circulates water of a given
temperature and it does so,

01:51:47.800 --> 01:51:51.120
and does so at a tempera
or that is sure to not

01:51:51.120 --> 01:51:53.170
cause vasoconstriction of the palm.

01:51:53.170 --> 01:51:54.003
And you may be asking,

01:51:54.003 --> 01:51:56.220
how can you just put
your hand into one glove

01:51:56.220 --> 01:51:57.053
and have this work?

01:51:57.053 --> 01:51:59.770
Well, that's how powerful these
glabrous skin surfaces are.

01:51:59.770 --> 01:52:02.850
Even just by cooling one palm,

01:52:02.850 --> 01:52:06.330
the core body temperature drops radically.

01:52:06.330 --> 01:52:08.960
Now that's their commercial technology.

01:52:08.960 --> 01:52:11.950
I know that some people out
there have started to experiment

01:52:11.950 --> 01:52:13.100
with a home version of this,

01:52:13.100 --> 01:52:15.640
which would be taking
a package for instance

01:52:15.640 --> 01:52:18.950
of frozen blueberries
or some other cold drink

01:52:18.950 --> 01:52:21.810
or cold metal object, and
actually bringing it into the gym

01:52:21.810 --> 01:52:23.420
or out on a run.

01:52:23.420 --> 01:52:25.130
There are even people
who are now developing

01:52:25.130 --> 01:52:29.230
cooled psych bicycle
handles for long rides.

01:52:29.230 --> 01:52:31.320
This might seem a little
kooky or crazy to you,

01:52:31.320 --> 01:52:33.800
but as you'll soon hear in the study

01:52:33.800 --> 01:52:37.300
I'm about to describe,
the increases in endurance

01:52:37.300 --> 01:52:39.890
and in the volume of strength training

01:52:39.890 --> 01:52:43.300
that people can conduct
if they appropriately cool

01:52:43.300 --> 01:52:46.190
their body through these
glabrous skin portals

01:52:46.190 --> 01:52:48.410
is actually quite significant.

01:52:48.410 --> 01:52:50.530
So again, as it relates to hyperthermia,

01:52:50.530 --> 01:52:52.880
if someone is overheating by all means,

01:52:52.880 --> 01:52:54.250
try and get them out of that heat,

01:52:54.250 --> 01:52:57.650
get them to stop exercising,
you can die from hyperthermia,

01:52:57.650 --> 01:52:59.080
try and cool the bottoms of the feet,

01:52:59.080 --> 01:53:01.340
the palms of their hands

01:53:01.340 --> 01:53:03.240
and the upper portion of their face.

01:53:03.240 --> 01:53:06.480
That does not mean it would be a bad idea

01:53:06.480 --> 01:53:08.200
to put cold water on
the top of their head.

01:53:08.200 --> 01:53:10.690
That probably would also help
and perhaps on their neck.

01:53:10.690 --> 01:53:14.610
What is probably not going
to be a good idea is to do

01:53:14.610 --> 01:53:17.330
the more standard thing of
draping someone in cold towels

01:53:17.330 --> 01:53:19.220
on the surface of their
body because as I mentioned,

01:53:19.220 --> 01:53:20.510
the beginning of the episode,

01:53:20.510 --> 01:53:22.390
that thermostat in the hypothalamus,

01:53:22.390 --> 01:53:25.700
the medial preoptic area
will typically react to that

01:53:25.700 --> 01:53:28.270
by increasing core body
temperature further.

01:53:28.270 --> 01:53:31.590
The effects of glabrous skin
cooling on physical performance

01:53:31.590 --> 01:53:32.820
are truly remarkable

01:53:32.820 --> 01:53:36.400
provided the glabrous skin
cooling is done correctly.

01:53:36.400 --> 01:53:40.000
And I want to point out that
the main degree of effect

01:53:40.000 --> 01:53:44.220
is on volume or the
ability to do more work.

01:53:44.220 --> 01:53:47.690
And I want to point this out
because I think that many people,

01:53:47.690 --> 01:53:49.360
certainly in the exercise
science community,

01:53:49.360 --> 01:53:50.550
but even in the general public,

01:53:50.550 --> 01:53:52.460
when they hear about some of these effects

01:53:52.460 --> 01:53:54.640
that are measured in the laboratory,

01:53:54.640 --> 01:53:59.130
they sort of look at those
effects a bit of scans

01:53:59.130 --> 01:54:01.090
and they think, well, that's not possible.

01:54:01.090 --> 01:54:03.430
Effects for instance,
that have been documented

01:54:03.430 --> 01:54:05.900
showing doubling or tripling
of the number of dips

01:54:05.900 --> 01:54:08.810
that one can do in a
relatively short amount of time

01:54:08.810 --> 01:54:11.810
or doubling of the number
of pullups one can do

01:54:11.810 --> 01:54:15.570
or 14% increases in strength
or even comparable degrees

01:54:15.570 --> 01:54:18.330
in increase in weight training output

01:54:18.330 --> 01:54:20.450
to people who are on
performance enhancing drugs,

01:54:20.450 --> 01:54:22.250
et cetera, et cetera.

01:54:22.250 --> 01:54:25.040
Part of the confusion is that the effects

01:54:25.040 --> 01:54:27.430
of proper palmer cooling,

01:54:27.430 --> 01:54:30.120
because it almost always
is done by palmer cooling

01:54:30.120 --> 01:54:32.460
and less often in these experiments

01:54:32.460 --> 01:54:33.787
by cooling of the bottoms of the feet

01:54:33.787 --> 01:54:35.570
and the upper portion of the face,

01:54:35.570 --> 01:54:40.570
but those effects tend to be
the ability to do more work

01:54:41.540 --> 01:54:45.210
over time and just to illustrate
some of the major effects

01:54:45.210 --> 01:54:46.370
that the Heller lab is seen

01:54:46.370 --> 01:54:48.120
and they are document
entered in this manuscript

01:54:48.120 --> 01:54:49.970
that I'll share with you in a moment.

01:54:50.990 --> 01:54:53.370
The typical protocol is
to have people come in

01:54:53.370 --> 01:54:54.770
and do some endurance training

01:54:54.770 --> 01:54:57.710
so running on a treadmill
and to have a condition where

01:54:58.700 --> 01:55:01.020
one group is actually doing palmer cooling

01:55:01.020 --> 01:55:03.640
while they are on a bike or on a treadmill

01:55:03.640 --> 01:55:07.000
and inevitably the outcome is
that they can do more work.

01:55:07.000 --> 01:55:09.950
They can pedal further at a given speed,

01:55:09.950 --> 01:55:13.190
or they can run longer at
a given speed than people

01:55:13.190 --> 01:55:14.810
who are not doing palmer cooling

01:55:14.810 --> 01:55:18.080
or who are receiving cooling by way of

01:55:18.080 --> 01:55:20.770
cold compress to the back
of the neck or ice pack

01:55:20.770 --> 01:55:22.820
to the armpits, et cetera.

01:55:22.820 --> 01:55:25.070
So the effects of palmer
cooling are very clear

01:55:25.070 --> 01:55:26.760
and very robust.

01:55:26.760 --> 01:55:29.590
And in the context of endurance exercise

01:55:29.590 --> 01:55:31.960
almost always allow
people to do more work,

01:55:31.960 --> 01:55:35.410
to go longer with less perceived
effort and to quit later

01:55:35.410 --> 01:55:36.243
so to speak.

01:55:37.160 --> 01:55:38.810
In terms of strength training,

01:55:38.810 --> 01:55:42.580
they've looked at the capacity
to perform sets of dips.

01:55:42.580 --> 01:55:44.810
So one of the more famous examples of this

01:55:44.810 --> 01:55:48.800
that Dr. Heller shares in the
episode that we did earlier,

01:55:48.800 --> 01:55:51.660
and that you can find at
hubermanlab.com involves

01:55:51.660 --> 01:55:55.940
someone coming in and doing
sets of dips, maybe 40 dips.

01:55:55.940 --> 01:55:58.360
This person actually could do
40 dips on their first set,

01:55:58.360 --> 01:56:00.660
then resting for a period
of two to three minutes

01:56:00.660 --> 01:56:02.470
and then doing 35

01:56:02.470 --> 01:56:04.530
and then resting for a period
of two or three minutes,

01:56:04.530 --> 01:56:06.870
and then doing progressively
fewer and fewer and fewer

01:56:06.870 --> 01:56:09.430
to the point where over a period of time,

01:56:09.430 --> 01:56:11.860
they add up the total number
of dips that they can do

01:56:11.860 --> 01:56:14.260
and then they have them come back

01:56:14.260 --> 01:56:16.960
after a period of recovery,
so not immediately after,

01:56:16.960 --> 01:56:18.710
but take a couple of days,

01:56:18.710 --> 01:56:21.570
come back and do effectively
the same protocol,

01:56:21.570 --> 01:56:22.930
but during their rest periods,

01:56:22.930 --> 01:56:25.920
they're doing two minutes
of palmer cooling,

01:56:25.920 --> 01:56:29.350
which essentially allows
heat to move out of the body,

01:56:29.350 --> 01:56:31.540
lowering core body
temperature in other words.

01:56:31.540 --> 01:56:35.350
And what they find is that
they see enormous increases

01:56:35.350 --> 01:56:37.340
in the total number of
dips that people can do,

01:56:37.340 --> 01:56:38.630
but that doesn't mean that the person goes

01:56:38.630 --> 01:56:39.910
from being able to do 40 dips,

01:56:39.910 --> 01:56:43.120
to being able to do 50 dips
or 60 dips on that first set,

01:56:43.120 --> 01:56:46.070
what it means is they are able
to do 40 on the first set,

01:56:46.070 --> 01:56:47.260
then 40 on the second,

01:56:47.260 --> 01:56:50.090
then 38 on the third
and so on and so forth

01:56:50.090 --> 01:56:52.930
so that the total duration
of the workout is extended

01:56:52.930 --> 01:56:55.530
and yet they're doing much more work,

01:56:55.530 --> 01:56:57.330
even though it takes more time.

01:56:57.330 --> 01:56:59.167
So that's an important point

01:56:59.167 --> 01:57:02.840
and I think a point that
perhaps wasn't as clear

01:57:02.840 --> 01:57:05.420
or as clearly made by me
in the previous episodes

01:57:05.420 --> 01:57:07.460
that discuss this topic.

01:57:07.460 --> 01:57:08.750
For those of you that are interested

01:57:08.750 --> 01:57:11.450
in exploring palmer cooling, first of all,

01:57:11.450 --> 01:57:15.090
I recommend taking a brief
glance or even a deep dive

01:57:15.090 --> 01:57:17.090
into this study,

01:57:17.090 --> 01:57:18.600
which is entitled work volume

01:57:18.600 --> 01:57:21.240
and strength training
responses to resistive exercise

01:57:21.240 --> 01:57:24.323
improve with periodic heat
extraction from the palm.

01:57:25.400 --> 01:57:30.400
In this study, they describe
big increases in anaerobic,

01:57:30.850 --> 01:57:32.660
meaning strength training output,

01:57:32.660 --> 01:57:35.280
things like improvement in dips,

01:57:35.280 --> 01:57:37.000
improvement in bench press,

01:57:37.000 --> 01:57:40.840
improvement in pull-ups, et
cetera, in human subjects.

01:57:40.840 --> 01:57:43.070
And it's a really nice study and points

01:57:43.070 --> 01:57:44.890
to some of the protocols
that you might be able

01:57:44.890 --> 01:57:46.810
to adapt in your own setup.

01:57:46.810 --> 01:57:48.920
For instance, over six
weeks of pull up training,

01:57:48.920 --> 01:57:53.920
palm cooling in between sets
improved volume by 144%,

01:57:54.640 --> 01:57:56.520
and this was in experienced subjects.

01:57:56.520 --> 01:57:58.890
So that's interesting
because a lot of studies

01:57:58.890 --> 01:58:01.380
of strength training and
improvements in hypertrophy

01:58:01.380 --> 01:58:04.610
and strength are done in
inexperienced untrained athletes,

01:58:04.610 --> 01:58:07.370
which changes the picture somewhat

01:58:07.370 --> 01:58:10.680
compared to experienced athletes.

01:58:10.680 --> 01:58:11.980
They found that strength,

01:58:11.980 --> 01:58:13.480
meaning the one repetition maximum,

01:58:13.480 --> 01:58:17.170
increased 22% over 10 weeks
in bench press training.

01:58:17.170 --> 01:58:20.050
And they point to the
particularly strong effects

01:58:20.050 --> 01:58:23.410
of using palmer cooling
when people reach plateaus

01:58:23.410 --> 01:58:25.120
in endurance and strength training.

01:58:25.120 --> 01:58:26.680
And there, I think it's
an important point.

01:58:26.680 --> 01:58:30.300
I think that if you're going
to explore palmer cooling,

01:58:30.300 --> 01:58:32.600
it's probably not the sort of
thing that you're going to do

01:58:32.600 --> 01:58:36.050
in every run or in every about of cycling

01:58:36.050 --> 01:58:38.640
or in every strength training session,

01:58:38.640 --> 01:58:42.520
but that it might be used to
vastly increase your volume

01:58:42.520 --> 01:58:44.590
or vastly increase your endurance

01:58:44.590 --> 01:58:46.700
in a given session or a set of sessions

01:58:46.700 --> 01:58:49.100
in order to push through plateaus.

01:58:49.100 --> 01:58:51.660
A particularly interesting
point in light of that

01:58:51.660 --> 01:58:54.710
is Dr. Heller has observed again and again

01:58:54.710 --> 01:58:57.650
that palmer cooling reduces
delayed onset muscle soreness,

01:58:57.650 --> 01:58:59.490
or it can eliminate it entirely.

01:58:59.490 --> 01:59:02.070
And that's very interest because
it also points to the fact

01:59:02.070 --> 01:59:05.350
that reducing core body
temperature may somehow be involved

01:59:05.350 --> 01:59:06.200
in short circuiting

01:59:06.200 --> 01:59:09.660
the normal mechanisms of
delayed onset muscle soreness.

01:59:09.660 --> 01:59:10.540
And you might say, well,

01:59:10.540 --> 01:59:12.380
how would temperature be involved

01:59:12.380 --> 01:59:14.360
in delayed onset muscle soreness?

01:59:14.360 --> 01:59:17.610
Well, I want to refer you
back to the meta-analysis

01:59:17.610 --> 01:59:20.390
that we talked about earlier,
where the short duration,

01:59:20.390 --> 01:59:23.990
very cold temperature exposure
after training did indeed

01:59:23.990 --> 01:59:25.520
reduce delayed onset muscle soreness

01:59:25.520 --> 01:59:28.990
in part through reduction,
excuse me, in creatine kinase.

01:59:28.990 --> 01:59:32.310
So it's not inconceivable that temperature

01:59:32.310 --> 01:59:35.000
and delayed onset muscle
soreness are related.

01:59:35.000 --> 01:59:37.210
And that raises perhaps
the most important point,

01:59:37.210 --> 01:59:42.210
which is the way that palmer
cooling can improve performance

01:59:42.570 --> 01:59:46.110
by way of reducing core
body temperature is known

01:59:46.110 --> 01:59:50.400
and that is because when
one engages in exercise

01:59:50.400 --> 01:59:52.120
or muscular output of any kind,

01:59:52.120 --> 01:59:54.930
strength or endurance exercise,

01:59:54.930 --> 01:59:58.410
the range of temperatures under
which a muscle can perform

01:59:58.410 --> 02:00:00.610
is actually very narrow.

02:00:00.610 --> 02:00:02.800
There's an enzyme called pyruvate kinase,

02:00:02.800 --> 02:00:05.640
which is critical to muscle contractions

02:00:05.640 --> 02:00:07.940
and pyruvate kinase can only function

02:00:07.940 --> 02:00:09.810
in a very narrow range of temperatures.

02:00:09.810 --> 02:00:11.550
If that temperature gets too hot,

02:00:11.550 --> 02:00:13.930
meaning if the muscle heats up locally,

02:00:13.930 --> 02:00:14.763
whether or not by running

02:00:14.763 --> 02:00:17.990
or cycling or swimming or weightlifting,

02:00:17.990 --> 02:00:19.870
the ability for that muscle to continue

02:00:19.870 --> 02:00:22.160
to contract is reduced and eventually

02:00:22.160 --> 02:00:23.950
is short circuited completely.

02:00:23.950 --> 02:00:26.890
And I think this is a much underexplored

02:00:26.890 --> 02:00:29.420
or at least a much under discussed aspect

02:00:29.420 --> 02:00:32.290
of so-called muscular
failure or the failure

02:00:32.290 --> 02:00:35.310
of one to continue to endure in running.

02:00:35.310 --> 02:00:36.143
So for instance,

02:00:36.143 --> 02:00:38.750
when you run as compared to
a bench press or something,

02:00:38.750 --> 02:00:39.950
you don't stop running

02:00:39.950 --> 02:00:42.470
because you can't actually
contract the muscles further,

02:00:42.470 --> 02:00:46.040
but somehow signals about the heating up

02:00:46.040 --> 02:00:48.740
of muscular tissue are
conveyed to the brain.

02:00:48.740 --> 02:00:49.760
There's a crosstalk there,

02:00:49.760 --> 02:00:53.370
it's probably bidirectional
and people stop, they quit.

02:00:53.370 --> 02:00:54.863
This is the quitting reflex.

02:00:56.100 --> 02:01:00.210
In strength training, one can
no longer perform a repetition

02:01:00.210 --> 02:01:01.760
or set of repetitions in part

02:01:01.760 --> 02:01:03.500
because of heating up
of the muscle locally.

02:01:03.500 --> 02:01:05.580
There are other mechanisms
as well, of course,

02:01:05.580 --> 02:01:07.030
and I realize that,

02:01:07.030 --> 02:01:10.390
but what's very clear from the
palmer cooling work is that

02:01:10.390 --> 02:01:13.260
by simply holding onto a cool object,

02:01:13.260 --> 02:01:14.910
remember not an object so cold

02:01:14.910 --> 02:01:17.140
that it constricts the
vessels of the palms

02:01:17.140 --> 02:01:20.070
or constricts the vessels
on the bottoms of the feet,

02:01:20.070 --> 02:01:22.800
but by holding onto a
relatively cool object

02:01:22.800 --> 02:01:26.120
in one or both hands in between
sets for two minutes or so,

02:01:26.120 --> 02:01:29.810
you can very efficiently reduce
your core body temperature

02:01:29.810 --> 02:01:32.920
and in doing so, reduce the
temperature of the muscles

02:01:32.920 --> 02:01:34.120
that are doing the work,

02:01:35.090 --> 02:01:37.910
increase the capacity for pyruvate kinase

02:01:37.910 --> 02:01:40.800
to continue to allow
your muscles to contract

02:01:40.800 --> 02:01:44.010
and thereby allow you to
do more volume of endurance

02:01:44.010 --> 02:01:45.050
and strength training.

02:01:45.050 --> 02:01:48.240
So a simple protocol that
Dr. Heller passed to me

02:01:48.240 --> 02:01:51.610
is find a relatively cool object.

02:01:51.610 --> 02:01:52.670
So you could, for instance,

02:01:52.670 --> 02:01:54.410
fill two bottles with cold water,

02:01:54.410 --> 02:01:56.460
maybe put a few ice cubes in there.

02:01:56.460 --> 02:01:58.620
This is not exact because
we're not talking about

02:01:58.620 --> 02:02:00.060
the commercial CoolMitt product here,

02:02:00.060 --> 02:02:01.820
we're talking about an at home version

02:02:01.820 --> 02:02:05.310
or use a pack of frozen
blueberries or broccoli

02:02:05.310 --> 02:02:07.830
sort of pack of those
as what he described.

02:02:07.830 --> 02:02:10.420
And then in between sets to put your hands

02:02:10.420 --> 02:02:12.120
and ideally you'd put
the bottoms of your feet,

02:02:12.120 --> 02:02:13.920
but that's not always
feasible in most gyms

02:02:13.920 --> 02:02:16.220
where they won't let you take
off your shoes and so forth,

02:02:16.220 --> 02:02:19.160
but to put the palms of your
hands on that cool surface

02:02:19.160 --> 02:02:22.570
for a minute or two minutes between sets

02:02:22.570 --> 02:02:24.930
and then returning to your sets of work.

02:02:24.930 --> 02:02:28.710
Now, if you are heating up
through other mechanisms

02:02:28.710 --> 02:02:30.020
like you're wearing a stocking cap

02:02:30.020 --> 02:02:31.540
and you're in a very warm environment,

02:02:31.540 --> 02:02:33.420
this might not have as potent effect

02:02:33.420 --> 02:02:35.110
as if you were to do this cooling

02:02:35.110 --> 02:02:36.770
in a more moderate environment,

02:02:36.770 --> 02:02:38.700
wearing lighter clothing, et cetera.

02:02:38.700 --> 02:02:41.510
So by all means warm
up to do your exercise,

02:02:41.510 --> 02:02:42.490
lubricate your joints,

02:02:42.490 --> 02:02:44.380
and get into a place where you're
not going to injure yourself,

02:02:44.380 --> 02:02:46.450
doing whatever form of exercise you do.

02:02:46.450 --> 02:02:48.950
But then if you'd like to
explore palmer cooling,

02:02:48.950 --> 02:02:51.160
I know a number of people
who've written to me saying

02:02:51.160 --> 02:02:52.260
they heard about palmer cooling

02:02:52.260 --> 02:02:53.500
on the episode with Dr. Heller.

02:02:53.500 --> 02:02:56.660
They've tried this and they
see quite excellent results.

02:02:56.660 --> 02:02:58.250
It does take some discipline.

02:02:58.250 --> 02:02:59.990
It's one thing to just
kind of hang out in the gym

02:02:59.990 --> 02:03:01.280
and play on your phone in between sets.

02:03:01.280 --> 02:03:04.140
It's another to do deliberate
cooling with your palms

02:03:04.140 --> 02:03:04.973
or the bottom of your feet

02:03:04.973 --> 02:03:06.237
or the upper portion of your face.

02:03:06.237 --> 02:03:07.920
You might get some weird looks,

02:03:07.920 --> 02:03:09.110
but of course you'll be the one

02:03:09.110 --> 02:03:11.090
doing significantly more volume,

02:03:11.090 --> 02:03:14.560
not experiencing delayed onset
muscle soreness and achieving

02:03:14.560 --> 02:03:16.040
better endurance and strength gains

02:03:16.040 --> 02:03:17.540
were you to do this properly.

02:03:17.540 --> 02:03:19.960
Now as a final topic related to the use

02:03:19.960 --> 02:03:22.650
of deliberate cold exposure
for improving health

02:03:22.650 --> 02:03:26.210
and performance, I'd like
to touch on this theme

02:03:26.210 --> 02:03:28.330
that exists online, on social media,

02:03:28.330 --> 02:03:31.800
on YouTube and in various
fitness communities

02:03:31.800 --> 02:03:34.340
of using deliberate cold
exposure to the groin,

02:03:34.340 --> 02:03:36.360
in particular to the testicles,

02:03:36.360 --> 02:03:39.310
in order to try and increase testosterone.

02:03:39.310 --> 02:03:41.663
And while this might sound really kooky,

02:03:42.520 --> 02:03:44.400
indeed this practice exists.

02:03:44.400 --> 02:03:47.150
Indeed if you were to go onto Amazon,

02:03:47.150 --> 02:03:50.718
there are actually ice pack underwear

02:03:50.718 --> 02:03:54.840
that are being marketed for
sake of increasing testosterone.

02:03:54.840 --> 02:03:57.840
Now, I am not aware of any
specific well-controlled studies

02:03:57.840 --> 02:04:00.420
that show that this indeed works.

02:04:00.420 --> 02:04:03.740
I can imagine based on what I
know about the nervous system,

02:04:03.740 --> 02:04:06.050
testosterone and cold, et cetera,

02:04:06.050 --> 02:04:07.380
that there are a couple of mechanisms

02:04:07.380 --> 02:04:11.040
by which one might experience
increases in testosterone

02:04:11.040 --> 02:04:13.160
as a consequence of
deliberate cold exposure.

02:04:13.160 --> 02:04:15.150
First off, let me say
there is no reason why

02:04:15.150 --> 02:04:18.000
you would have to apply these ice packs

02:04:18.000 --> 02:04:20.410
in the way that I just described.

02:04:20.410 --> 02:04:22.250
One could of course take a cold shower.

02:04:22.250 --> 02:04:26.320
One could of course use cold
immersion of various kinds,

02:04:26.320 --> 02:04:28.700
and you're still going
to get that exposure

02:04:28.700 --> 02:04:31.430
of the groin and the testicles to cold.

02:04:31.430 --> 02:04:34.720
Now I should point out
that people do report

02:04:34.720 --> 02:04:38.490
at least anecdotally
increases in testosterone

02:04:38.490 --> 02:04:40.040
as a consequence of this practice

02:04:40.040 --> 02:04:41.220
and I have to imagine

02:04:41.220 --> 02:04:43.080
that they are measuring
their serum testosterone,

02:04:43.080 --> 02:04:44.080
that they're not just guessing

02:04:44.080 --> 02:04:46.460
that their testosterone went up.

02:04:46.460 --> 02:04:48.530
If you know of a study
exploring this directly,

02:04:48.530 --> 02:04:51.550
please let me know, put in the
comment section on YouTube,

02:04:51.550 --> 02:04:53.200
or even just email me.

02:04:53.200 --> 02:04:57.420
We have a email that you can
find it, hubermanlab.com.

02:04:57.420 --> 02:04:58.910
Please email me the reference.

02:04:58.910 --> 02:05:00.630
I wasn't able to find a reference,

02:05:00.630 --> 02:05:04.260
but I can imagine two
reasonably plausible mechanisms

02:05:04.260 --> 02:05:07.570
by which deliberate cold
exposure to the groin,

02:05:07.570 --> 02:05:10.770
in particular the testicles,
would increase testosterone.

02:05:10.770 --> 02:05:13.280
The first is somewhat direct,

02:05:13.280 --> 02:05:17.110
which is that anytime
you cool a body surface,

02:05:17.110 --> 02:05:18.530
that if it's cold enough,

02:05:18.530 --> 02:05:20.320
you're going to get vasoconstriction.

02:05:20.320 --> 02:05:21.960
And then subsequently you're going to get

02:05:21.960 --> 02:05:24.730
a rebound increase in vasodilation,

02:05:24.730 --> 02:05:26.020
meaning you're going to constrict

02:05:26.020 --> 02:05:27.450
the blood vessels in that area.

02:05:27.450 --> 02:05:29.560
And then after the cold is removed,

02:05:29.560 --> 02:05:31.939
there's going to be more
blood flow to that area.

02:05:31.939 --> 02:05:32.938
And of course,

02:05:32.938 --> 02:05:34.500
blood flow relates to organ health

02:05:34.500 --> 02:05:35.840
and tissue health generally.

02:05:35.840 --> 02:05:40.840
So perfusion of that region
and the gonads to be specific

02:05:41.550 --> 02:05:42.910
with additional blood,

02:05:42.910 --> 02:05:46.240
you could imagine in some
ways increasing testosterone,

02:05:46.240 --> 02:05:48.340
that's reasonably plausible.

02:05:48.340 --> 02:05:51.060
The other probably more likely mechanism

02:05:51.060 --> 02:05:54.940
relates to the dopamine
increases caused by cold exposure

02:05:54.940 --> 02:05:56.340
that we talked about earlier.

02:05:56.340 --> 02:05:59.690
Again, anytime you have a
somewhat stressful stimulus,

02:05:59.690 --> 02:06:01.480
but in particular with cold exposure,

02:06:01.480 --> 02:06:03.300
it seems that the catecholamines,

02:06:03.300 --> 02:06:07.360
norepinephrine, epinephrine
and dopamine all increase

02:06:07.360 --> 02:06:10.840
and dopamine is known to be in the pathway

02:06:10.840 --> 02:06:13.100
that can stimulate testosterone.

02:06:13.100 --> 02:06:15.130
And so while there isn't
a direct relationship

02:06:15.130 --> 02:06:17.300
between dopamine stimulating testosterone,

02:06:17.300 --> 02:06:19.080
there is an interesting
pathway way whereby

02:06:19.080 --> 02:06:21.330
dopamine increases can trigger increases

02:06:21.330 --> 02:06:23.350
in things like luteinizing hormone,

02:06:23.350 --> 02:06:26.100
which can trigger
increases in testosterone

02:06:26.100 --> 02:06:28.190
as well as estrogen for that matter.

02:06:28.190 --> 02:06:30.220
So I know that there are
a lot of people out there

02:06:30.220 --> 02:06:32.010
that are interested in
the use of cold exposure

02:06:32.010 --> 02:06:33.400
for increasing testosterone.

02:06:33.400 --> 02:06:36.390
And some of those people
in communities are

02:06:36.390 --> 02:06:40.280
indeed using cold exposure
directly on the gonads,

02:06:40.280 --> 02:06:42.460
on the testees in order to do this.

02:06:42.460 --> 02:06:46.400
I'm not certain that that
direct contact is necessary.

02:06:46.400 --> 02:06:51.070
And in some cases it might
actually be quite dangerous

02:06:51.070 --> 02:06:54.120
or you at least should be
careful in terms of tissues there

02:06:54.120 --> 02:06:55.810
and avoiding damage.

02:06:55.810 --> 02:06:59.920
But nonetheless, I think
that a dopamine impact

02:06:59.920 --> 02:07:04.070
on testosterone is very likely
given the 250% increases

02:07:04.070 --> 02:07:05.340
in dopamine that have been observed

02:07:05.340 --> 02:07:07.680
with cold water immersion and
all of that points to the fact

02:07:07.680 --> 02:07:09.610
that cold water immersion

02:07:10.730 --> 02:07:13.360
very likely increases testosterone,

02:07:13.360 --> 02:07:15.300
but as a downstream consequence

02:07:15.300 --> 02:07:18.180
of the cold water immersion
effects on dopamine

02:07:18.180 --> 02:07:19.550
and luteinizing hormone,

02:07:19.550 --> 02:07:22.260
and again there's no reason
to think that the increases

02:07:22.260 --> 02:07:25.290
in luteinizing hormone would
also increase estrogen.

02:07:25.290 --> 02:07:29.350
Probably not to dangerous or levels

02:07:29.350 --> 02:07:31.600
that one would want to avoid,

02:07:31.600 --> 02:07:33.190
but I don't think that there's anything

02:07:33.190 --> 02:07:35.680
particularly specific about cold

02:07:35.680 --> 02:07:38.180
for inducing testosterone
and not other hormones.

02:07:38.180 --> 02:07:39.560
I think it's very likely to increases

02:07:39.560 --> 02:07:41.570
a number of different hormones.

02:07:41.570 --> 02:07:44.320
I do hope that there will be
a systematic study on this

02:07:44.320 --> 02:07:45.850
in the not too distant future.

02:07:45.850 --> 02:07:47.760
I also hope to not be a subject

02:07:47.760 --> 02:07:50.400
in the cooling of the gonads experiment.

02:07:50.400 --> 02:07:52.590
Now I promise you the last
topic was the last topic,

02:07:52.590 --> 02:07:54.620
but there's one other
really important point

02:07:54.620 --> 02:07:56.440
that I think everyone should be aware of

02:07:56.440 --> 02:07:59.070
if you're going to use
deliberate cold exposure.

02:07:59.070 --> 02:08:01.970
And that brings us back
to the very first thing

02:08:01.970 --> 02:08:03.510
that we discussed today,

02:08:03.510 --> 02:08:05.210
along the lines of
deliberate cold exposure,

02:08:05.210 --> 02:08:07.200
which is that your baseline temperature

02:08:07.200 --> 02:08:11.490
is going to be lowest about
two hours before you wake up,

02:08:11.490 --> 02:08:13.760
it's going to increase in the morning

02:08:13.760 --> 02:08:15.070
and as you wake up

02:08:15.070 --> 02:08:17.280
and increase throughout
the day and afternoon,

02:08:17.280 --> 02:08:20.390
and then start to drop in the
evening and come down at night

02:08:20.390 --> 02:08:21.453
as you head to sleep.

02:08:23.010 --> 02:08:26.520
I also want you to remember
that if you are to cool

02:08:26.520 --> 02:08:30.370
the external portion of your
body, in particular your torso,

02:08:30.370 --> 02:08:33.830
the net effect of that is
going to be an increase

02:08:33.830 --> 02:08:35.510
in body temperature.

02:08:35.510 --> 02:08:38.940
So for many people, not
all, but for many people,

02:08:38.940 --> 02:08:42.010
if you are going to do
deliberate cold exposure,

02:08:42.010 --> 02:08:45.220
you are going to increase
your core body temperature

02:08:45.220 --> 02:08:46.053
and that makes sense

02:08:46.053 --> 02:08:48.640
if you think about how
deliberate cold exposure

02:08:48.640 --> 02:08:52.440
can increase metabolism by
increasing thermogenesis.

02:08:52.440 --> 02:08:56.120
What that all means is
that if you are doing

02:08:56.120 --> 02:08:58.740
your deliberate cold
exposure early in the day,

02:08:58.740 --> 02:09:01.530
you are going to get
yet a further increase

02:09:01.530 --> 02:09:03.080
in core body temperature

02:09:03.080 --> 02:09:05.660
that would be associated with wakefulness,

02:09:05.660 --> 02:09:07.490
your ability to be alert that morning

02:09:07.490 --> 02:09:10.320
or throughout the day and so on.

02:09:10.320 --> 02:09:13.920
It also means that if you do
your deliberate cold exposure

02:09:13.920 --> 02:09:16.330
very late in the evening, or at night,

02:09:16.330 --> 02:09:21.330
so 6:00 PM, 7:00 PM, 9:00 PM and so on,

02:09:21.340 --> 02:09:23.787
you are going to increase
your core body temperature

02:09:23.787 --> 02:09:25.830
and if you recall,

02:09:25.830 --> 02:09:27.640
a decrease in core body temperature

02:09:27.640 --> 02:09:31.350
of one to three degrees
is not just beneficial,

02:09:31.350 --> 02:09:34.380
but is necessary in order
to get into deep sleep

02:09:34.380 --> 02:09:36.410
and remain in deep sleep.

02:09:36.410 --> 02:09:39.730
So the takeaway from this
is deliberate cold exposure

02:09:39.730 --> 02:09:42.280
done properly will increase
your core body temperature

02:09:42.280 --> 02:09:44.270
and make you feel more alert.

02:09:44.270 --> 02:09:46.230
So if you're doing it early in the day,

02:09:46.230 --> 02:09:48.210
that's probably terrific

02:09:48.210 --> 02:09:50.990
given that most of us want
to be alert during the day.

02:09:50.990 --> 02:09:54.560
However, if you do it too late
in the day, evening or night,

02:09:54.560 --> 02:09:57.040
it can disrupt sleep by way of disrupting

02:09:57.040 --> 02:09:58.820
your core body temperature.

02:09:58.820 --> 02:10:02.360
Now, the caveat to that is I myself

02:10:02.360 --> 02:10:05.190
tend to do deliberate cold
exposure early in the day.

02:10:05.190 --> 02:10:07.600
Maybe not first thing in the
morning, but mid morning,

02:10:07.600 --> 02:10:09.630
maybe as late as three
or four in the afternoon

02:10:09.630 --> 02:10:10.463
in some cases.

02:10:10.463 --> 02:10:12.800
In the longer days of summer,
I might do it even later,

02:10:12.800 --> 02:10:15.650
five or 6:00 PM and have
no trouble sleeping.

02:10:15.650 --> 02:10:18.970
I have done deliberate cold
exposure very late at night,

02:10:18.970 --> 02:10:21.780
10:00 PM, 11:00 PM and so on

02:10:21.780 --> 02:10:23.230
as part of a 30 day challenge

02:10:23.230 --> 02:10:25.990
of doing deliberate cold
exposure every day for 30 days

02:10:25.990 --> 02:10:27.570
and I got sloppy with my timing

02:10:27.570 --> 02:10:29.340
and then in order to not miss a day,

02:10:29.340 --> 02:10:31.010
I would do it at 11 o'clock at night.

02:10:31.010 --> 02:10:31.910
And I must say

02:10:31.910 --> 02:10:35.370
I found that I could still
fall asleep very easily,

02:10:35.370 --> 02:10:38.100
even doing deliberate cold
exposure very late at night.

02:10:38.100 --> 02:10:42.320
However, on those particular
days, I was particularly busy

02:10:42.320 --> 02:10:45.610
and so I was particularly
exhausted when I arrived

02:10:45.610 --> 02:10:46.900
at the deliberate cold exposure

02:10:46.900 --> 02:10:49.140
and I had no trouble falling asleep

02:10:49.140 --> 02:10:50.600
after doing deliberate cold exposure,

02:10:50.600 --> 02:10:53.050
and then taking a nice warm
shower and then going to sleep.

02:10:53.050 --> 02:10:56.060
But I could imagine that
because of the increases

02:10:56.060 --> 02:10:59.380
in core body temperature caused
by deliberate cold exposure,

02:10:59.380 --> 02:11:02.100
that were one to do that
too late in the day,

02:11:02.100 --> 02:11:04.910
evening or night that it could
indeed disrupt your sleep.

02:11:04.910 --> 02:11:06.240
